!! viz-graph.allspeak — the editor's Graph pane, as a companion module.
!! **The vocabulary the picture is built from is the script's own, where the script offers one.** An attribute is a note a script writes about itself and the runtime ignores — text for a tool — so the recorder reads two keys and the pane shows what they collect: `@viz start` / `@viz stop` name the window in the script's own words, and `@show Total, Row` says which values are worth watching at that arrival, which reach the sidebar beside the line and visit of the mark they belong to. Nothing else is read, so a note written for a person or for another tool passes both by.
!!
!!
!! The pane draws a run: a recording sitting beside a script, read as a sequence, as an arrival count per line, and as the flow between the marks. That is half of the editor by size, and none of it is needed until somebody looks at a run — so it is a module, fetched, compiled and run on the first click on **Graph**, and messaged from then on. The `svg` vocabulary is here rather than in the editor, and this is where the plugin is needed: the pane is compiled when it is fetched, which is why the page loads the plugin whether or not a run is ever opened.
!!
!! What it owns. Everything inside the pane, and the pane itself. The drawing and its state, the four gestures (the wheel scrolls the lines, shift-wheel zooms the lines, control-wheel the steps, a drag pans), the label names down the right-hand edge, the source drawn behind the heat, the key, the caption, the estimate shown before a long draw and the calibration file that estimate is learned from, and the status line for as long as it has something to say on it.
!!
!! What crosses the boundary, and what does not. One dict in: the run's text, the script's text, the three localised flow words, and the path the recording was expected at — the last only so that "no recording beside this script" can name the file it looked for. The gestures do not cross: pan and zoom are registered here, on the pane, because a message per drag event would put a round trip inside every pointer move. Nor does the pane's visibility, which is the editor's mode system to run — the editor says whether this pane is on screen, and the module's own `VizShown` is what its handlers consult, because that is the one thing it cannot see for itself. **One small dict goes the other way**, and only on a press: which line the mark under the pointer names, and — because a row of dots otherwise says the same thing however far along it a reader points — **which arrival that dot is on its line, and how many that line had**. The count is the one the dot's own colour is drawn from, so a reader comparing a pale dot with a red one in the same row is reading the number the report gives them. What a line *means* is the editor's model and the sidebar's to show, so the pane reports and decides nothing about it.
!!
!! Acquisition stays with the editor. The recording and the script are fetched there, because the editor is the one that knows which tab is open; this module fetches only what is its own, the calibration file, and never executes anything in order to draw a run.
!   -- the module, and the pane it owns --

    script VizGraph
!! @hash ff50e871
!! @verified ff50e871
!!!
!! What the module holds from the editor's page, and the state the boundary is kept in.
!!
!! The pane and the status line are attached **by id** — `viz.json` names both, and the editor does the same `attach` for its own elements — so the module has handles on the page without the editor declaring or creating a single element for it. Everything else here is either the run as it arrived, a number learned by drawing, or the three localised flow words the editor's string table holds.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **The readout is an `svgtext` in the drawing, declared here rather than with the other shapes below.** It was a `div` on the host and it landed at the top left of the *window*, over the editor's toolbar — a `div` positioned absolutely goes to the nearest positioned ancestor, and the host is not one. In the drawing it cannot escape, and the foot is where a reader looks for what the pane is saying. It is declared here because the code that writes it is up in the module's plumbing, above the shape block, and the compiler is single-pass: a name has to be declared above the line that uses it.
!!
!! §2 **`VizAlertWhy` and `VizAlertMsg` are what this pane says to the panel, and the flags that decide what the panel *does* with it are the panel's own.** The reason it is worth waiting is composed here, by the prediction, and empty when there is no rate to judge by; everything else about the notice — whether a drawing is still going, whether the panel has been put on screen, how long it has been — lives in `viz-alert.allspeak`, which owns the panel and its clock.
!!
!! §3 **The freeze, which is the menu's other row and this module's own state.** While it is set no line is reported at all, so the sidebar keeps the reading the reader asked it to keep. It lifts on the first hover that finds the pointer back over the picture *having been outside it* — `VizAway` is that second half, and `VizFrozenLook` has why it is needed. Declared here rather than with the row's own state because `VizOpen` reads the message the freeze arrives in, and a variable has to be declared above the statement that writes it.
!!
!! §4 **A flag for the clip's own state, declared and set here because the plumbing can do that.** The clip's own names are declared far below — after the module has already stopped — so the *first* draw is the earliest place they can be given values, and this flag is how it knows it is the first. Setting it here is legal exactly because its declaration is here too; after the plumbing's `stop`, nothing is reachable at load.
!   -- the page, by id --
    div VizHost
    span StatusSpan
!   -- the run, and what was learned about drawing it --
    variable VizTrace
    ! Declared up here rather than beside the drawing's own variables: `VizDrawRun` asks for a draw and
    ! reads this straight afterwards, and the compiler is single-pass, so a use may not come before the
    ! declaration that introduces it. (This is the second time in this file that has bitten.)
    variable VizPending
    variable VizSource         ! the script, drawn behind the heat
    variable TracePath         ! where the recording was looked for, so a miss can name it
    variable TraceSize         ! bytes of the recording, which is what predicts the drawing time
    variable DrawStarted       ! `now` when the drawing began
    variable DrawMillis        ! how long it took
    svgtext VizBusy ! §1
    ! **The alert panel, which is the editor's page element and not one of the pane's own.** It is
    ! attached by id, exactly as `VizHost` and `StatusSpan` are, and it is the only thing on this side
    variable VizAlertWhy      ! the advice the prediction composed, sent to the panel with the estimate
    variable VizAlertMsg       ! the dict the panel is told with: what it is, and when ! §2
    variable VizBusyText       ! what the corner says while a drawing is under way, and after it
    variable VizBusySecs       ! the drawing's own seconds, whole part
    variable VizBusyTenths     ! ...and its tenths: the language has no fractions, and a corner that rounded
                               ! a drawing of seven hundred milliseconds to `0 s` would be worse than none
    variable VizEstimate       ! what the last drawing's rate says this one will cost, in seconds; 0 = no rate
                               ! yet. Declared here with the host's own arithmetic rather than with the view's
                               ! symbols because `VizPredictJudged` writes it and the compiler is single-pass:
                               ! a declaration below that line is not there when the line runs.
    variable VizMillisPerKb    ! learned: milliseconds per kilobyte of recording, on this machine
    variable VizCalib
    variable VizCalibParsed
    variable VizPredicted
    variable VizCalibText
!   -- the flow words, as the editor's string table has them --
    variable StrFlowCall
    variable StrFlowJump
    variable StrFlowReturn
!   -- the boundary's own state --
    variable VizRun            ! the dict the editor sent, as it arrived
    variable VizShown          ! 1 while the editor has this pane on screen
    variable VizFrozen ! §3
    variable VizAway           ! 1 once a pointer has been seen *outside* the pane, so a return is a return
    variable VizMsgKind        ! which kind of message it was: a run, or the freeze
    variable VizFreezeMsg      ! the dict sent back when the pane lifts the freeze itself
    ! What a drag is holding. Declared with the gestures rather than with the rest of the view's state
    ! because the handlers below are the first thing in the file to read it, and a variable has to be
    ! declared before the code that uses it.
    variable VizSlide          ! 0 the picture, 1 the lines bar, 2 the steps bar
    variable VizClipInit ! §4
    put 0 into VizClipInit
!! @hash aef177dd
!! @verified 9030b091
!!!
!! The pane is the module's from the moment it loads, and the gestures come with it.
!!
!! A handler belongs to the program that wants it, so these are registered here rather than wired from the editor: the window the gestures move is this module's own state, and the pointer events arrive many times a second — a message per event would put a round trip inside every move. `on drag` and `on drop` are document-level because a drag that begins on the pane continues wherever the pointer goes; the editor registers its own pair for the Blocks divider, and each ignores the mode it is not in.
!!
!! The pane's visibility is the one thing a module cannot see for itself, which is what `VizShown` is for: the editor turns it on and off with a message, and every handler below consults it.
!   -- the pane, and the line that says what is happening --
    attach VizHost to `se-graph-host`
    attach StatusSpan to `se-status`
!   -- the gestures --
    on pick VizHost
    begin
        if VizShown is 0 return
        gosub to VizGrab
    end
    on wheel VizHost
    begin
        if VizShown is 0 return
        gosub to VizWheel
    end
    ! **The fifth pointer event, and the one that does not move the window.** A hover does two things and
    ! draws nothing, which is the whole of its design: it names the line (and the arrival in effect on it)
    ! to the sidebar, and it says what a control would do before it is pressed. `VizHover` says why the
    ! second is words rather than a drawing. `mouseleave` arrives here too, with the coordinates the
    ! pointer crossed the boundary at, so the same arithmetic carries the words away.
    on hover VizHost
    begin
        if VizShown is 0 return
        gosub to VizHover
    end
    on drag
    begin
        if VizShown is 1 gosub to VizDrag
    end
    on drop
    begin
        if VizShown is 1 gosub to VizRelease
    end

!! @hash d836ad91
!! @verified a09323c1
!!!
!! The boundary: one message in, and the module parks until the next one.
!!
!! `release parent` is what lets the editor's `run` return — the module is loaded, attached and listening before the editor sends its first run, and from then on the two only ever meet at a message.
    on message go to VizOpen
    release parent
    stop
!! @hash 47612d91
!! @verified 47612d91
!!!
!! Drawing a run that has arrived, and saying what it cost.
!!
!! What a recording will cost is said **before** the wait rather than discovered during it: a draw of a big run takes tens of seconds, and a pane that sits empty for that long reads as a fault, while a line that says what is happening costs nothing. The two constants are the whole of the judgement, and nothing is said on a first run — there is no measurement yet, and a guess would be worse than silence. What it actually cost is remembered at the end, overwritten rather than averaged: a machine's speed is not a thing to smooth, and the last measurement is the only evidence there is.
!!
!! The status line is the module's own during the wait, so the three-second clear is its own copy of the editor's: this one clears this message and nothing else.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **A recording that is not there is a state to draw, not a reason to stop drawing.** This used to jump away to `GraphNone`, which named the missing file on the status line and returned — leaving the pane showing whichever recording it drew last. Two tabs a click apart then showed one recording, which is the confusion a reader cannot see through: the picture looks like an answer and belongs to another file. Taking the notice as a *subroutine* keeps the naming and lets the draw carry on, so the empty state is drawn — the script plain, no arrivals — and `GraphNone` is still the one place that says why.
VizDrawRun:
    if VizTrace is empty gosub to GraphNone ! §1
    put the length of VizTrace into TraceSize
    gosub to VizPredict
    ! Said, and given a frame to be painted in, *before* the clock starts — so the twenty milliseconds the
    ! browser takes for that frame is neither charged to the drawing nor counted into the rate the pane
    ! learns from it.
    gosub to VizBusyNotice
    gosub to VizRequest
    ! A request that had to wait is drawn after this entry has gone, so it is not timed here: timing it
    ! would record the turn-round of a queue as the cost of a draw, and the estimate that learning
    ! feeds is only worth having if it is a draw's own time.
    if VizPending is 1 return
    gosub to VizBusyDone
    gosub to VizRemember
    fork to ClearStatus
    return

ClearStatus:
    wait 3 seconds
    set the content of StatusSpan to ``
    stop

!! @hash 598096cc
!! @verified 4695d2e5
!!!
!! The module's messages: take the run, stand down, or hold the picture still.
!!
!! `open` 0 is the editor saying the pane is no longer on screen, which is the module's cue to leave the pointer alone; 1 carries the run itself. The flow words arrive with it because they are the editor's string table's, localised for the file's language, and the path arrives so that a recording that is not there can be named rather than merely missed.
!!
!! **The freeze comes through the same door, told apart by the message's own `kind`.** It is not a run — it carries no text and nothing is redrawn — so a message that fell through to the run's branch would be read as an *empty* recording and clear the picture; hence the test before `open` is looked at. That is the whole reason this boundary has a `kind` at all.
!!
!! This is the only place the two programs meet, and the dict is the whole of the interface — so a field added here is the whole of the change on both sides.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **The explanation is cleared on every prediction, or a stale one would outlive the recording it was written for.** `VizAlertWhy` is only *set* when the prediction passes its threshold, so a recording predicted at fifty-eight seconds would go on explaining itself — with that recording's byte count — over a drawing of a two-kilobyte one. Cleared here rather than in `VizAlertStart`, because this is where the sentence is written and the tie between the two is what has to hold.
!!
!! §2 Say where the number comes from. It is a measurement of the last drawing, not a promise, and a stale one is self-explanatory when the reader knows that — which matters, because a single slow run sets the rate and the next run corrects it.
!!
!! §3 **And the advice points at the *view*, not at the script.** It used to send the reader off to narrow `viz start`/`viz stop` — which means editing and re-running the script, and answers a different question. What they want is less of *this* recording on screen, and that is the clip bar. Graham's wording, 2026-10-06.
!!
!! §4 **And it is a sentence for the alert panel, which is the second move of the same fix.** It was `alert` first — the browser's own box, whose button could be neither greyed nor dismissed on cue, and which Graham had to click twice — and then a line on the editor's status bar. The status line worked, but it is one line in a strip at the far end of the window and it says the number once, where the panel is what a reader waiting thirty seconds is looking at, and it says the same number again, with how long they have been waiting, for as long as the wait lasts. The sentence is composed in `VizAlertSay` with the count, so the reason, the estimate and the elapsed seconds cannot disagree about what is happening.
!!
!! §5 And remember what it actually cost, so the next prediction is a measurement rather than a guess. Overwritten rather than averaged: a machine's speed is not a thing to smooth, and the last measurement is the only evidence there is. What the readout says while a drawing is under way, and the `wait` that lets it be read at all.
!!
!! §6 **It says `Working` in words, and it is coloured to be noticed.** Every drawing that rebuilds the picture — a marker released, a wheel notch, a script re-read — takes seconds on a recording worth clipping at all, and a pane that simply goes quiet for that long reads as a pane that has lost the reader's click. The word comes first because that is the part a reader needs; the estimate follows because it is the part that says how long to wait.
!!
!! §7 **Two numbers, and only the second is a measurement.** The estimate is the last drawing's own rate applied to this recording's size — stale on a machine that has changed — and it is said in seconds because that is the unit somebody waiting thinks in. With no rate yet the readout says only that a drawing is happening, which is still the one thing the reader cannot otherwise tell.
!!
!! §8 **The `wait` is not a delay: it is the whole of how the message is seen.** This script runs on the browser's own thread, so a line set and then painted over by seconds of drawing would never be read — `wait` hands the thread back for a frame first. Twenty milliseconds against a drawing that costs hundreds, and the clock is started after it so the wait is not charged to the rate the pane learns.
!!
!! §9 **The corner readout is written before the drawing is asked for, and it is the only thing this does now.** The readout lives *in the drawing*, so until 2026-10-13 it was written into an element that did not exist yet on the first drawing of a run, which threw (`Cannot set properties of null`) — and the panel used to be armed from here first, so that the reader's message survived whatever became of that write. The elements are built before the notice runs now (`VizBuildElements`, called from `VizOpen`), so the order is free again, and the panel is told where every drawing begins instead.
!!
!! §10 And what it says when the drawing is done, which is the number the reader wanted in the first place.
!!
!! §11 **In seconds with a tenth, and assembled rather than divided**, because this is where the language's integer arithmetic shows: `0.7` is not reachable by dividing 700 by 1000, and `0 s` for a drawing of seven hundred milliseconds would be a worse answer than no corner at all. Whole part and tenth are taken separately and joined; the tenth is floored, so the number never claims more time than was spent.
VizOpen:
    put the message into VizRun
    put property `kind` of VizRun into VizMsgKind
    if VizMsgKind is `freeze` go to VizFreeze
    if property `open` of VizRun is 0
        begin
        put 0 into VizShown
        ! The freeze goes with the pane: a picture nobody is looking at cannot be holding the pointer
        ! still, and the editor clears its own copy as it leaves (see `DoExitGraph`).
        put 0 into VizFrozen
        put 0 into VizAway
        gosub to VizTipHide
        ! The panel is taken down by the editor, which is the one that knows the pane has left the screen —
        ! and it tells the module on this same path (see `TellAlertClosed` in `viz.allspeak`).
        stop
        end
    put property `trace` of VizRun into VizTrace
    put property `source` of VizRun into VizSource
    put property `path` of VizRun into TracePath
    put property `call` of VizRun into StrFlowCall
    put property `jump` of VizRun into StrFlowJump
    put property `return` of VizRun into StrFlowReturn
    put 1 into VizShown
    ! **The elements before the notice that writes them.** A drawing announces itself and waits before it
    ! builds anything, and that announcement goes into the pane's own corner readout — so the elements
    ! exist first. `VizBuildElements` is a no-op once they do, so a second run pays nothing for the call.
    gosub to VizBuildElements
    gosub to VizDrawRun
    stop

!   The menu's freeze, arriving as a `kind` of its own: the pane is told to hold where it is or to let
!   go, and it says nothing back here — the only thing it ever reports about the freeze is the lift it
!   makes itself, in `VizFrozenLook`.
VizFreeze:
    put property `frozen` of VizRun into VizFrozen
    ! A freeze begins with the pointer where the menu was, which is inside the picture: the journey that
    ! lifts it has not happened yet, so the memory of one goes with the flag that starts it.
    put 0 into VizAway
    stop
!   Nothing to draw, and the message names the file that was missing rather than what a marker is.
!   A `return` rather than a `stop`: the entry called it with `gosub`, and the pane is left on screen
!   with the status line saying why it is empty.
GraphNone:
    set the content of StatusSpan to `No recording beside this script: ` cat TracePath
    fork to ClearStatus
    return

VizPredict:
    put `` into VizAlertWhy ! §1
    if TraceSize is 0 return
    put 0 into VizMillisPerKb
    rest get VizCalib from `/read/.viz-calibration.json` or go VizPredictJudged
    put json of VizCalib into VizCalibParsed
    put property `millis_per_kb` of VizCalibParsed into VizMillisPerKb
VizPredictJudged:
    put 0 into VizEstimate
    if VizMillisPerKb is 0 return
    ! Multiply before dividing: the rate is per kilobyte and the recording is counted in bytes.
    multiply TraceSize by VizMillisPerKb giving VizPredicted
    divide VizPredicted by 1024
    if VizPredicted is less than 5000 return
    divide VizPredicted by 1000
    ! Kept for the corner as well as used in the sentence below: this is the ETA a reader is shown while the
    ! picture is being built, and it is a measurement of the last drawing rather than a promise about this one.
    put VizPredicted into VizEstimate
    put `This recording is ` cat TraceSize cat ` bytes - about ` cat VizPredicted cat ` seconds to draw. You may need to narrow the range viewed: drag the two markers on the clip bar above the picture to the part you want to watch.` into VizAlertWhy ! §2–§4
    return

VizBusyNotice: ! §5–§9
    put `Working` into VizBusyText
    if VizEstimate is greater than 0 put `Working - about ` cat VizEstimate cat ` seconds` into VizBusyText
    set the text of VizBusy to VizBusyText
    wait 20 millis
    return

VizBusyCost: ! §10–§11
    ! The corner readout's sentence, and the only place it is composed: the panel says the same number in
    ! its own words, from the milliseconds the pane sends it. Leaves `VizBusyText` as `drew in X.Y`, with
    ! the whole part and the tenth beside it for anything that wants them.
    divide DrawMillis by 1000 giving VizBusySecs
    multiply VizBusySecs by 1000 giving VizBusyTenths
    take VizBusyTenths from DrawMillis giving VizBusyTenths
    divide VizBusyTenths by 100 giving VizBusyTenths
    put `drew in ` cat VizBusySecs into VizBusyText
    put VizBusyText cat `.` cat VizBusyTenths into VizBusyText
    return

VizBusyDone:
    if DrawMillis is less than 1
    begin
        set the text of VizBusy to ``
        return
    end
    gosub to VizBusyCost
    set the text of VizBusy to VizBusyText cat ` s`
    return

VizRemember:
    if TraceSize is 0 or DrawMillis is less than 1 return
    multiply DrawMillis by 1024 giving VizPredicted
    divide VizPredicted by TraceSize giving VizMillisPerKb
    put `{` cat `"millis_per_kb": ` cat VizMillisPerKb cat `}` into VizCalibText
    rest post VizCalibText to `/write/.viz-calibration.json` or return
    return
!! @hash d5cfd121
!! @verified e24be747
!!!
!! The view itself: everything from here down is the drawing.
!!
!! The third view's drawing, grown from the front. Plot-only on purpose: the editor already renders the script beside it, so this draws what the editor cannot — the sequence, the arrivals, and the flow between them.
!!
!! **What it draws.** The script as a picture with the run's arrivals marked on it, the flow drawn between them, a window over the whole that the gestures move, and the instruments that say what the window is showing. It is a *section* rather than a program — the editor gives it a panel and a recording and calls `VizOpen` — and everything it needs is declared below.
! ---- the view, below here, is what a host runs ----
!! @hash a34baa58
!! @verified a34baa58
!!!
!! The view's own symbols, all prefixed `Viz…`.
!! Names are the scarce resource in a flat table of declared symbols — which is not a figure of speech: an earlier version collided with the host's own `Y2` and the compiler refused it as a duplicate. Everything the section declares is therefore `Viz…`, so it can sit inside an editor that has its own drawings, elements and variables.
!! The panel is this module's own — `VizHost`, attached by id to the editor's page — and the run arrives in `VizTrace` with the script beside it, fetched by the editor and handed over as a dict. The view fetches nothing but its own calibration file, and never executes anything in order to draw a run.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 The flow, one path per kind of movement, because the colour *is* the kind. `branch` — the compiler's own `if`/`while`/`wait` jumps — is deliberately not among them: scaffolding is not a program jump, and the trace keeps it a separate kind precisely so a reader can be spared it. The prototype drew it in grey behind a "compiler jumps" toggle; wanted here, that is a fourth path and a button.
!!
!! §2 The window on the run that the picture shows, in the trace's own units — steps across, lines down. A *window*, not a pair of scale factors: a factor would have to be relative to a fit that changes with the recording, where an absolute one sits pinned at one end — the prototype's own lesson, the same control reading 51.6 px a step for one window and 0.7 for another. It is fitted to the whole recording when a run is opened, so the first picture is the one this pane has always drawn; a gesture then moves the window, and nothing else about the drawing changes.
!!
!! §3 **The clip is a *list of kept step ranges*, not one range.** That is what lets a reader keep either the window between the markers *or* the two parts outside it: keeping the inside intersects every range with the window, and keeping the outside subtracts it, which splits a range in two. The list can only ever cover less — a cut narrows, and nothing but `reset` widens — which is the rule the whole control is built on.
!!
!! §4 **A pair of holders rather than a json value, and that is a cost decision.** This list is walked once per record, and a json access costs a parse of the whole array (~15ms on a 435KB trace), while two array reads and two integer compares are the cheapest thing in this file. `VizKeepN` is how many ranges are live; the spare room past it is there because keeping the outside can split one range into two, and a list that is rebuilt needs somewhere to be built.
!!
!! §5 **And a history deep enough to undo.** Every cut saves the list it replaced as one string — `0-20`, or `0-4;12-20` — so an undo is a split and a draw rather than a re-filter of the recording, which is what makes it instant. Strings rather than saved sets: the list is a handful of numbers, while a set would be a copy of the recording. `VizHistN` is how many are on it, and it is the redo's depth as well, which is why `VizHistAt` is kept beside it rather than being the top.
!!
!! §6 **The run the picture shows need not start at step 0.** Unclipped it does, which is why these read as they always did; clipped, the picture's steps are the clip's own range, so the first step is the clip's start and the width is its span. Two names rather than a subtraction at every site: the fit, the clamp, the bars and the handle drag all have to agree, and they agree by reading the same two numbers. `VizSpanSteps` is set in pass one, where `VizMaxSteps` is known; `VizMinSteps` here, where the filter is.
!!
!! §7 **The whole recording's records, read from the parse only when a cut needs them.** The picture is drawn from `VizEvents`, which a cut replaces with what is left; a cut filters *this*, so the ranges are always read against the same thing — which is what makes a second cut narrow a first rather than replace it, and what an undo re-filters to rebuild a state the history names.
!!
!! §8 **It is not kept.** Graham's reading was right: the recording is a file, the parse is already here keyed by its text, and one parse per *cut* — not per draw — costs nothing beside the walk that follows it. Holding a second copy of every record would cost memory for nothing.
!!
!! §9 The recording this window was fitted to. The *same* recording is the same run, and a visit back to the pane should find the zoom and the pan where they were left; a different one means the window means nothing, and is fitted afresh. This is what replaced the host calling `VizReset` on every entry — which was the same thing as throwing the view away each time, and is not what a return visit is for.
!!
!! §10 **Every drawn row's own two measures, kept so the bars can be rebuilt when the zoom moves.** A bar's left edge is where its line's *first character* is, at the document's eight units a column — so it scales with the text and the indentation stays on the text at every zoom. Its width is the line's own length for *reading*, pinned, so that narrowing the window does not shrink the whole redaction into the margin. Those two are the same value at the floor, where the text is at its own size, and differ at every other zoom — so the path cannot be built once and left, and it must not be rebuilt from the *source* either, because reading the file's lines back is the walk that costs seconds. Hence two numbers a row, kept from the pass that reads the source anyway, and one string built from them per zoom. Graham's specification, 2026-10-11: *"the right end should never change — it's always fixed at the end of the text at normal size. The left end should move with the starting position at all zoom levels"*, and its purpose in his words: *"to keep the indents visible but prevent the entire redaction shrinking"*.
!!
!! §11 **Two markers on the steps axis that set the clip range without a zoom** — because the zoom is the expensive gesture on a big recording, and marking a range costs one drawing where reaching it by wheel and pan takes five or six. The bar is the *recording's* own steps, not the picture's: `VizRunFrom`/`VizRunTo` are the bar's axis: the **working set's** own step extent, measured in pass one over whatever the set is — the whole recording, or what a clip has left of it. That is what brings the markers home to the ends after a cut instead of leaving them standing where they were chosen on a bar that still spanned the recording.
!!
!! §12 `VizSelFrom`/`VizSelTo` are the **selection**: the window the two markers make, in the bar's own steps. The markers are the reader's and not the clip's — a drag moves them and commits nothing, and only the `keep` control turns them into a clip — so they need a home that is not the applied range.
!!
!! §13 `VizClipInX`/`VizClipOutX` are where the two markers were *drawn*, which is what the hit test has to agree with (the same reasoning the scrollbar handles' boxes are kept for). `VizClipInXW`/`VizClipOutXW` are the same two steps mapped through the **window**, which is where the guides and the washes are placed: they say where the selection falls in the *picture*, and the picture's own mapping is right at any zoom rather than only at the fit.
!!
!! §14 **The controls above the bar, and their boxes as numbers.** A pane element cannot take a click, so a control here is a *place*: the box is drawn from these and a press is matched against the same numbers, which is the only way the two can be kept from disagreeing. The withdrawn attempt at this hard-coded a position into the harness, so when the press did not take there was no way to tell a missed hit test from a phase that never ran — hence variables, drawn and hit-tested alike.
!!
!! §15 `><` — take the window *out* of the run — is deliberately not here yet: it needs the set to be a set rather than a range, which is the next piece of this work. A control that did nothing would be worse than its absence.
!!
!! §16 **The freeze, which is the menu's other row and the pane's own state.** While it is set no line is reported at all, so the sidebar keeps the reading the reader asked it to keep — and the first hover back *over the picture* lifts it, which is what makes the freeze a pause rather than a mode. The four `VizFrz…` are the pane's own box in client pixels, which is the whole of how a return is told from a departure: `mouseleave` arrives with the pointer already outside it (measured, 2026-10-12).
!!
!! §17 The source, drawn behind everything else: declared and created first, so the marks sit on top of it. It is shown through `VizPane`, a nested `<svg>` the size of the frame, and `VizGlyphs` is the picture itself. See the note where they are created for why it takes two elements to do it. **`VizRedact` is the text's own shape** — one bar per line, behind the glyphs — and it lives in the same pane for the same reason: see where it is created.
!!
!! §18 Scratch values are named by role rather than pushed on a stack, which would be the neater idiom in Python: the view has to run in the JS runtime, where there is no `stack` type and `push`/`pop` mean the argument and call stacks instead. See language-pack-issues.md #16.
!!
!! §19 The y labels are a pool, not a set built to fit: an element is a declared symbol, so the view cannot make a label per tick any more than it can make a mark per arrival. Four are declared and assigned; the count is the axis's resolution, which is all a tick count is.
!!
!! §20 **The flow key: the three colours the *lines* are drawn in.** A dash rather than a swatch, because a transfer is drawn as a line and the shape should say so; the word beside it is the language's own keyword for that kind of transfer (see `SetStrings`), so a reader meets the word they would write rather than a name invented for the picture. `branch` has no entry because it is not drawn.
!!
!! §21 It shares its row with the status line, on the left. The heat key that used to sit above it is gone — see the ramp in the mark loop for why no range of counts can name those colours — and the caption that used to sit opposite is gone too: it printed the run's line range and its step count, both of which the status line already carries, so the row it occupied is now the status's.
!!
!! §22 **The scrollbars, and why the picture needs them.** Past a hundred per cent a reader can see *where* they are — the axis says which lines are on screen — but not *how much* of the run is on screen, and those are different questions. A scrollbar answers the second one at a glance and in the vocabulary everybody already has: the handle's length is the visible proportion, its position is the window's place in the run, and at the fit it fills the bar and cannot be moved because there is nowhere to move it to. Two of them, because there really are two axes here — the lines down the right margin, the steps along the foot.
!!
!! §23 They live in the margins, outside the plot, with the axis and the keys: they are instruments, and a flow line crossing an instrument is a flow line crossing the thing you read the picture with.
!!
!! §24 **The clip bar, above the picture rather than below it.** It is the steps axis's own instrument, so it belongs where the steps are — and above is where it can do the most good, because its two markers carry *guides* down through the whole plot: a reader dragging one sees the exact x it is at, over the marks it is choosing between, which is what a range chosen against a chart needs.
!!
!! §25 **And the mask**: the two areas outside the range are washed over while the range is being chosen, so the reader sees what is about to be cut away and not merely where the edges are. It is painted over the picture, which is the one thing the margins otherwise never do — deliberately, because the thing being shown *is* the part of the picture that will go.
!!
!! §26 All of them are moved by attribute writes, which is what lets a drag show the reader where they are without redrawing anything (see `VizClipMove`).
!!
!! §27 **And the controls, which are boxes with their words drawn in them.** They cannot be buttons — a pane element takes no click — so each is a `rect` whose box the hit test matches arithmetically and an `svgtext` drawn centred inside it, which is the reader's half of the same place. **Three controls: the two cuts and the reset.** The arrow legends are the design's glyphs — `←--- ---→` keeps the window between the markers, the arrows facing away from it, and `---→ ←---` keeps the two parts *outside* it, the arrows facing each other across the gap they remove. Each one answers to the pointer now: `VizHover` says in words what pressing it would do, which is the half `><` and `<>` cannot carry on their own.
!!
!! §28 **The drawing keeps its own clock, at its own two ends.** Nothing else can measure it honestly: a gesture asks for a draw directly (the editor's own entry point is only the *first* draw of a run), and the drawing hands the thread back as it breathes, so a clock kept by any caller would time the frame it was given and not the work. `DrawMillis` is what the corner prints and what `VizRemember` learns the rate from, so the two can never disagree about what a drawing cost.
!!
!! §29 **A draw is a long job, so it takes a ticket rather than a lock.** It says it is running here and clears that at its end; anything that wants a draw in the meantime asks `VizRequest`, which *remembers* the request instead of losing it. What must never happen is two draws in flight at once — they build the picture in the same variables, so two would each write the other's halves — but the gestures are the last thing that should wait, because a wheel notch that is dropped is a notch the reader has to repeat.
!!
!! §30 **And the panel is told here, at the start of *every* drawing, which is the fix for a clip that gave no sign of life.** It used to be armed by `VizBusyNotice` alone, and that is called only from `VizDrawRun` — the entry for a *new run* — so a gesture, and a clip is a gesture, drew without ever putting the panel up. Graham's report: *"the first clip of a large recording takes some time to run, but I didn't see a popup appear."* `Draw` is the one place every drawing passes through, run or gesture, so the message at its start and the one at its end cannot miss one. **The panel is another program now** (`viz-alert.allspeak`) and this is the arming message; the guard that keeps one ticker alive across many drawings lives there, where the ticker is.
!!
!! §31 **The arrivals are replaced, never left over, and the reading happens *inside* the guard that has a recording to read.** **And an empty recording is always worth drawing**: the frame pass is skipped when nothing about the picture has changed, and two *unset* variables compare equal, so a pane with no recording concluded "no change" and skipped the pass that builds the source document — leaving the redaction bars to walk an extent that did not exist. The line below this entry's marker is that test made explicit, and with it the empty picture is the script drawn as it always is, minus every mark, rule and flow (there are none to draw). Measured in a browser on 2026-10-13, which is the only place it can be seen: `test-and-or.allspeak`, no recording, Graph → no runtime error, no page error, the source drawn, no marks. The old text of this note: Two faults met here and looked like one. The clearing must come first, because `put json of` raises on an empty value and the raise ends the draw; and the parse must not be consulted when there was nothing to parse, because `VizParsed` is scratch that keeps the *last* recording's document — so a guard reading it re-set the previous tab's arrivals and the pane went on showing a recording that belongs to another file. Both were found by asserting the empty state rather than by reading this. **Only when there is no recording at all.** This used to run on every draw, and it is the reason a clip could not be taken from the set on screen: it emptied `VizEvents` *before* the clip block read it, so every cut walked the whole recording — measured 2026-10-07, `VizEvents` was `[]` (two characters) at every filter run while `VizAll` held the recording. The swap fills `VizEvents` again later in the same draw, so the *picture* was always right and nothing looked wrong; what was wrong was that the kept set never survived to the next draw.
!!
!! §32 **The recording is parsed once, not once per draw.** Measured on a 435KB trace: one `element N of` costs about 15ms, because the runtime parses the whole array *and* stringifies the element it returns (`Core.js`, the `element` and `item` cases) — so every loop here is quadratic in the number of records, and re-parsing per draw paid that again for nothing. Keyed on the recording's own text, which is what "the same run" means.
!!
!! §33 **A clip is a filter over the records, and it is applied here — before anything else reads them.** Pass one measures the events, the marks are placed from them, the rules are the lines they name and the hit test walks them, so replacing `VizEvents` with the kept ones is the whole of it: the extent, the rule set, the busiest line over the ramp and the `visit N of T` the sidebar reads all follow from the same set. That is what makes the notice on the status line a fact rather than a decoration — the picture really is of the part of the run that is left.
!!
!! §34 The tests below are the re-fit key, and it is `VizSetTag` rather than the list itself: a cut bumps it, so a cut is *visible* to the two places that decide a picture — without that it would change nothing, because it does not touch the recording's text or the script, which is what those places compare. `VizMinSteps` is the run's first step in the picture — 0, or the first step the reader kept — and it is what the fit, the clamp and the bars read instead of a hard 0. **A cut is a set of ranges over *this* recording, so a new recording drops it.** That also keeps the bar's axis honest: the axis is the working set's own step extent, taken from pass one, which measures the *set* once a cut is on — so a list carried over would be an axis against a run it never saw. **The clip's state, the first time a draw happens and only then.** None of it can be declared-and-set in the plumbing, because its names are declared below the plumbing's `stop` — where nothing runs at load — and none of it can be read before it has been given a value, since reading an unset variable is a runtime error rather than a zero. So it is born at the top of the first draw, behind a flag that the plumbing *can* set. `VizClipInit` is declared and set up there.
!!
!! §35 **And the kept set is built when the *list* changes, not on every draw.** This is the whole of why the clip did not pay at first: rebuilding it per draw cost more than the records it left out, so a clipped gesture was slower than an unclipped one (measured on a 435KB trace: 41s a draw against 16s). Built once, every later draw walks only the kept records — and since a walk is quadratic in what it walks, that is where the saving is. The *draw* builds it rather than the cut, so that a host asking for a draw — or a test — never has to know how the set is made.
!!
!! §36 **And the kept set goes back on *every* draw, not only when the filter runs.** The load block above has just put the *whole recording's* events into `VizEvents` — that is what its last line does, on every draw — so a draw that skipped the filter (every draw after the cut, the cache key being unchanged) went on walking the full recording. That is the fault Graham found: the clip was applied correctly and then thrown away, so a narrowed picture scrolled as slowly as the whole one. Measured with a probe on his recording: after a clip to 96 of 533 records the *next* draw walked 533 again.
    variable VizReady        ! set once the elements exist, so a redraw only redraws
    variable VizParsed
    variable VizEvents
    variable VizEvent
    variable VizArgs
    variable VizCategory
    variable VizIndex
    variable VizLine
    variable VizSteps
    variable VizMinLine        ! the picture's first line: 1, because the picture is the whole file
    variable VizMaxLine        ! and its last: the file's own line count
    variable VizMaxSteps
    variable VizSpanLines
    variable VizDotSize
    variable VizBreathe
    variable VizCount
    variable VizSeen        ! how many times each line has been reached so far, in this draw
    variable VizPictureNew  ! 1 when this draw's recording or source is not the last one drawn, so the parts
                             ! of the picture that depend on neither are built here and not on every gesture
    variable VizRunCounts   ! how often each line is reached in the whole recording
    variable VizMaxCount    ! the busiest line's total, which the ramp is scaled against
    variable VizBand
    variable VizHeatD
    variable VizHeatE
    variable VizHeatF
    variable VizHeatG
    variable VizKeyX
    variable VizKeyTextX
    variable VizKeyPitch
    variable VizColourCall
    variable VizColourJump
    variable VizColourReturn
    variable VizMargin
    variable VizPlotW
    variable VizPlotH
    variable VizPlotRight
    variable VizPlotBottom
    variable VizRowHalf      ! half of VizPlotH: the half row between a line's own y and its row's top
    variable VizRowY         ! where one row's baseline goes inside the picture: each row names its own
    variable VizFlowCallD ! §1
    variable VizFlowJumpD
    variable VizFlowReturnD
    ! The arrow head's three corners, computed per transfer — the head is what tells a reader which
    ! end of a vertical segment is the destination, which at low zoom is otherwise a coin toss.
    variable VizHeadL
    variable VizHeadR
    variable VizHeadY
    ! One faint rule across the frame for every line the run touched, so that a mark or a transfer's
    ! end can be placed on a line at low zoom, where the text is a texture and a mark is a dot.
    variable VizRulesD
    ! The lines the recording *names* — an arrival, or an end of a transfer — as a padded string, and
    ! one line of it at a time for the membership test. See `VizRuleAdd`: the language has no set.
    variable VizRuledLines
    variable VizRuledLine
    variable VizRuleLine
    variable VizRuleEnd
    variable VizRuleY
    variable VizRuleX         ! where the rule begins: one em past the end of the line it names
    variable VizRuleWidth
    variable VizKind           ! the transfer's kind, while choosing its path
    variable VizSegment        ! one transfer's path data, segment and head together
    variable VizX
    variable VizY
    variable VizY2
    variable VizTickStep
    variable VizXStep
    variable VizTickValue
    variable VizTickPos
    variable VizOutside
    variable VizViewX0 ! §2
    variable VizViewXW
    variable VizViewY0
    variable VizViewYH
    variable VizViewTemp
    variable VizViewFit
    variable VizFitXW
    variable VizFitYH
    ! §3–§4
    variable VizKeepFrom      ! array: each kept range's first step
    variable VizKeepTo        ! array: each kept range's last step
    variable VizKeepN         ! how many of them are live
    variable VizKeepFrom2     ! array: the list being built by a cut, before it replaces the live one
    variable VizKeepTo2
    variable VizKeepN2
    variable VizKeepI         ! scratch: the index over the ranges
    variable VizKeepA         ! scratch: one range's start, while it is being rewritten
    variable VizKeepB
    variable VizKeepKept      ! 1 while a record is inside one of the ranges
    variable VizKeepTail      ! what a cut keeps: 0 the whole recording, 1 the window, 2 the two parts outside it
    ! **Which set is filtered: 1 the recording, 0 the set on screen.** A cut filters what the last cut left
    ! — the same answer, since filtering a filtered set composes, and proportional to what the reader is
    ! looking at rather than to the whole run. Giving the whole recording back, and an undo, read the
    ! recording: they are the two that widen, and the two that cannot be answered from what is left.
    variable VizKeepWhole
    variable VizKeepSet       ! and what the last cut kept, which is what the notice names
    variable VizRangeFrom     ! scratch: one range, on its way into the list being built
    variable VizRangeTo
    variable VizKeepCount     ! records kept, which is the notice's first figure
    variable VizKeepCap       ! how many ranges the holders hold: past it a splitting cut keeps less
    variable VizAllTo         ! the recording's own last step, remembered while the list is empty
    variable VizSetTag        ! bumped by every cut, so the two places that decide a picture see it
    variable VizLastSetTag
    variable VizNewSet      ! 1 when a cut is what decided this picture, so only the steps are re-fitted
    ! §5
    variable VizHist           ! array: the kept list before each cut, as text
    variable VizHistN
    variable VizHistAt
    variable VizHistI          ! scratch over it
    variable VizHistText       ! scratch: the list as text, and the piece being read back
    variable VizHistPiece
    variable VizHistParts     ! scratch: the list split on `;` by the loader
    variable VizHistPair      ! scratch: one range split on `-` by it
    variable VizMinSteps ! §6
    variable VizSpanSteps
    ! The filter's own scratch: the records it kept, and how many of the recording's anchor and
    ! transfer records it kept and saw — the two figures the notice on the status line carries.
    variable VizKept
    variable VizKeptKey     ! the clip the kept set was built for, so only a change rebuilds it
    variable VizParsedFrom  ! the recording the parse came from, so it is read once and not per draw
    variable VizAll ! §7–§8
    variable VizClipKept
    variable VizClipTotal
    variable VizClipIs        ! 1 while the filter is looking at a record it might keep
    variable VizStatusText
    variable VizStatusEnd
    variable VizStatusZoom
    variable VizDrawing
    ! The source behind the heat. It is a *picture* rather than text — one `<image>` for the whole
    ! run — because an element is a declared symbol, so there cannot be one `svgtext` per line; the
    ! picture is built once per run and only its geometry moves after that.
    variable VizSourceLines      ! the script, split into lines by `json split`
    variable VizSourceCount
    variable VizSourceLine
    variable VizSourceIndex
    variable VizSourceAt
    variable VizLastTrace ! §9
    variable VizLastSource    ! and the script the picture was drawn from, for the same reason
    variable VizNewRun        ! 1 when this draw's recording is not the last one drawn
    variable VizGlyphBody         ! the picture's rows, escaped and encoded once per run
    variable VizGlyphWidth        ! the document's natural size, in the units the text is drawn at
    variable VizGlyphHeight
    variable VizGlyphEm           ! the em of the text, which is the gap a rule clears before it begins
    variable VizGlyphEmText       ! ...and the same em as the `<text>` element spells it, to two places
    variable VizGlyphRows         ! a window may not go below this: past it the text is at its own size
    variable VizGlyphMax
    ! The ruler's scratch: the hidden element that measures the font, the string it measures, how long
    ! that string is, and the em it works out. The element is a page element rather than an svg one —
    ! it is created in the host and never drawn.
    div VizRuler
    variable VizRulerText
    variable VizRulerCount
    variable VizRulerWidth
    variable VizRulerEm
    variable VizRulerFrac
    ! The redaction's own scratch: the path data for the bars, one line's bar width, where that bar begins,
    ! and where its row begins.
    variable VizRedactD
    variable VizRedactW
    variable VizRedactX
    variable VizRedactY
    ! §10
    variable VizBarIndents     ! array: each drawn line's indentation, in columns
    variable VizBarBodies      ! array: ...and the length of its text after that indentation
    variable VizBarAt          ! the row the rebuild is on
    variable VizBarCount       ! how many rows were kept
    variable VizRedactPin      ! the pin the bars were last built at, in thousandths; 0 forces a rebuild
    ! And the pin itself: the document's own shrinkage, inverted, in thousandths.
    variable VizRedactScale
    ! And the two the indent walk needs: the line being trimmed, and how much came off it.
    variable VizIndentWork
    variable VizIndentLen
    variable VizWindowY
    variable VizWindowW
    variable VizWindowH
    ! The diagnostic's own scratch: the document it builds, the line it is on, where that line lands, and
    ! the two measurements the copy of the text needs — its size and how far its baseline sits below the
    ! middle of a row. See the create block for why it exists at all.
    variable VizLabelSpace     ! the width a label's name and its one-em gap take, in frame units
    ! And what `VizLabelCheck` answers: whether the line in `VizSourceLine` is a label, and how long its
    ! name is — the line without the colon.
    variable VizIsLabel
    variable VizLabelLen
    ! Which axes a zoom notch applies to. Shift is the lines and control is the steps — the modifiers
    ! decide, so one handler serves both.
    variable VizZoomAcross
    variable VizZoomDown
    ! And the pair they do not: both modifiers together *scroll* the steps, which until now had no way
    ! of being moved but a drag. It is not a third kind of zoom but a second axis for the scroll, so
    ! the flag sits with the scroll and `VizWheel` is where the pair is turned into it.
    variable VizScrollAcross
    ! The gestures' own scratch. `VizGrabFromX/Y` is where the window stood when the drag began, so
    ! a pan is measured from the grab rather than accumulated step by step — an accumulated one
    ! drifts, because the runtime's division truncates and every step loses its remainder.
    variable VizGrabPos
    variable VizGrabbed
    variable VizTroughV      ! 1 above the lines bar's handle, 2 below it, 0 on it or off the bar
    variable VizTroughH      ! and the same either side of the steps bar's handle
    variable VizGrabX
    variable VizGrabY
    variable VizGrabFromX
    variable VizGrabFromY
    variable VizDragPos
    variable VizDragX
    variable VizDragY
    variable VizDx
    variable VizDy
    ! What the pointer's own arrival needs: which line it is on, which arrival is *in effect* there, and
    ! the scratch the walk over the recording uses. `VizMark` is the dict a press carries back to the
    ! editor — the one thing that leaves this module. `VizHitText` is the pane's own foot, which says the
    ! same thing beside the picture so a reader's eye does not have to travel to the sidebar for it.
    variable VizHit
    variable VizHitVisit
    variable VizHitTotal
    variable VizHitValues      ! what that arrival captured, if the script asked for anything
    variable VizHitText        ! the report as one line, under the row the status line is on
    variable VizHitI           ! the walk over the kept arrivals
    variable VizHitX           ! one arrival's x, in the picture's own units
    variable VizHitAnchor      ! which anchor the innermost band belongs to, so its total can be read back
    variable VizEffSeen        ! the walk's per-anchor arrival count, keyed by pc: the visit number
    variable VizEffPc          ! scratch: one arrival's anchor, used as that key
    variable VizEffCount       ! scratch: this arrival's number on its anchor
    variable VizEffBand        ! scratch: the block it heads, `[first, last]` — or its own line, unscoped
    variable VizEffFirst       ! scratch: the band's first line
    variable VizEffLast        ! scratch: the band's last line
    variable VizEffDepth       ! scratch: the deepest band head taken so far, which is the innermost
    variable VizEffValues      ! scratch: one arrival's captured values, before they are merged
    variable VizValueKeys      ! scratch: the names in them
    variable VizValueK         ! scratch: the walk over those names
    variable VizValueName      ! scratch: one of them
    variable VizValueText      ! scratch: its value
    variable VizMark
    variable VizPixelsW
    variable VizPixelsH
    variable VizPixelsK
    variable VizPaneH
    variable VizWheelAmount
    variable VizZoomXW
    variable VizZoomYH
    variable VizMove
    ! ---- the scrollbars ----
    ! A bar is a window drawn as a proportion, so the arithmetic that turns one into the other is the
    ! same for both axes and is written once, in `VizBarFit`, reading these five.
    variable VizBarLen
    variable VizBarExtent
    variable VizBarSpan
    variable VizBarStart
    variable VizBarOrigin
    variable VizSlideSize
    variable VizSlideTravel
    variable VizSlidePos
    variable VizSlideRoom
    ! Where each handle was drawn, kept because the pointer arrives between draws: the hit test has to
    ! agree with what is on screen, and the drawn box is the only thing that does.
    variable VizHandleVPos
    variable VizHandleVSize
    variable VizHandleHPos
    variable VizHandleHSize
    ! ---- the clip bar ----
    variable VizRunFrom ! §11–§13
    variable VizRunTo
    variable VizSelFrom
    variable VizSelTo
    variable VizSelKey      ! the applied range the selection was put home for
    variable VizClipInX
    variable VizClipOutX
    variable VizClipInXW    ! the selection's ends in the picture's own x, for the guides and the washes
    variable VizClipOutXW
    variable VizClipWash    ! a wash's width, needing its own name: `take A from B` assigns to B
    variable VizClipPos     ! one step's x, or one x's step, while the mapping runs
    variable VizClipSpan    ! the axis's width in steps, which the mapping divides by
    variable VizClipStep
    variable VizClipOther   ! the other marker's step, while one is being dragged past it
    variable VizClipMin     ! the least a range may be, which is never more than the run is
    variable VizGrabMarker  ! where the grabbed marker stood when the press named it
    ! §14–§15
    variable VizCtlY        ! the strip the controls sit in, above the bar
    variable VizCtlH
    variable VizKeepX       ! `←--- ---→` — make the window between the markers the run
    variable VizKeepW
    variable VizOuterX      ! `---→ ←---` — make the two parts *outside* the window the run
    variable VizOuterW
    variable VizResetX      ! `reset` — the whole recording back, above the bar's end
    variable VizResetW
    variable VizPtrX
    variable VizPtrY
    variable VizPtrA
    ! **The pointer's own two units, and the tooltip's place and words.** `VizRawX`/`VizRawY` are where a
    ! press or a hover arrived from — the two gestures that need the pointer turned into the drawing's
    ! units — and `VizPointerUnits` is the one place that conversion is written, because it is the one
    ! thing here that has already been got wrong once (the letterbox).
    variable VizRawX
    variable VizRawY
    variable VizHoverPos
    variable VizHoverShown  ! which control the tip is about: 0 none, 1 keep, 2 outside, 3 reset
    variable VizCtrlHit     ! which control the pointer is over, from `VizControlAt`
    ! The line the pointer is over, and the last one *reported* — the pair that makes a hover say one
    ! thing per line rather than one per pointer event. `VizRowLine` is 0 when the pointer is over no row
    ! at all (the frame, the bars above, the key below, or outside the pane).
    variable VizRowLine
    variable VizRowSent
    variable VizRowSentVisit ! and which arrival it named, so a move *along* a row is heard when it crosses one
    variable VizRowTmp
    variable VizRowMsg
    variable VizFrzX ! §16
    variable VizFrzX2
    variable VizFrzY
    variable VizFrzY2
    variable VizTipText
    variable VizTipLen
    variable VizTipW        ! the tip's width in drawing units, from the words it carries
    variable VizTipX
    ! The window as it was before the gesture now running, so `VizRedraw` can tell whether anything
    ! changed. See `VizNote` for why it is kept rather than re-derived.
    variable VizMoved
    variable VizWasX0
    variable VizWasXW
    variable VizWasY0
    variable VizWasYH

    svg VizCanvas
    rect VizFrame
    svg VizPane ! §17
    path VizRedact
    svg VizPlot
    svgimage VizGlyphs
    ! The diagnostic's own layer: the label lines of the script, right-aligned at the frame's edge, so
    ! that the picture's text and the drawing's mapping can be compared side by side. Temporary.
    path VizRules         ! a faint rule across the frame for every line the run touched
    path VizHeat0           ! the marks, coloured by how much work the line carried
    path VizHeat1
    path VizHeat2
    path VizHeat3
    path VizFlowCall      ! the flow, one path per kind of movement: the colour is the kind
    path VizFlowJump
    path VizFlowReturn
    svgtext VizYLabel0 ! §18–§19
    svgtext VizYLabel1
    svgtext VizYLabel2
    svgtext VizYLabel3
    svgtext VizXLabel0
    svgtext VizXLabel1
    svgtext VizXLabel2
    svgtext VizXLabel3
    rect VizFlowKeyMark0 ! §20–§21
    rect VizFlowKeyMark1
    rect VizFlowKeyMark2
    svgtext VizFlowKeyLabel0
    svgtext VizFlowKeyLabel1
    svgtext VizFlowKeyLabel2
    ! The status line, along the bottom: what the window is showing — the two zooms as percentages of
    ! the fitted picture, and the span in the trace's own units. It exists to be *quoted*, which is why
    ! it carries the numbers raw rather than saying them in words: a reader copying a line into a bug
    ! report needs it to be exact, and the picture needs it to be short.
    svgtext VizStatus
    ! The mark's own report, on the line below the status line. It lives here rather than in the panel
    ! beside the picture because of where a reader's eye already is: they are pointing at a dot, and on a
    ! big screen the foot of the sidebar is the other side of the room. Graham, 2026-10-05.
    svgtext VizHitStatus
    rect VizBarV ! §22–§23
    rect VizHandleV
    rect VizBarH
    rect VizHandleH
    rect VizBarClip ! §24–§26
    rect VizClipBand
    rect VizClipMarkIn
    rect VizClipMarkOut
    rect VizClipGuideIn
    rect VizClipGuideOut
    rect VizClipMaskIn
    rect VizClipMaskOut
    ! The notice line, on the bar's own row above the plot: the clip is stated here rather than on the
    ! status line, where it ran into the flow key's words. A pane literal, so English like the rest of
    ! the pane's own strings.
    svgtext VizClipNotice
    rect VizKeepBox ! §27
    svgtext VizKeepText
    rect VizOuterBox
    svgtext VizOuterText
    rect VizResetBox
    svgtext VizResetText
    ! **The tooltip: a box and its words, drawn over the row below the controls.** They are the last
    ! elements created, which is what puts them in front of the bar — a tooltip is the one thing in this
    ! pane that is meant to be on top of what it explains. It is moved and emptied by attribute writes
    ! only, because a hover arrives at pointer rate: see `VizHover`, which never draws.
    rect VizTipBox
    svgtext VizTip
    ! And the readout's own style is set where it is created, in the built-once block below.
Draw:
    put now into DrawStarted ! §28
    put 1 into VizDrawing ! §29
    ! **The panel is told, rather than armed from here.** It is another program now (`viz-alert.allspeak`), so
    ! what crosses is a dict: what the drawing is expected to cost, why waiting is worth it, and the moment
    ! this drawing began — the last of those because the panel counts the wait from the drawing's own clock.
    ! The editor relays it, since a module can only `send` to its parent.
    put `{}` into VizAlertMsg
    set property `kind` of VizAlertMsg to `alert`
    set property `what` of VizAlertMsg to `arming`
    set property `estimate` of VizAlertMsg to VizEstimate
    set property `why` of VizAlertMsg to VizAlertWhy
    set property `started` of VizAlertMsg to DrawStarted
    send VizAlertMsg to parent ! §30
    if VizTrace is empty put json of `[]` into VizEvents ! §31
    if VizTrace is not empty
    begin
        if VizTrace is not VizParsedFrom ! §32
        begin
            put json of VizTrace into VizParsed
            put VizTrace into VizParsedFrom
            ! A new recording is new objects, so the kept set belongs to the old one.
            put `` into VizKeptKey
        end
    end
    if VizTrace is empty put `` into VizParsedFrom
    ! **Nothing at all to draw is a draw that stops here, and it is the *pair* that is tested.** A pane hands
    ! over its script and its recording separately, so a draw can arrive with neither — the first draw after
    ! Graph is pressed, on a script that has never been run, is exactly that. Everything below paints from a
    ! frame pass, three of whose inputs are compared *against the previous draw*: with both unset, "no
    ! change" was the conclusion, the pass was skipped, and the redaction bars walked a document nobody had
    ! built. Graham found the first fault on this path on 2026-10-13 (the clip filter's read, §30) and this
    ! draw is what was behind it. **A source without a recording is *not* this case**: that picture is the
    ! script itself, built by the pass below like any other, and it is the ordinary way a pane opens.
    if VizTrace is empty and VizSource is empty return
    if VizClipInit is 0 ! §33–§34
    begin
        put 0 into VizKeepN
        put 1 into VizKeepWhole
        ! **The two tags start *equal*, or the first draw of a recording reads as a cut.** A cut is the
        ! only thing allowed to re-fit the steps alone and leave the lines; the first picture must be
        ! fitted both ways, and a new recording already forces that through `VizNewRun`.
        put 0 into VizSetTag
        put 0 into VizLastSetTag
        put -1 into VizSelKey
        put 0 into VizHistN
        put 0 into VizHistAt
        put -1 into VizKeptKey
        put 1 into VizClipInit
    end
    if VizTrace is not VizLastTrace
    begin
        gosub to VizCutWhole
        ! A new recording takes the history with it: those ranges are steps in a run that is no longer on
        ! screen, so an undo into them would cut the picture to a set nobody asked for.
        put 0 into VizHistN
        put 0 into VizHistAt
    end
    put 0 into VizMinSteps
    if VizSetTag is not VizKeptKey gosub to VizKeepRun ! §35
    put VizKept into VizEvents ! §36
!! @hash 157dd69e
!! @verified e9a06a05
!!!
!! Geometry is in viewBox units, and the run is shown through a window.
!!
!! Nothing is measured in pixels: the frame is fixed at 1000x740 and the browser fits it to whatever panel it is given, which is what lets the whole script run against a stub DOM in node. What the window decides is *which part of the run* fills that frame. It starts fitted to the whole recording, so the first picture is the one this pane has always drawn; then the wheel scrolls it, shift and control zoom it on their own axes, the two together scroll it across, and a drag pans it. Graham's scheme, from Kdenlive and Audacity, with control added for the second axis.
!! The picture never changes; the window over it does. `VizPane` is a nested `<svg>` the size of the frame, and its `viewBox` is the whole of the zoom and the pan — one attribute per draw, and the picture itself is drawn once when the run is opened. That is what keeps the text the shape it was written in: a window whose aspect followed the zoom instead is what stretched the letters as you zoomed in. The pane's size being the frame is also the clipping, which is the one thing a picture cannot do for itself — an `<image>` either scales its content to its own box or is clipped by the outer viewport.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 The drawing's frame, in viewBox units. These belong to the host's panel in the end — they are the one place the view still assumes a size — but a constant keeps the maths in whole numbers, which this runtime wants anyway: its arithmetic is integer-first and division truncates.
!!
!! §2 **The plot is the margin band on every side, and the band below it carries the instruments.** The canvas is 1000 by 740 and the plot is 880 by 580 inside a 60-unit margin. The foot holds the steps scrollbar, then the step labels, then one row carrying the status line on the left and the flow key on the right; the right-hand margin holds the y labels and the lines scrollbar. That leaves the canvas taller than its contents, which is deliberate for now: a sidebar is coming that will change the panel's shape, and the height is better chosen then, with the width it will have.
!!
!! §3 **The ruler: the pane's own way of asking the browser how wide a character is.** The picture's geometry is in whole units — a column is eight of them, and the rules, the label names, the document's width and the bars all multiply by that — while the *text* is laid out by the browser at whatever advance the monospace font it picks actually has. Those two only agree by luck: the font the pane names is `monospace`, and no font promises an advance of eight thirteenths of an em. Measured 2026-10-11 in chromium on this machine: `monospace` at 13 has an advance of **7.827** units, so every column of text sat 2.2% left of the column the pane drew — and the error *accumulates with depth*, which is how it was found: Graham, looking at a deeply nested block, *"the offsets are too much"*.
!!
!! §4 So the pane measures the advance and stretches the *font* to the geometry rather than the other way round. **Why this way and not the alternatives**: the geometry's eight units a column is the number the rules, the label widths, the document's size and the bars are all built from, and the prose says so in three files. Moving the measurement into the geometry would touch five sites and every one of those claims; moving it into the font touches one attribute, and the font is the thing that was wrong. **And it cannot break when the measurement fails** — a page with no layout gives a nonsense width, the guard below rejects it, and the em stays at thirteen, which is exactly the picture this pane drew before.
!!
!! §5 `visibility:hidden` keeps it laid out; `left:-10000px` keeps it out of the host's own layout, which a wide child would otherwise push a scrollbar into.
!!
!! §6 The source, behind the frame's contents, shown through a window. `VizPane` is a nested `<svg>` the size of the frame — and it is there because it is the one thing a picture cannot do for itself. An `<image>` either scales its content to its own box, which is what stretched the text as the zoom changed, or is clipped by the outer viewport, which is the whole canvas. A picture that is *moved* rather than resized therefore needs a container that clips, and a nested viewport does that by default: it is `overflow: hidden`, and it has no viewBox of its own until one is given. That viewBox is the whole of the zoom and the pan — one attribute per draw.
!!
!! §7 The style is not decoration. With none, the plugin defaults to `width:100%;height:100%`, and CSS beats the width and height attributes, so the pane would fill the canvas whatever it was told.
!!
!! §8 **The redaction: the text's own shape, drawn behind it.** One bar per line, as wide as that line's text and as tall as the whole eighteen-unit row — so it covers the glyphs *and* the leading above and below them, which is what makes the block of a line read as a block rather than as a line.
!!
!! §9 **It is in the pane, and that is the whole of how it zooms.** The pane's `viewBox` is the *document*, eighteen units to a row and eight to a column, so a bar drawn in those units is scaled by the browser as the glyphs beside it are — which is what makes their *height* follow the row at every zoom.
!!
!! §10 **The fill is the rules' colour, a tenth darker, and both halves of that are Graham's.** He asked for the horizontal lines' colour first — *"I'd prefer the text to stay black and to try the horizontal line colour for the redaction"* — and then, having looked at it, for ten per cent off: *"the redaction bars can be a bit darker, as the text is easily readable and the trace information is still the loudest thing in the picture."* The rules are `#eee` where they are created; this is `#d6d6d6`, which is that times 0.9 a channel. The measurements, so the next nudge starts from numbers rather than from taste: against the page 15.48 becomes 12.36; the text on it goes from 5.16 to **4.12** and is still plainly readable; a rule crossing a bar goes from 1.00 — identical, and so invisible — to 1.25, which is a shade clear but only a shade. The text itself is left exactly as it was, dark on light, which is the reading a redacted page has.
!!
!! §11 **The plot, and it is the mask.** A nested `<svg>` over the frame — 60,60 to 940,640 in canvas units, its `viewBox` the same, so one unit inside is one unit outside and *no* coordinate below has to change — whose whole purpose is to clip. Everything that belongs to the plot is created *in* it: the source picture's overlay, the rules, the marks and the flow. A transfer whose other end is off-screen is now drawn at its true position and cut off by this viewport, which is the only way a picture of a sliced run can say "control came from above and left that way". The axis, the key, the caption and the status line stay outside it, because they live in the margin and a line crossing them would be a line crossing the reader's instruments.
!!
!! §12 This is the mechanism the picture already used — see `VizPane` — rather than a `clipPath`, which the plugin has no element for and which would take a second element anyway. A nested viewport clips by default; the style is here for the same reason it is there: with no style of its own the plugin defaults the element to `width:100%;height:100%`, and CSS beats the attributes.
!!
!!
!!
!!
!!
!! §13 Created last, so they are drawn over everything: nothing overlaps them, and last is the safe default. Both of the *troughs* are fixed — a bar is always the whole of its axis — so their geometry is set once here and only the handles move. The handles are pills rather than rectangles because a scrollbar handle reads as a pill; `rx` of half the thickness is what makes one.
!!
!! §14 The colours are the canvas's own greys: the trough a shade darker than the panel, the handle lighter again, so the pair reads as a groove with something in it rather than as two rectangles. The cursors are the whole of what makes a bar *feel* like a bar — `ns-resize` down the side and `ew-resize` along the foot — and they are the reason the style clause is here rather than plain attributes.
!!
!! §15 **The clip bar, above the picture.** It sits in the pane's top margin, which held nothing: the plot begins at 60 and the step numbers are down at the foot, so this is the one free band, and above is where the bar does the most good — its markers carry guides down the whole plot, so the reader dragging one sees the exact x it is at, over the very marks they are choosing between.
!!
!! §16 The rail is two units of a pale grey, the band is the range, and the markers are twenty units tall so there is something to grab; the cursor style says so, as it does on the scrollbar handles.
!!
!! §17 **The three controls above the bar, drawn from the numbers the hit test uses.** They sit in their own strip above the rail and against its right end, laid out from `VizPlotRight` rather than from constants so they follow the frame if it ever stops being 1000 wide. Their legends are the arrow pairs of the design, drawn *in* them; the words that explain each one belong to a tooltip, which is owed and not built yet — until then the notice names what the last cut kept.
!!
!! §18 A mark is a *stroked* hairline with a round cap, not a filled one: `M x y h0.01` alone fills to nothing at all, and stroked without the cap it is a 0.01-long rectangle — so the cap belongs to the mark, not to a scheme. Its width is set with the geometry on every draw, where the span is known; see the note where the mark's size is set.
!!
!! §19 The heat ramp's four bands, coolest first, and the only scheme. The marks were once drawn a second time by a plain single-colour path; the fold removed the duplication, and with it the template that the mark-size and line-cap mistakes were both copied from. **The ramp, and it is a spectrum between blue and red.** It used to run blue, yellow, orange, red, which is not a progression a reader can read as one — the eye has no way to say whether yellow is between blue and red or off to the side of them. These four are on the line the eye does interpolate: blue, violet, magenta, red. The *coldest* is what every mark starts as, which is what makes a picture of a run show the program warming up; see the shading in the mark loop.
!!
!! §20 The flow, a path per kind, in the prototype's own colours. A transfer says where control went, and *how* it went is the interesting part: a `gosub` and a `return` are the same gesture drawn the same way until the colour tells them apart. `branch` is not drawn at all — see the note on the flow paths' declarations, §1, for why the scaffolding is left out rather than greyed. One place for each, because the key draws them too: a legend that named a colour other than the one on the wires would be worse than no legend, and two copies is how that happens.
!!
!! §21 **The masks last, and that is the whole of their reason: SVG paints in document order, so an element created before the marks would sit *under* them and hide nothing.** One covers the recording before the range and one what follows it, so a reader choosing a range sees the part that is about to go. Their x and width are set per draw and per drag; only their shape is fixed here. **The wash has to be given as a *string*, not as the number 0.62** — the language has no fractions and `set attribute ... to 0.62` is a compile error ("not an integer"), where a backtick value is just the text of the attribute and never goes near the arithmetic.
    ! ---- built once, on the first draw ----
    ! **The elements are built by a call, and `Draw` then jumps over its own subroutine.** They have to
    ! exist before anything writes to them: the notice that a drawing is under way is written *before*
    ! the draw is asked for, and it writes the corner readout, which is one of these elements — so on the
    ! first drawing of a session that write reached an element that did not exist yet and threw
    ! (`Cannot set properties of null`). That is Graham's `VizBusy` item, and it is why
    ! `viz-modes-check` stops part-way through its checks. `VizOpen` calls the same subroutine before it
    ! asks for a draw, so the elements are there by the time the notice runs.
    gosub to VizBuildElements
    go to VizBuilt
VizBuildElements:
    ! The panel is the module's own (`VizHost`, attached by id to the editor's page) and the run
    ! arrives in `VizTrace`, fetched by the editor and handed over as a dict. Building the elements
    ! once is what makes a redraw a redraw — the marks are attributes, so a second call replaces them
    ! instead of adding a second picture.
    if VizReady is 1 return

    put 60 into VizMargin ! §1–§2
    put 880 into VizPlotW
    put 580 into VizPlotH
    put VizMargin into VizPlotRight
    add VizPlotW to VizPlotRight
    put VizMargin into VizPlotBottom
    add VizPlotH to VizPlotBottom
    ! Half the frame's height, which is half a row of text once `VizPlaceY` divides by the window: the
    ! glyphs of a row are centred in it, so a line's own y is that much below the top of its row. Kept as
    ! a value rather than done in the mapping so the division stays a division by the window alone.
    divide VizPlotH by 2 giving VizRowHalf
    ! The rows a window may not go below. The text is drawn at eighteen units a line, so a window of
    ! this many rows is one where a source line is already as tall as it is drawn — there is no more
    ! detail to be had past it, and zooming further only blows the picture up. Derived rather than
    ! chosen, so it follows the frame if the frame ever comes from the host.
    divide VizPlotH by 18 giving VizGlyphRows

    create VizCanvas in VizHost
    set attribute `viewBox` of VizCanvas to `0 0 1000 740`
    set attribute `preserveAspectRatio` of VizCanvas to `xMidYMid meet`
    set attribute `font-size` of VizCanvas to 15
    set attribute `fill` of VizCanvas to `#8b93a1`

    create VizRuler in VizHost ! §3–§5
    set the style of VizRuler to `position:absolute;left:-10000px;top:0;visibility:hidden;white-space:pre;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px`

    create VizFrame in VizCanvas
    set attribute `x` of VizFrame to 60
    set attribute `y` of VizFrame to 60
    set attribute `width` of VizFrame to 880
    set attribute `height` of VizFrame to 580
    set attribute `fill` of VizFrame to `none`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizFrame to `#3a4048`

    create VizPane in VizCanvas style `overflow:hidden` ! §6–§7
    set attribute `preserveAspectRatio` of VizPane to `none`
    set attribute `x` of VizPane to VizMargin
    set attribute `y` of VizPane to VizMargin
    set attribute `width` of VizPane to VizPlotW
    set attribute `height` of VizPane to VizPlotH
    create VizRedact in VizPane ! §8–§10
    set attribute `fill` of VizRedact to `#d6d6d6`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizRedact to `none`

    ! The picture, one document unit to one unit — the size the text is actually written at — and
    ! placed by the pane's viewBox rather than by its own rectangle. Its width and height are set by
    ! `VizSourceMeasure`, because they depend on the run.
    create VizGlyphs in VizPane
    set attribute `preserveAspectRatio` of VizGlyphs to `none`
    set attribute `x` of VizGlyphs to 0
    set attribute `y` of VizGlyphs to 0

    create VizPlot in VizCanvas style `overflow:hidden` ! §11–§12
    set attribute `preserveAspectRatio` of VizPlot to `none`
    set attribute `x` of VizPlot to VizMargin
    set attribute `y` of VizPlot to VizMargin
    set attribute `width` of VizPlot to VizPlotW
    set attribute `height` of VizPlot to VizPlotH
    set attribute `viewBox` of VizPlot to VizMargin cat ` ` cat VizMargin cat ` ` cat VizPlotW cat ` ` cat VizPlotH


    ! A rule per line the run touched, faint and across the whole frame. It is drawn *over* the source
    ! so a line can be picked out of the texture, and *under* everything else so it never competes
    ! with a mark: at low zoom a mark is a dot in mid-space, and the rule is what says which line the
    ! dot belongs to. Kept faint deliberately — it is a grid, not a reading.
    create VizRules in VizPlot
    set attribute `stroke` of VizRules to `#eee`
    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizRules to 1
    set attribute `fill` of VizRules to `none`

    create VizYLabel0 in VizCanvas
    create VizYLabel1 in VizCanvas
    create VizYLabel2 in VizCanvas
    create VizYLabel3 in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizYLabel0 to `end`
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizYLabel1 to `end`
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizYLabel2 to `end`
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizYLabel3 to `end`

    create VizXLabel0 in VizCanvas
    create VizXLabel1 in VizCanvas
    create VizXLabel2 in VizCanvas
    create VizXLabel3 in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizXLabel0 to `middle`
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizXLabel1 to `middle`
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizXLabel2 to `middle`
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizXLabel3 to `middle`

    ! The caption sits under the plot and names the window this run covered. A recording is a
    ! file beside the script, so a run that failed to record leaves the *previous* one in place
    ! and the picture looks exactly as convincing as a fresh one — the line numbers are what
    ! let a reader tell, and they are only useful if they are on the picture.
    create VizStatus in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizStatus to `start`
    create VizHitStatus in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizHitStatus to `start`

    ! ---- the scrollbars ----
    create VizBarV in VizCanvas ! §13–§14
    set attribute `x` of VizBarV to 944
    set attribute `y` of VizBarV to VizMargin
    set attribute `width` of VizBarV to 10
    set attribute `height` of VizBarV to VizPlotH
    set attribute `rx` of VizBarV to 5
    set attribute `fill` of VizBarV to `#7a8290`
    create VizHandleV in VizCanvas style `cursor:ns-resize`
    set attribute `x` of VizHandleV to 944
    set attribute `width` of VizHandleV to 10
    set attribute `rx` of VizHandleV to 5
    set attribute `fill` of VizHandleV to `#c9ced8`
    create VizBarH in VizCanvas
    set attribute `x` of VizBarH to VizMargin
    set attribute `y` of VizBarH to 646
    set attribute `width` of VizBarH to VizPlotW
    set attribute `height` of VizBarH to 10
    set attribute `rx` of VizBarH to 5
    set attribute `fill` of VizBarH to `#7a8290`
    create VizHandleH in VizCanvas style `cursor:ew-resize`
    set attribute `y` of VizHandleH to 646
    set attribute `height` of VizHandleH to 10
    set attribute `rx` of VizHandleH to 5
    set attribute `fill` of VizHandleH to `#c9ced8`
    create VizBarClip in VizCanvas ! §15–§16
    set attribute `x` of VizBarClip to VizMargin
    set attribute `y` of VizBarClip to 40
    set attribute `width` of VizBarClip to VizPlotW
    set attribute `height` of VizBarClip to 2
    set attribute `fill` of VizBarClip to `#d8d4cb`
    create VizClipBand in VizCanvas
    set attribute `y` of VizClipBand to 34
    set attribute `height` of VizClipBand to 12
    set attribute `fill` of VizClipBand to `#c9d2e2`
    create VizClipMarkIn in VizCanvas style `cursor:ew-resize`
    set attribute `y` of VizClipMarkIn to 31
    set attribute `width` of VizClipMarkIn to 4
    set attribute `height` of VizClipMarkIn to 20
    set attribute `rx` of VizClipMarkIn to 1
    set attribute `fill` of VizClipMarkIn to `#5c6b7f`
    create VizClipMarkOut in VizCanvas style `cursor:ew-resize`
    set attribute `y` of VizClipMarkOut to 31
    set attribute `width` of VizClipMarkOut to 4
    set attribute `height` of VizClipMarkOut to 20
    set attribute `rx` of VizClipMarkOut to 1
    set attribute `fill` of VizClipMarkOut to `#5c6b7f`
    ! The two guides, from the markers down through the plot: one unit wide, so they read as a plumb line
    ! rather than as another rule. Their height is the plot's, and their y is where the bar's band ends.
    create VizClipGuideIn in VizCanvas
    set attribute `width` of VizClipGuideIn to 1
    set attribute `height` of VizClipGuideIn to VizPlotH
    set attribute `fill` of VizClipGuideIn to `#5c6b7f`
    create VizClipGuideOut in VizCanvas
    set attribute `width` of VizClipGuideOut to 1
    set attribute `height` of VizClipGuideOut to VizPlotH
    set attribute `fill` of VizClipGuideOut to `#5c6b7f`
    ! The notice and the working readout: the first on the bar's own row, the second on the strip below
    ! the plot that the bar used to occupy.
    create VizClipNotice in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizClipNotice to `start`
    set attribute `font-size` of VizClipNotice to 13
    set attribute `fill` of VizClipNotice to `#5c6b7f`
    move VizClipNotice to VizMargin 24
    create VizBusy in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizBusy to `start`
    set attribute `font-size` of VizBusy to 15
    set attribute `font-weight` of VizBusy to 600
    set attribute `fill` of VizBusy to `#b2431f`
    move VizBusy to VizMargin 690
    ! The bar's axis starts at the pane's own zero — the fit starts there, so a marker at the far left is
    ! step zero and lines up with the picture's left edge — and pass one replaces both ends with the
    ! working set's own extent.
    put 0 into VizRunFrom
    put 0 into VizRunTo
    ! And where the two markers stand before the first draw has put them anywhere, so a press in the
    ! strip meets two numbers rather than two unset names — the same guard the scrollbar handles get by
    ! being drawn before anything can press them.
    put 0 into VizSelFrom
    put 0 into VizSelTo
    put VizMargin into VizClipInX
    put VizPlotRight into VizClipOutX
    put VizMargin into VizClipInXW
    put VizPlotRight into VizClipOutXW
    put 8 into VizCtlY ! §17
    put 18 into VizCtlH
    put 84 into VizResetW
    put 84 into VizOuterW
    put 84 into VizKeepW
    take VizResetW from VizPlotRight giving VizResetX
    take VizOuterW from VizResetX giving VizOuterX
    take 8 from VizOuterX
    take VizKeepW from VizOuterX giving VizKeepX
    take 8 from VizKeepX
    create VizKeepBox in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`
    set attribute `x` of VizKeepBox to VizKeepX
    set attribute `y` of VizKeepBox to VizCtlY
    set attribute `width` of VizKeepBox to VizKeepW
    set attribute `height` of VizKeepBox to VizCtlH
    set attribute `rx` of VizKeepBox to 3
    set attribute `fill` of VizKeepBox to `#e6e2d8`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizKeepBox to `#9aa4b1`
    create VizKeepText in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizKeepText to `middle`
    set attribute `font-size` of VizKeepText to 11
    set attribute `fill` of VizKeepText to `#40506b`
    set the text of VizKeepText to `←--- ---→`
    put VizKeepW into VizMove
    divide VizMove by 2
    add VizKeepX to VizMove
    move VizKeepText to VizMove 21
    create VizOuterBox in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`
    set attribute `x` of VizOuterBox to VizOuterX
    set attribute `y` of VizOuterBox to VizCtlY
    set attribute `width` of VizOuterBox to VizOuterW
    set attribute `height` of VizOuterBox to VizCtlH
    set attribute `rx` of VizOuterBox to 3
    set attribute `fill` of VizOuterBox to `#e6e2d8`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizOuterBox to `#9aa4b1`
    create VizOuterText in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizOuterText to `middle`
    set attribute `font-size` of VizOuterText to 11
    set attribute `fill` of VizOuterText to `#40506b`
    set the text of VizOuterText to `---→ ←---`
    put VizOuterW into VizMove
    divide VizMove by 2
    add VizOuterX to VizMove
    move VizOuterText to VizMove 21
    create VizResetBox in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`
    set attribute `x` of VizResetBox to VizResetX
    set attribute `y` of VizResetBox to VizCtlY
    set attribute `width` of VizResetBox to VizResetW
    set attribute `height` of VizResetBox to VizCtlH
    set attribute `rx` of VizResetBox to 3
    set attribute `fill` of VizResetBox to `#e6e2d8`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizResetBox to `#9aa4b1`
    create VizResetText in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizResetText to `middle`
    set attribute `font-size` of VizResetText to 11
    set attribute `fill` of VizResetText to `#40506b`
    set the text of VizResetText to `reset`
    put VizResetW into VizMove
    divide VizMove by 2
    add VizResetX to VizMove
    ! The baseline: `VizCtlY` plus thirteen, which sets eleven-unit glyphs inside an eighteen-unit box.
    move VizResetText to VizMove 21

    ! **The tooltip, created last so that it draws in front of everything above.** It is empty at rest —
    ! a box with nothing in it and its fill taken away — because an empty `svgtext` still draws nothing
    ! only if its words are empty, and a `rect` is *always* drawn. So the box is the thing switched on
    ! and off, and the words go with it: see `VizHover`.
    create VizTipBox in VizCanvas
    set attribute `rx` of VizTipBox to 3
    set attribute `fill` of VizTipBox to `none`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizTipBox to `#9aa4b1`
    create VizTip in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizTip to `start`
    set attribute `font-size` of VizTip to 11
    set attribute `fill` of VizTip to `#40506b`
    put `` into VizTipText
    set the text of VizTip to VizTipText
    put 0 into VizHoverShown

    ! **The kept list's holders, and the history's.** `VizKeepCap` is how many ranges a list may hold; the
    ! spare pair is the same size because a cut to the outside builds a whole new list before it replaces
    ! the live one. The history is 32 deep, which is more cuts than anybody makes on one recording, and
    ! each entry is a few bytes of text.
    put 16 into VizKeepCap
    set the elements of VizKeepFrom to VizKeepCap
    set the elements of VizKeepTo to VizKeepCap
    set the elements of VizKeepFrom2 to VizKeepCap
    set the elements of VizKeepTo2 to VizKeepCap
    set the elements of VizHist to 32

    create VizHeat0 in VizPlot ! §18–§19
    set attribute `fill` of VizHeat0 to `none`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizHeat0 to `#3b7dd8`
    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat0 to `round`
    create VizHeat1 in VizPlot
    set attribute `fill` of VizHeat1 to `none`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizHeat1 to `#8f5fd8`
    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat1 to `round`
    create VizHeat2 in VizPlot
    set attribute `fill` of VizHeat2 to `none`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizHeat2 to `#d2467f`
    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat2 to `round`
    create VizHeat3 in VizPlot
    set attribute `fill` of VizHeat3 to `none`
    set attribute `stroke` of VizHeat3 to `#e5484d`
    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat3 to `round`

    put `#e0a44a` into VizColourCall ! §20
    put `#43c6a8` into VizColourJump
    put `#c07ad0` into VizColourReturn
    create VizFlowCall in VizPlot
    set attribute `stroke` of VizFlowCall to VizColourCall
    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizFlowCall to 2
    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowCall to `none`
    create VizFlowJump in VizPlot
    set attribute `stroke` of VizFlowJump to VizColourJump
    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizFlowJump to 2
    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowJump to `none`
    create VizClipMaskIn in VizCanvas ! §21
    set attribute `y` of VizClipMaskIn to VizMargin
    set attribute `height` of VizClipMaskIn to VizPlotH
    set attribute `fill` of VizClipMaskIn to `#f2efe9`
    set attribute `fill-opacity` of VizClipMaskIn to `0.62`
    create VizClipMaskOut in VizCanvas
    set attribute `y` of VizClipMaskOut to VizMargin
    set attribute `height` of VizClipMaskOut to VizPlotH
    set attribute `fill` of VizClipMaskOut to `#f2efe9`
    set attribute `fill-opacity` of VizClipMaskOut to `0.62`
    create VizFlowReturn in VizPlot
    set attribute `stroke` of VizFlowReturn to VizColourReturn
    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizFlowReturn to 2
    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowReturn to `none`

    ! The key, laid out once — only the ranges it names change with the run. It sits on the caption's
    ! line, in the right half of the frame so a long caption cannot run into it, and its swatches are
    ! the ramp's own colours, in the right-hand half of the foot: which run this is and what the
    ! colours mean, side by side.
    divide VizPlotW by 2 giving VizKeyX
    add VizMargin to VizKeyX
    divide VizPlotW by 8 giving VizKeyPitch

    ! **The flow key, which is now the whole key.** It says *how* control reached a line, which the
    ! marks' colours cannot say at all. The heat key that shared this row is gone with the counts it
    ! named: the ramp means "how hot, by when", and no range of visit counts can stand for that.
    divide VizPlotW by 2 giving VizKeyX
    add VizMargin to VizKeyX
    create VizFlowKeyMark0 in VizCanvas
    set attribute `x` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to VizKeyX
    set attribute `y` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to 700
    set attribute `width` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to 12
    set attribute `height` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to 2
    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to VizColourCall
    create VizFlowKeyLabel0 in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizFlowKeyLabel0 to `start`
    add 18 to VizKeyX
    move VizFlowKeyLabel0 to VizKeyX 711
    set the text of VizFlowKeyLabel0 to StrFlowCall
    add VizKeyPitch to VizKeyX
    take 18 from VizKeyX
    create VizFlowKeyMark1 in VizCanvas
    set attribute `x` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to VizKeyX
    set attribute `y` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to 700
    set attribute `width` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to 12
    set attribute `height` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to 2
    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to VizColourJump
    create VizFlowKeyLabel1 in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizFlowKeyLabel1 to `start`
    add 18 to VizKeyX
    move VizFlowKeyLabel1 to VizKeyX 711
    set the text of VizFlowKeyLabel1 to StrFlowJump
    add VizKeyPitch to VizKeyX
    take 18 from VizKeyX
    create VizFlowKeyMark2 in VizCanvas
    set attribute `x` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to VizKeyX
    set attribute `y` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to 700
    set attribute `width` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to 12
    set attribute `height` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to 2
    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to VizColourReturn
    create VizFlowKeyLabel2 in VizCanvas
    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizFlowKeyLabel2 to `start`
    add 18 to VizKeyX
    move VizFlowKeyLabel2 to VizKeyX 711
    set the text of VizFlowKeyLabel2 to StrFlowReturn
    add VizKeyPitch to VizKeyX
    take 18 from VizKeyX

    put 1 into VizReady
    return

VizBuilt:
!! @hash 765a73c5
!! @verified f99c111e
!!!
!! What a draw does, in order.
!!
!! Pass one measures the run — the lines it touched, the steps it took, the busiest of them — and everything below is drawn from those measurements: the rules from pass one's set of named lines, the marks and the transfers from the same walk at the scales it settled. Nothing below re-reads the recording to decide anything pass one could have decided, which is what keeps a redraw cheap and the picture self-consistent.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **The drawing is swapped, not cleared and redrawn.** Everything a draw produces is built in the variables below — the four mark strings, the flow string, every label's text and the picture's window — and *nothing* is written to the DOM until the end, in one run of statements that cannot be interrupted. So the screen always shows one whole picture or another, never a half-built one, and there is no buffer to keep: the buffer is the variables and the swap is the last block.
!!
!! §2 **Why nothing is written here any more, and it is worth knowing.** This block used to blank the marks and every label before the passes began, on the reasoning that a redraw interrupted by a yield would otherwise show the last run's marks under the new run's axis. It did not, because the axis and the marks are written in the same uninterrupted run — and it made things worse, because the passes *do* yield (every hundred marks, so a long draw cannot freeze the browser) and a blank is exactly what the browser then painted: the flash on a slow machine. A redraw now leaves the previous picture up until the next one is complete, which is what a double buffer would do.
!!
!! §3 What remains here is the resetting of the accumulators, which are grown by `cat` and would otherwise double up. Nothing else may live here: no value may depend on what the passes below measure, because on a redraw it would silently carry the *previous* draw's number. The size of a mark is where that cost two rounds to spot — its own note says how.
    ! ---- drawn every time ----
    put `` into VizHeatD ! §1–§3
    put `` into VizHeatE
    put `` into VizHeatF
    put `` into VizHeatG
    put `` into VizFlowCallD
    put `` into VizFlowJumpD
    put `` into VizFlowReturnD
    put `` into VizRulesD
!! @hash 3588a844
!! @verified 55620f7b
!!!
!! The frame the picture is drawn in is the run's own extent, not the file's.
!!
!! The rules cover exactly the lines the recording names — an arrival, or either end of a transfer — and the axis is scaled to the steps it took, so a picture of a run that touched nine lines shows nine.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **Nothing below depends on the window, so it is built once per picture and not once per gesture.** It reads the recording and nothing else — the extent the axis is scaled to, the rule set, and the size one mark is drawn at, which is a judgement off the span. It used to run above the test for a new recording, so every wheel notch re-read the whole trace *and* paid the `wait` this loop breathes with, which alone is worth about a second and a half on a big one. The condition is the same one the fit is decided by, read here because the fit comes later and cannot be asked yet; the two cannot disagree, since both read `VizTrace` and `VizSource`.
!!
!! §2 **The run's width in steps, which is not `VizMaxSteps` once a clip is on.** `VizMaxSteps` is the run's *last* step, wherever the picture starts; the fit, the clamp, the bars and the handle drag all want the width — how much run there is to move a window over — and with a clip that is the clip's own span. Unclipped `VizMinSteps` is 0, so this is `VizMaxSteps` and every one of those four sites reads exactly what it always did.
!!
!! §3 **The clip's own axis: the working set's extent, always.** It used to be the recording's own steps while there was no clip and to stand still once there was one, which is why a cut left the markers where they had been chosen — they were placed on the recording's scale while the picture above them was the range. Measured here, over the set the picture is of, as part of the walk pass one has just made, so the measurement is free: measuring it separately cost 37 seconds on a 435KB recording, which is what walking 2,300 records at 15ms a record costs.
    ! ---- pass one: the frame the picture is drawn in ----
    put 0 into VizPictureNew ! §1
    if VizTrace is not VizLastTrace put 1 into VizPictureNew
    if VizSource is not VizLastSource put 1 into VizPictureNew
    ! **The range is part of the key, and this is one of the two places it has to be.** A clip does
    ! not change the recording's text, so without this test the guard would let a clipped draw
    ! through on the previous picture's measurements — its extent, its rule set, its busiest line.
    ! The same test is made at the fit below; the two decide the same thing and must not disagree.
    if VizSetTag is not VizLastSetTag put 1 into VizPictureNew
    if VizPictureNew is 0 go to VizFrameDone
    ! A leading and trailing space, so that every entry added is padded on both sides and ` 15 ` can
    ! never match inside ` 115 `. Cleared with the pass that fills it rather than with the paths above,
    ! because a gesture no longer runs the pass and an empty set is a picture with no rules.
    put ` ` into VizRuledLines
    put 0 into VizBreathe
    put 0 into VizMaxSteps
    put 0 into VizMaxCount
    put json of `{}` into VizRunCounts
    put 0 into VizIndex
    while VizEvents has element VizIndex
    begin
        ! Give the browser a turn every hundred marks. Without this the page's thread is held for the
        ! whole pass — tens of seconds on a big recording — and the browser offers to stop the script
        ! instead of painting the frame that is already built.
        add 1 to VizBreathe
        if VizBreathe is greater than 100
        begin
            put 0 into VizBreathe
            wait 1 millis
        end
        put element VizIndex of VizEvents into VizEvent
        put property `cat` of VizEvent into VizCategory
        put property `args` of VizEvent into VizArgs
        if VizCategory is `window`
        begin
            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizMaxSteps
            add 0 to VizMaxSteps
        end
        if VizCategory is `anchor`
        begin
            put property `line` of VizArgs into VizLine
            add 0 to VizLine
            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps
            add 0 to VizSteps
            if VizSteps is greater than VizMaxSteps put VizSteps into VizMaxSteps
            ! How often this line is reached in the whole recording, kept for the ramp: the busiest
            ! line is what the bands are scaled against, so it has to be known before any mark is
            ! coloured. See the mark loop.
            put property VizLine of VizRunCounts into VizCount
            if VizCount is not greater than 0 put 0 into VizCount
            add 1 to VizCount
            set property VizLine of VizRunCounts to VizCount
            if VizCount is greater than VizMaxCount put VizCount into VizMaxCount
            gosub to VizRuleAdd
        end
        if VizCategory is `transfer`
        begin
            put property `from_line` of VizArgs into VizLine
            add 0 to VizLine
            gosub to VizRuleAdd
            put property `to_line` of VizArgs into VizLine
            add 0 to VizLine
            gosub to VizRuleAdd
        end
        add 1 to VizIndex
    end

    if VizMaxSteps is less than 1 put 1 into VizMaxSteps
    take VizMinSteps from VizMaxSteps giving VizSpanSteps ! §2
    if VizSpanSteps is less than 1 put 1 into VizSpanSteps
    put VizMinSteps into VizRunFrom ! §3
    put VizMaxSteps into VizRunTo
    ! **The recording's own last step, remembered while nothing has been cut.** A cut to the *outside* has
    ! to name the far end of the run, and this is the one place that measures it: pass one over the whole
    ! recording, which is what the set is until the first cut. Kept rather than measured again, because
    ! measuring it separately is a walk of every record — 37 seconds on a 435KB trace.
    if VizKeepN is 0 put VizMaxSteps into VizAllTo
    ! **And the selection goes home whenever the applied range changes**, which is the reader's expectation:
    ! the bar says where the selection sits in what is on screen, and a cut has just changed what that is.
    ! Keyed on the range rather than reset every draw, or a pan — which redraws — would throw away a
    ! selection the reader had just made.
    if VizSetTag is not VizSelKey
    begin
        put VizRunFrom into VizSelFrom
        put VizRunTo into VizSelTo
        put VizSetTag into VizSelKey
    end
!! @hash 77844de2
!! @verified 6e051bfd
!!!
!! A mark has to stay legible at the pane's scale, or it is not a mark.
!!
!! Its size is the one number here that is a judgement rather than a measurement, and it has a history — see §1 for the clamp that made every redraw draw a different size.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 One size for every mark, and it is set *here* because this is the first point at which the span is known. It used to live in the per-draw block at the top of this pass, which is before pass one has measured anything, so it divided VizPlotH by the span of the *previous* draw — and by nothing at all on the first draw, where an empty span divides to Infinity and clamps down to the ceiling of 14. So a first draw drew 14-unit dots and every redraw drew 3-unit ones, which is what "returning to Graph reduces the markers to tiny dots" was.
!!
!! §2 The row-proportional rule it replaces also only ever applied between 41 and 193 lines: shorter runs were clamped up to 14 anyway, and on a long run a row is a couple of units, so the floor of 3 was a pinprick by construction. That clamp — not the cap — was the mistake, and a mark has to stay legible at the pane's scale to be a mark at all. When this view takes the host panel's size rather than asserting a 1000x700 frame, this is the number that should come from it.
    ! ---- the size of a mark ----
    put 14 into VizDotSize ! §1–§2
!! @hash a051e625
!! @verified 74df2991
!!!
!! The window is held as two ranges in the trace's own units, never as a scale factor.
!!
!! A factor would have to be relative to a fit that changes with the recording, and an absolute one ends up pinned at one end — the prototype's own lesson, where the same control read 51.6 px a step for one window and 0.7 for another. Multiplying before dividing keeps that arithmetic exact, as it does for the marks.
!! The window doubles as the reset. Both gestures clamp at the edges of the run, so zooming out returns to the whole picture and panning stops at its ends; there is no reset button to find. The window is fitted once per *recording* and kept across visits, so the pane comes back as it was left; opening a different run is what re-fits it, and `VizReset` is there for a host that wants to force one.
!!
!! The zoom stops where the text is drawn at its own size. A source line is eighteen units tall, so a window of fewer than `VizGlyphRows` rows would only blow the text up, and there is nothing more to see. Applied before the clamp to the run, so a recording shorter than that many lines simply has no vertical zoom — the whole run is already legible and there is nothing to zoom into.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 Fitted to the whole run the first time that recording is drawn and left alone after that: a redraw is what a gesture causes, and re-fitting there would undo the gesture that asked for it. The recording is the key rather than the visit, because leaving the pane and coming back to the *same* run should find the window exactly as it was — coming back is how a reader works, and a visit that resets is a visit that has to be redone.
!!
!! §2 The comparison is over the whole recording, and it is the honest one: a recording is a file beside the script, so editing the script leaves it alone and the window still means what it meant, while re-running the script writes a new one and the window is fitted again.
!!
!! §3 **The range joins the key here too, and this is the second of the two places.** A clip changes what the picture is *of*, so the window has to be fitted afresh or the fit would stay fitted to the part that has just been cut away: the extent, `VizFitXW` — which is what the status line measures the zoom against — and the bars would all still describe the whole run. Both keys are advanced at this one point, where a picture is decided, so they cannot drift apart.
!!
!! §4 **A cut fits the steps afresh and leaves the lines exactly where the reader put them.** The steps are what the cut changed, so the horizontal window is fitted to the new range; the lines are what the reader was looking at, and a clip that threw their vertical zoom away each time made choosing a range a punishment. `VizFitXW`/`VizFitYH` are still moved, because they are the numbers the status line measures the zoom *against* — so the percentage goes on meaning what it says.
!!
!! §5 Giving the whole recording back is not a cut: it is fitted *both* ways, so it takes the ordinary fit below rather than the steps-only one here. `VizViewFit` is cleared for it because the fit being asked for is this one, and the path below is what writes it — leaving it set sent the picture to `VizWindow` with the *previous* window, which the harness caught as an unclip that left the steps at the clipped range.
!!
!! §6 Computed here, at the top of the draw, because two things need it and only one of them is the pane: the pane's `viewBox` is this, and so is the factor that turns a line's length in characters into a place in the frame — which is what the left end of every rule is made of. It used to be computed inside `VizSourcePicture`, which is the last thing a draw does, so a rule reading it would have been reading the draw before: the trap this file has already paid for twice, in the size of a mark and in a value measured before the pass that measures it.
!!
!! §7 The window's `y` is in the *document's* own units so that a row of text lands on the line the axis names, and its width is what the frame's aspect allows at that row height — there is no wider one.
VizFrameDone:
	! ---- the window on the run ----
    put 0 into VizNewRun ! §1–§2
    if VizTrace is not VizLastTrace put 1 into VizNewRun
    put 0 into VizNewSet ! §3
    if VizSetTag is not VizLastSetTag put 1 into VizNewSet
    put VizTrace into VizLastTrace
    put VizSetTag into VizLastSetTag
    ! The picture first, because the fit below is over the extent it measured.
    if VizNewRun is 1 go to VizPictureFresh
    if VizSource is not VizLastSource go to VizPictureFresh
    go to VizPictureDone
VizPictureFresh:
    gosub to VizSourceMeasure
    put VizSource into VizLastSource
    ! **A new picture can mean a new extent, so the window is fitted again.** The extent is the *file's*
    ! line count, and editing the script changes it — so a window that was fitted to the old file can
    ! be left off the end of the new one. The recording is not the only input that can move the ground
    ! under the window, and this is the one that does it.
    gosub to VizReset
VizPictureDone:
    if VizNewRun is 1 gosub to VizReset
    if VizNewSet is 1 ! §4
    begin
        if VizKeepSet is 0 ! §5
        begin
            put 0 into VizViewFit
            go to VizPictureFit
        end
        put VizMinSteps into VizViewX0
        put VizSpanSteps into VizViewXW
        put VizSpanSteps into VizFitXW
        put VizSpanLines into VizFitYH
        put 1 into VizViewFit
        gosub to VizClamp
        go to VizWindow
    end
    if VizViewFit is 1 go to VizWindow
VizPictureFit:
    ! The run starts at `VizMinSteps` — 0 unclipped, the clip's own start when clipped — so a clip
    ! fits the picture to the range the reader chose and not to the whole recording they cut it from.
    put VizMinSteps into VizViewX0
    put VizSpanSteps into VizViewXW
    put VizMinLine into VizViewY0
    put VizSpanLines into VizViewYH
    ! The fit is kept, because it is what the status line measures the zoom *against*: the two window
    ! sizes on their own say what is in view but not how far in it is, and "how far in" is the part a
    ! reader can recognise. It is the only thing here that a second copy is right for — recomputing it
    ! would mean asking `VizClamp` what it allowed, which is not a question it answers.
    put VizSpanSteps into VizFitXW
    put VizSpanLines into VizFitYH
    put 1 into VizViewFit
VizWindow:
    gosub to VizClamp

    ! ---- the window on the run, in the pane's own units ----
    take VizMinLine from VizViewY0 giving VizWindowY ! §6–§7
    multiply VizWindowY by 18
    multiply VizViewYH by 18 giving VizWindowH
    multiply VizWindowH by VizPlotW giving VizWindowW
    divide VizWindowW by VizPlotH
!! @hash 99c976c3
!! @verified b8945c8a
!!!
!! The source is drawn behind the heat, once, and shown through a window.
!!
!! An element is a declared symbol, so there cannot be one `svgtext` per source line — but one `<image>` carries the whole run, and its `href` is an SVG *document* the view builds itself out of `json split` and `replace`. That is why it is a document rather than a canvas raster: the language can already build one, so nothing new had to be invented to reach a canvas it has no words for, and it stays vector, so the text does not go soft past the scale it was rendered at.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 Built when either of its two inputs has changed, and not otherwise. A new script is new text to draw; a new recording is a new range of lines to draw it for. Both are compared as whole strings, which is what they are — and it is the same reasoning as the window's, on the other input: the thing that decides is the *content*, not the visit. Drawing on every draw would be the redraw Graham asked to be rid of, and drawing only on a new recording would leave an edited script showing its old text.
!!
!! §2 No scale factors are kept, and that is deliberate. AllSpeak's arithmetic is integer-first and division truncates (`divide 10 by 3` is 3), so a fraction here would be lost: a 626-line span against a 580-unit pane gives 0, and the picture would collapse onto a single line. Multiplying *before* dividing keeps the intermediate large and the result exact to a unit — the documented scaled-integer pattern, applied where it matters most.
    ! ---- the source behind the heat ----
    ! §1

    ! §2
!! @hash f9d49283
!! @verified d71d28a1
!!!
!! One path per kind of mark.
!!
!! An AllSpeak element is a declared symbol, so a script cannot create N elements for N marks. What it can do is grow one path's `d` attribute by concatenation and let the browser draw the whole layer from it, so the marks cost string building rather than DOM nodes. A trace of 9 arrivals and 10 transfers is two elements. Per-mark colour would need one path per colour band, which is what the heat ramp will use.
!! And the heat is relative, not absolute. Each mark's colour is its line's *running* count against the busiest line in the recording, so every line starts cold and the top of the ramp means the hottest line in *this* run. The editor used to send per-line counts for this and the view no longer reads them: they arrive keyed to a neighbouring line, so a mark's colour was its neighbour's heat. See the note in TODO.md.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 A mark outside the window is left out rather than drawn and clipped. There is no clip rectangle to draw into — the plugin has no element for one — and a mark outside the frame paints over the axis and the key, so leaving it out is the honest version. The tests are strict, which by itself lets a mark *on* an edge through: 940 is the right edge, and `940 is greater than 940` is false, so a mark on the last step is kept. **How hot the line had become *by this mark*, in the run's own scale.** The count is the view's own and it is a *running* one: how many times this line has been reached so far. The band is that count against the *busiest line in the recording*, so the ramp is **relative, not absolute** — every line starts cold, and the hottest line in the run is what the top of the range means. That is what makes the picture work as a first impression: a run whose busiest line is reached six times and one reached six thousand both use the whole ramp, and neither is a flat colour.
!!
!! §2 Two earlier versions of this were wrong, and both are worth remembering. The first shaded by the *clock* — `band x steps / maxSteps` — which reaches the top colour only at the very last step of a run, where no mark sits, so a picture drawn that way shows blue, violet and magenta and never red; Graham read the missing red off a screenshot before I did. The second counted in a fixed ladder — one visit, two to three, four to seven, eight or more — which is *absolute*: on a busy run every mark is at the top of the ramp and the picture is a flat wash.
!!
!! §3 Counting here rather than reading the editor's `line_counts` also removes the one input that could disagree with the marks: those counts arrive keyed to a different line from the anchors that place the marks, by one or two, so a mark's colour was its *neighbour's* heat. See the note in TODO.md.
!!
!! §4 `multiply` before `divide` because the runtime truncates every division and a proportion computed the other way rounds to nothing on a big run.
!!
!! §5 One x decides a transfer, because a transfer is a vertical segment: its two ends differ only in their line, so if the x is outside the window the whole segment is.
!!
!! §6 **And it is drawn whenever it *crosses* the window — in full, at its true ends.** Two earlier answers to this were wrong in the same way. Clamping each end to the frame put an end on the border rather than on a line, which reads as a fault; leaving the whole segment out then lost the fact itself, because a transfer from off-screen is the one thing a picture of a *sliced* run can only say by showing a line leave the frame. Graham's correction: *"the panel displaying all of each line and leaving the existing mask to hide what goes outside."* So the ends stay where the lines are and the plot's nested viewport cuts them off, which is the same mechanism the source picture is shown through. A transfer wholly above or wholly below the window is still left out — it is not in the picture at all.
!!
!! §7 The head is a chevron drawn in the path data, not a marker element — the plugin has none, and a stroked chevron needs no fill, so it joins the segment's own stroke.
!!
!! §8 It goes at the *destination* end, `to_line`, with its wings behind the tip rather than in front of it — and which side is behind depends on which way the segment runs, so the wings are placed nine units back from the tip in the direction it came from. No head is drawn when both ends are the same line: a head with no direction would be claiming one.
    ! ---- pass two: the marks, at those scales ----
    ! How many times each line has been reached *in this draw*, which is what the ramp is drawn from.
    ! It is thrown away and started again on every draw because a redraw is a recount: the window may
    ! have moved, but the run has not, so the counts have to come out the same.
    put json of `{}` into VizSeen
    put 0 into VizIndex
    while VizEvents has element VizIndex
    begin
        ! Give the browser a turn every hundred marks. Without this the page's thread is held for the
        ! whole pass — tens of seconds on a big recording — and the browser offers to stop the script
        ! instead of painting the frame that is already built.
        add 1 to VizBreathe
        if VizBreathe is greater than 100
        begin
            put 0 into VizBreathe
            wait 1 millis
        end
        put element VizIndex of VizEvents into VizEvent
        put property `cat` of VizEvent into VizCategory
        put property `args` of VizEvent into VizArgs
        if VizCategory is `anchor`
        begin
            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps
            add 0 to VizSteps
            take VizViewX0 from VizSteps giving VizX
            multiply VizX by VizPlotW
            divide VizX by VizViewXW
            add VizMargin to VizX
            put property `line` of VizArgs into VizLine
            add 0 to VizLine
            ! The line's own y, which is the middle of the row of text it names — `VizPlaceY` is the one
            ! place that mapping lives, because this, the rules and the axis labels have to agree.
            put VizLine into VizTickValue
            gosub to VizPlaceY
            put VizTickPos into VizY
            put property VizLine of VizSeen into VizBand ! §1–§4
            if VizBand is not greater than 0 put 0 into VizBand
            add 1 to VizBand
            set property VizLine of VizSeen to VizBand
            multiply VizBand by 3 giving VizCount
            divide VizCount by VizMaxCount
            if VizCount is greater than 3 put 3 into VizCount
            put VizCount into VizBand

            put 0 into VizOutside
            if VizX is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside
            if VizX is greater than VizPlotRight put 1 into VizOutside
            if VizY is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside
            if VizY is greater than VizPlotBottom put 1 into VizOutside
            if VizOutside is 0
            begin
                if VizBand is 0 put VizHeatD cat `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `h0.01` into VizHeatD
                if VizBand is 1 put VizHeatE cat `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `h0.01` into VizHeatE
                if VizBand is 2 put VizHeatF cat `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `h0.01` into VizHeatF
                if VizBand is 3 put VizHeatG cat `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `h0.01` into VizHeatG
            end
        end
        if VizCategory is `transfer`
        begin
            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps
            add 0 to VizSteps
            take VizViewX0 from VizSteps giving VizX
            multiply VizX by VizPlotW
            divide VizX by VizViewXW
            add VizMargin to VizX
            put property `from_line` of VizArgs into VizLine
            add 0 to VizLine
            put VizLine into VizTickValue
            gosub to VizPlaceY
            put VizTickPos into VizY
            put property `to_line` of VizArgs into VizLine
            add 0 to VizLine
            put VizLine into VizTickValue
            gosub to VizPlaceY
            put VizTickPos into VizY2
            put 0 into VizOutside ! §5–§6
            if VizX is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside
            if VizX is greater than VizPlotRight put 1 into VizOutside
            if VizY is less than VizMargin and VizY2 is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside
            if VizY is greater than VizPlotBottom and VizY2 is greater than VizPlotBottom put 1 into VizOutside
            if VizOutside is 0
            begin
                put property `kind` of VizArgs into VizKind ! §7–§8
                take 5 from VizX giving VizHeadL
                add 5 to VizX giving VizHeadR
                if VizY2 is greater than VizY take 9 from VizY2 giving VizHeadY
                if VizY2 is less than VizY add 9 to VizY2 giving VizHeadY
                put `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `L` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY2 into VizSegment
                if VizY2 is not VizY put VizSegment cat `M` cat VizHeadL cat ` ` cat VizHeadY cat `L` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY2 cat `L` cat VizHeadR cat ` ` cat VizHeadY into VizSegment
                ! One path per kind, because the colour is the kind. `branch` — the compiler's own
                ! jumps — is left off rather than drawn faintly: scaffolding is not a program jump,
                ! and drawing it would put the `if` and `while` machinery into the flow.
                if VizKind is `call` put VizFlowCallD cat VizSegment into VizFlowCallD
                if VizKind is `jump` put VizFlowJumpD cat VizSegment into VizFlowJumpD
                if VizKind is `return` put VizFlowReturnD cat VizSegment into VizFlowReturnD
            end
        end
        add 1 to VizIndex
    end
!! @hash 5ba04be0
!! @verified 52ac5048
!!!
!! The status line exists to be quoted.
!!
!! It carries the numbers raw rather than in words, because a reader copying a line into a bug report needs it exact and the picture needs it short.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 Percentages rather than the window sizes alone, because that is the reading a person recognises, and the window as well, because a percentage names a size but not a position — two windows of the same size over different parts of the run are the same number and different pictures. Multiplying before dividing keeps the intermediate large: the runtime truncates every division, and a ratio computed the other way rounds a 125% zoom to 1. **The clip is stated, and it has to be** — but on its own row now, up on the bar, rather than leading this line: once a clip is on, the `visit N of T` the sidebar reads counts the arrivals *in the range* and the ramp's reddest mark is the range's busiest line, so a picture that did not say it was clipped would be a picture that lies about both. It ran into the flow key's words when it led this line, which is what moved it. See `VizClipEdge`.
!!
!! §2 **And it starts from empty.** This line is the accumulator, so dropping it as "the clip's reset" took the reset away with it and the status line grew by a copy of itself on every draw — which is what the harness caught, as every check that reads the window off this line.
!!
!! §3 **And how many records the picture is of, because that is what explains the speed of everything else.** A reader watching a clipped picture get quicker has no way to see *why*, and the number is the reason: every walk in the drawing is proportional to it, and the runtime re-reads the whole array per record. Graham asked for it on 2026-10-07 for exactly that — "to help users get a feel for why the program gets sluggish".
!!
!! §4 **One line below it, and the pair is the whole of the foot.** The status says what the window is showing; this says what the mark under the pointer is, which is the one thing a row of dots cannot say for itself — every dot on a row names the same line and the same doc block, so the visit is what tells them apart. Set here rather than on a press because a press leaves the picture alone: the row is fixed for the life of the drawing, and `VizSelect` only changes the words.
    ! ---- the status line ----
    put `` into VizStatusText ! §1–§2
    multiply 100 by VizFitXW giving VizStatusZoom
    divide VizStatusZoom by VizViewXW
    put VizStatusText cat `zoom x ` cat VizStatusZoom cat `% y ` into VizStatusText
    multiply 100 by VizFitYH giving VizStatusZoom
    divide VizStatusZoom by VizViewYH
    put VizStatusText cat VizStatusZoom cat `%   ` into VizStatusText
    put VizViewX0 into VizStatusEnd
    add VizViewXW to VizStatusEnd
    take 1 from VizStatusEnd
    put VizStatusText cat `steps ` cat VizViewX0 cat `-` cat VizStatusEnd cat `, lines ` into VizStatusText
    put VizViewY0 into VizStatusEnd
    add VizViewYH to VizStatusEnd
    take 1 from VizStatusEnd
    set the text of VizStatus to VizStatusText cat VizViewY0 cat `-` cat VizStatusEnd cat `   ` cat VizClipKept cat ` records` ! §3
    ! On the flow key's own row, at the left. It used to sit a row below, and the caption — which
    ! printed the run's line range and step count, both of which are already in this line — sat
    ! opposite the keys. Graham's reading: the caption repeats what is visible elsewhere, so the
    ! status takes its row and the foot of the canvas loses one line.
    move VizStatus to 60 711
    move VizHitStatus to 60 728 ! §4
!! @hash 9e15932a
!! @verified 62d40caf
!!!
!! The axis is the picture's yardstick, and it is the same grid as the text.
!!
!! Its labels are placed with the mapping the marks use, so a label sits on the line it names at any zoom; a tick whose line falls outside the window is left blank rather than dragged to the edge, because the pool is four labels and a label that lies about where it sits is worse than an absent one.
    ! ---- the axis ----
    ! Four ticks a side, spaced by a third of the *window* rather than of the whole run, and placed
    ! with the same mapping the marks use, so a label sits on the line it names at any zoom. A tick
    ! whose line falls outside the window is left with no text rather than dragged to the edge: the
    ! pool is four labels, and a label that lies about where it sits is worse than an absent one.
    divide VizViewYH by 3 giving VizTickStep
    if VizTickStep is less than 1 put 1 into VizTickStep
    divide VizViewXW by 3 giving VizXStep
    if VizXStep is less than 1 put 1 into VizXStep

    put VizViewY0 into VizTickValue
    gosub to VizPlaceY
    move VizYLabel0 to 52 VizTickPos
    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the
    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.
    set the text of VizYLabel0 to ``
    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin
        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotBottom set the text of VizYLabel0 to VizTickValue

    add VizTickStep to VizTickValue
    gosub to VizPlaceY
    move VizYLabel1 to 52 VizTickPos
    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the
    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.
    set the text of VizYLabel1 to ``
    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin
        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotBottom set the text of VizYLabel1 to VizTickValue

    add VizTickStep to VizTickValue
    gosub to VizPlaceY
    move VizYLabel2 to 52 VizTickPos
    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the
    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.
    set the text of VizYLabel2 to ``
    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin
        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotBottom set the text of VizYLabel2 to VizTickValue

    add VizTickStep to VizTickValue
    gosub to VizPlaceY
    move VizYLabel3 to 52 VizTickPos
    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the
    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.
    set the text of VizYLabel3 to ``
    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin
        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotBottom set the text of VizYLabel3 to VizTickValue

    ! **The step-axis labels sit one row lower than they did**, at 670 rather than 664: the steps
    ! scrollbar runs along the foot of the frame and the labels' ascenders would otherwise touch it.
    ! 670 still leaves the caption below them at 690 and the heat key beside it at 679 alone.
    put VizViewX0 into VizTickValue
    gosub to VizPlaceX
    move VizXLabel0 to VizTickPos 670
    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the
    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.
    set the text of VizXLabel0 to ``
    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin
        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotRight set the text of VizXLabel0 to VizTickValue

    add VizXStep to VizTickValue
    gosub to VizPlaceX
    move VizXLabel1 to VizTickPos 670
    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the
    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.
    set the text of VizXLabel1 to ``
    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin
        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotRight set the text of VizXLabel1 to VizTickValue

    add VizXStep to VizTickValue
    gosub to VizPlaceX
    move VizXLabel2 to VizTickPos 670
    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the
    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.
    set the text of VizXLabel2 to ``
    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin
        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotRight set the text of VizXLabel2 to VizTickValue

    add VizXStep to VizTickValue
    gosub to VizPlaceX
    move VizXLabel3 to VizTickPos 670
    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the
    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.
    set the text of VizXLabel3 to ``
    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin
        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotRight set the text of VizXLabel3 to VizTickValue
!! @hash fafcd75b
!! @verified fafcd75b
!!!
!! A rule is a leader, and the flow is drawn in full and masked.
!!
!! Each rule runs from one em past the last character of the line it names to the right-hand edge of the frame, so it points at its line instead of crossing it — the indentation and the first word are the part the eye reads first, and they are the part a rule drawn from the frame's left edge used to run straight through. And a transfer is drawn at its true ends whenever it *crosses* the window, the part outside the frame being cut off by the plot — a nested `<svg>` the size of the frame, the same mechanism the source picture is shown through. **Two earlier answers to this were wrong in the same way:** clamping each end to the frame put an end on a border rather than on a line, which reads as a fault; leaving the whole segment out then lost the fact itself, because a line leaving the frame is the only way a picture of a *sliced* run can say that control came from somewhere off-screen.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 A hairline at every line the recording *names* — an arrival, or either end of a transfer — and at no other line. The set is built as the recording is read, and not from the window's per-line counts, which cover every line the run executed: on a recording that walks a chain of labels the counts carry more than twice as many lines as anything happened on, and a rule for each of those was a grid drawn over lines with nothing on them. Graham's "too many of them, and too dark".
!!
!! §2 It exists for the zoomed-out view, where a mark is a dot in mid-space and the text behind it is a texture: the rule is what says which line the dot belongs to, and it spares a reader the choice between zooming in to find out and hovering to ask the sidebar.
!!
!! §3 **The rule is a leader, not a line through the code.** It begins one em past the last character of the line it names and runs to the right-hand edge of the frame, so it points at its line rather than crossing it. What it spares is the indentation and the first word — the part that says what the line is, and the part the eye reads first.
!!
!! §4 The line's length is a count of *characters*, so it becomes a width by the same 8 units a column that the picture is measured in, and then by the factor the picture is drawn at: the pane holds `VizWindowW` of the document's units across `VizPlotW` of the frame. One em is the text's own font size — `VizGlyphEm`, not a number here, because the gap cleared has to be the size of the letters cleared or it is not a gap.
!!
!! §5 A row the picture does not draw has no characters to clear — a doc block is blank, see `VizSourceMeasure` — so its rule begins at the left edge and is the longest kind there is. The test is that one's, for the same reason: the left end is measured from what is drawn, not from what the file says.
!!
!! §6 **And when the line's own text reaches the frame's edge, the leader has nowhere to be.** The text is cut off there at that zoom — the price of the anchoring, above — so a rule beginning off the frame would leave its mark with no line to sit on, which is the one thing a rule is for. Those lines keep the rule they had, from the frame's left edge: they are the one case where crossing the text costs nothing, because what is crossed is already gone.
    ! ---- the rules ----
    put `` into VizRulesD ! §1–§2
    put VizMinLine into VizRuleLine
    add VizSpanLines to VizMinLine giving VizRuleEnd
    while VizRuleLine is less than VizRuleEnd
    begin
        put ` ` cat VizRuleLine cat ` ` into VizRuledLine
        if VizRuledLines includes VizRuledLine
        begin
            ! The line's own y, by the same `VizPlaceY` the marks and the labels use — so a rule runs
            ! through the middle of the text it names rather than above it.
            put VizRuleLine into VizTickValue
            gosub to VizPlaceY
            put VizTickPos into VizRuleY
            ! Only the rules in view. The loop runs over the whole run and the window is a slice of it,
            ! so a line outside the window maps to a y outside the frame — and a rule drawn there
            ! would be a line across the axis or the caption. The test is the marks' own test, and
            ! inclusive for the same reason: a line on the very edge is on the edge, not outside it.
            put 0 into VizOutside
            if VizRuleY is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside
            if VizRuleY is greater than VizPlotBottom put 1 into VizOutside
            put `` into VizSourceLine ! §3–§5
            take 1 from VizRuleLine giving VizSourceAt
            if VizSourceAt is less than VizSourceCount put element VizSourceAt of VizSourceLines into VizSourceLine
            if VizSourceLine starts with `!!` put `` into VizSourceLine
            multiply the length of VizSourceLine by 8 giving VizRuleX
            add VizGlyphEm to VizRuleX
            multiply VizRuleX by VizPlotW
            divide VizRuleX by VizWindowW
            add VizMargin to VizRuleX
            if VizRuleX is greater than VizPlotRight put VizMargin into VizRuleX ! §6
            if VizRuleX is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizRuleX
            ! To the frame's right-hand edge, which is as far as the drawing goes — unless the line is a
            ! label, whose name is drawn at that edge: then the rule stops one em short of the name, so the
            ! two read as a signpost beside the line rather than as one long line with a word on the end.
            ! The space the name and the gap need is measured in the same document units the left end was.
            take VizRuleX from VizPlotRight giving VizRuleWidth
            gosub to VizLabelCheck
            if VizIsLabel is 1
            begin
                multiply VizLabelLen by 8 giving VizLabelSpace
                add VizGlyphEm to VizLabelSpace
                multiply VizLabelSpace by VizPlotW
                divide VizLabelSpace by VizWindowW
                take VizLabelSpace from VizPlotRight giving VizLabelSpace
                take VizRuleX from VizLabelSpace giving VizRuleWidth
            end
            if VizRuleWidth is less than 1 put 1 into VizRuleWidth
            if VizOutside is 0 put VizRulesD cat `M` cat VizRuleX cat ` ` cat VizRuleY cat `h` cat VizRuleWidth into VizRulesD
        end
        add 1 to VizRuleLine
    end
!! @hash 516c5bc2
!! @verified 2b7d71cf
!!!
!! The source is anchored to the left edge of the pane, and it is the `viewBox`'s `x` that says so.
!!
!! The window's `y` moves over the document as the lines move and its height is the vertical zoom, but its `x` is always zero: what the recording does cannot drag the source sideways. The left-hand end of a line is the part that says what the line is — the indentation and the first word — so losing sight of it is losing the reading, and the window's `x` used to be derived from the horizontal window, which is exactly what took it away whenever the run was panned along. The price is the tail of a line wider than the frame, which is not reachable on screen at all now: the frame shows about a hundred and ten columns at the legible floor, so most lines fit and a few do not.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **And the drawing is over, which the panel is told here for the same reason it was told at the start.** A clip's finish has to reach the panel as a *gesture's* finish, and `VizBusyDone` — which knows what a drawing cost — is only reached from `VizDrawRun`. **The measurement goes and the words stay here**: the panel composes what it says, and the corner readout composes the same number for its own line (`VizBusyCost`). Before the pending request below, deliberately: the re-entry is a *new* drawing and tells the panel so again, so a cost sent after it would disarm the drawing that has just started.
    ! ---- the window on the source's picture ----
    ! The document itself is built once, in `VizSourceMeasure`, and only when the source changes: it
    ! holds *every* line of the file, so the rows line up with the axis and a line the run never
    ! reached still has its row. What is rebuilt on a redraw is the window alone — the pane's `viewBox`
    ! and the `href` naming the document.
    gosub to VizSourcePicture
    ! And the labels, which are rebuilt on every draw because they follow the window.
    ! And the scrollbars, which are the same window read a second way.
    gosub to VizBars
    ! And the clip bar, which is the recording read a second way: the two markers stand where the range
    ! is, and at the recording's ends when there is none.
    gosub to VizClipBar

    set attribute `d` of VizHeat0 to VizHeatD
    set attribute `d` of VizHeat1 to VizHeatE
    set attribute `d` of VizHeat2 to VizHeatF
    set attribute `d` of VizHeat3 to VizHeatG
    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizHeat0 to VizDotSize
    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizHeat1 to VizDotSize
    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizHeat2 to VizDotSize
    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizHeat3 to VizDotSize
    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat0 to `round`
    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat1 to `round`
    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat2 to `round`
    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat3 to `round`
    set attribute `d` of VizFlowCall to VizFlowCallD
    set attribute `d` of VizFlowJump to VizFlowJumpD
    set attribute `d` of VizFlowReturn to VizFlowReturnD
    set attribute `d` of VizRules to VizRulesD
    subtract DrawStarted from now giving DrawMillis
    put 0 into VizDrawing
    ! **The cost, as the measurement the pane took.** What the panel *says* is the panel's to compose, so
    ! this sends milliseconds and the corner readout keeps the sentence (`VizBusyCost`). The panel is the
    ! one that knows whether a reader is looking at it, and a drawing too quick to mention ends the same
    ! way as one nobody was waiting for.
    put `{}` into VizAlertMsg
    set property `kind` of VizAlertMsg to `alert`
    set property `what` of VizAlertMsg to `cost`
    set property `millis` of VizAlertMsg to DrawMillis
    send VizAlertMsg to parent ! §1
    ! Whatever asked while this was running. Once, not in a loop: a request made *during* the draw
    ! below is served by that draw's own end, and that is what makes a chain of them settle.
    if VizPending is 1
    begin
        put 0 into VizPending
        gosub to Draw
    end
    return
!! @hash aa30c655
!! @verified cd84104b
!!!
!! The gestures are four entry points, and this module registers them.
!!
!! `VizGrab` on pick, `VizPan` on drag, `VizWheel` on wheel and `VizReset` when a run is opened afresh. They live here rather than with the editor because the window is the view's own state and the events arrive many times a second — the handlers are registered at the top of this file, on the pane, and never travel as messages.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 The host registers three events and calls in here: `VizGrab` on pick, `VizPan` on drag, and `VizWheel` on wheel. They live with the view rather than with the host because they are the view's own state; a host that wants the picture to move has only to wire them up. There are four entry points in all — those three and `VizReset` — and that is the whole of the interface.
!!
!! §2 Graham's scheme, from Kdenlive and Audacity: the wheel scrolls, shift-wheel zooms the lines, control-wheel the steps, and a drag pans. **The drag is the way the run is moved**, and the one gesture that asks for no modifier — Graham's own correction, having found the modifier pair for the scroll-across unnatural to reach for. That pair is *parked*, see `VizWheel`. The modifier therefore decides which axis a zoom applies to and the direction does not, which is why the wheel handler asks for `the wheel shift` rather than reading the sign of the amount.
!!
!! §3 Which gesture a notch is, is the modifiers' business, not the wheel's. Shift zooms the lines, control zooms the steps, and with neither the wheel scrolls down the run, which is what it does in every other application. Control is the horizontal modifier here against the usual pull of the browser's own page zoom, which this takes over: the listener is registered on the pane and calls `preventDefault`, so the page never sees it, and the pane is where the gesture is being aimed.
!!
!! §4 **Both modifiers together used to scroll the steps, and that is parked.** It was a normalization rather than a fourth gesture — two zooms at once is what the plain arithmetic gave, and the pair was taken out here instead so the one test below still sorted a notch into a scroll or a zoom. It worked, and Graham did not want it: *"I've placed over-emphasis on the use of modifier keys … for panning I found myself wanting to mouse click/drag as this seems to be the natural instinct."* The drag does that job, so the binding is gone and the machinery is left standing: `VizScrollAcross` and `VizScroll`'s second axis are still here, and **restoring the gesture is the three lines that set the flags** — clear the two zooms and put 1 into `VizScrollAcross`. Nothing else refers to them.
!!
!! §5 **The gesture is never what waits.** This used to return at once when a draw was running, so a slow draw swallowed every notch meanwhile — the reader had to repeat themselves, and at a deep zoom on a big recording that is what "the wheel has stopped working" feels like. It now always applies, and `VizRequest` is what remembers that a draw is owed.
!!
!! §6 Where the window stands when the drag begins, and **which control the drag is holding**: the picture itself (0), the lines bar (1) or the steps bar (2). It is decided here because the move cannot tell where the press was, and once only because a drag that changed its mind half way would be two gestures in one.
!!
!! §7 The window is kept rather than accumulated: a pan measured step by step from the last event drifts, because the runtime's division truncates and every step throws its remainder away. Measured from the grab instead, the picture is where the pointer says it is, however many events there have been.
!!
!! §8 **A press on a control acts at once, and nothing else happens.** That is the settled interaction: a drag only selects and a press is what commits, so the two are different gestures with different consequences rather than one gesture with a side effect. `VizGrabbed` stays 0, so no drag follows a control press and the drop that ends it finds nothing to release.
!!
!! §9 5 is the window, 6 is the outside, 7 is the whole recording — and the first two are the same call, `VizCut`, with `VizKeepTail` saying which. That is what keeps three controls from being three answers to one question.
!!
!! §10 **A drag commits nothing, and that is the settled interaction.** It used to apply the clip on the drop, which made the gesture that *selects* also the gesture that *acts* — so a range could not be adjusted without paying for a cut, and a reader who had finished choosing could not change their mind for free. The controls above the bar commit now; the markers are the reader's to move, and moving them costs no drawing at all.
!!
!! §11 Move the window so the picture follows the pointer. Two scales sit between a pointer and a window: the panel's pixels against the drawing's viewBox units, which differ because the browser fits the drawing to the panel; and those units against the window, which differ because the window is a slice of the run. Both are divisions, and the runtime truncates, so each is multiplied out first — the same scaled-integer rule the marks use.
!!
!! §12 How long a viewBox unit is, in *thousandths of a pixel of the panel*. The drawing keeps its own 1000x700 frame and the browser fits it to the panel with `meet`, so the scale is the smaller of the two ratios. The thousandths are not decoration: the ratio is well under one, the runtime's division truncates, and without them it would come out as zero and a drag would move nothing.
!!
!! §13 The panel is read from `VizHost` rather than from the canvas, and that is a limitation worth naming: the canvas is an `svg`, and `svg` is the one element type the plugin registers without the `dom` extra, so `the width of` will not compile for it. The host's box is the canvas's box in practice, since the canvas is created to fill it. **And the canvas is 740 tall, not 700.** The constant here was written for the canvas the view started with; the extra forty units went in with the flow key and this line did not follow, so every drag and every wheel moved the window 5.7% short of what the pointer did.
!!
!! §14 `VizPixelsW` and `VizPixelsH` are the panel's own two sides and are left *untouched*, because the scrollbar's hit test needs them to work out how much of the panel the fitted canvas does not fill; the height scaled to a thousand-wide canvas is a value in its own right and gets its own name, which is a thing worth being strict about — the first version of the hit test read the scaled number as though it were the height and put every hit 350 pixels out. See `VizHandleAt`.
!!
!! §15 **The two bars, drawn from the window, and why the picture needs them.** Past a hundred per cent a reader can see *where* they are — the axis says which lines are on screen — but not *how much* of the run is on screen, and those are different questions. The vertical bar is the lines axis, the horizontal the steps axis; each handle is the window's share of its axis, its length the fraction in view — which is why the fit fills the bar — and its offset how far into the run the window has gone.
!!
!! §16 That offset is scaled to the part of the bar the handle can *move* along rather than to the whole bar, and the difference is the one thing worth stating: a handle of half the bar can travel half the bar, so the ends of the run put it at the ends of the bar instead of three quarters of the way along. Every scrollbar does this, and the reason is plain — the handle is a picture of the window, and the window can be at an end of the run.
!!
!! §17 The lines bar needs no minimum: the legible floor holds the window to a tenth of even a short file, so the handle cannot vanish. The steps axis can be zoomed to four steps out of thousands, and a handle a quarter of a unit long is neither visible nor hittable, so ten units — the bar's own thickness — is the floor. Where the true fraction is below it the handle is very slightly longer than the truth it reports, which is what a real scrollbar does too.
!!
!! §18 One bar, from five numbers about its axis: the bar's length, the size of the whole thing, the size of the part in view, where that part starts, and where the whole thing starts. Two axes and one rule, which is the whole point of writing it down once rather than twice.
!!
!! §19 reads VizBarLen, VizBarExtent, VizBarSpan, VizBarStart, VizBarOrigin leaves VizSlideSize, VizSlidePos, VizSlideTravel, VizSlideRoom
!!
!! §20 **Which control the pointer went down on — and why this is a hit test rather than a handler.** The SVG plugin's shapes are not browser elements and the runtime's `on pick` accepts only those, so a `rect` cannot be given a handler of its own: the pane's pick is the only one there is, and it has to work out where it landed. The answer has to be in the *drawing's* units — the units the handles are drawn in — and the pane's own box is not that, for the reason below.
!!
!! §21 The two boxes sit in different margins, so they cannot overlap and the order of the tests cannot matter. Each is the drawn handle grown by four units: ten units is a thin thing to grab with a mouse, and every scrollbar a reader has used lets them miss it slightly. The *troughs* are deliberately not hit — a press there is a pan of the picture, which is the gesture the pane already had, and a jump-to-page would be a second way to move that dragging back cannot undo.
!!
!! §22 So: from the pane's corner, less the letterbox, at the fitted scale — and that conversion is `VizPointerUnits`, shared with `VizHover` rather than written twice. The letterbox is what the pan does not care about and a hit test does: the browser fits the canvas inside the pane and centres the difference, so the canvas's top-left corner is *not* the pane's, and every unit of that offset is a unit a hit test would be wrong by. It is half of what the canvas leaves over on each side, which the pane's two sides and the scale between them give.
!!
!! §23 **The clip bar's two markers, in the strip above the plot.** A band of its own, so this test cannot be confused with anything else on the canvas — the plot begins at 60 and nothing else is drawn above it — and each marker is the drawn one grown by four units, the same forgiveness the handles get. The band between them is deliberately *not* hit: a press there is a pan of the picture, which is the gesture the strip would otherwise take away.
!!
!! §24 **The pointer, in the drawing's own units, and the one place that conversion is written.** Two scales stand between a pointer and the picture: the pane's pixels against the canvas's viewBox units, and the letterbox the browser leaves when it fits a 1000x740 canvas into a panel of another shape and centres the difference. A hit test that ignored the letterbox is one this file has already had — and a *second* copy of the arithmetic is how it would come back, so the press and the hover both come through here.
!!
!! §25 reads `VizRawX`/`VizRawY` (client pixels), leaves `VizPtrX`/`VizPtrY` (viewBox units).
!!
!! §26 **Which of the three cut controls the pointer is over, if any.** 1 is the window, 2 the outside, 3 the reset, 0 none — and it is the *control*, not the code a press puts in `VizSlide`, because the hover has no such code and the two must not be able to disagree about which box is which.
!!
!! §27 Each control is its drawn box exactly: a control is small and its box is a number, so there is no forgiveness to add and nothing to guess at — which is the whole reason the box is a variable rather than a constant written twice.
!!
!! §28 reads `VizPtrX`/`VizPtrY`, leaves `VizCtrlHit`.
!!
!! §29 **What a control says before it is pressed, and the one gesture here that must never draw.** `><` and `<>` are two arrow pairs with no key, and the notice line only ever names what the *last* cut kept; the tooltip is the reader's half of the controls, and `on hover` was added to the language for exactly this.
!!
!! §30 A hover arrives at pointer rate, so this does nothing but attribute writes — the same discipline the drag's guide-and-wash feedback follows — and it goes further by saying nothing at all when the answer has not changed, because a tip written back in the same words is a layout the browser is asked to redo dozens of times a second. `VizHoverShown` is that memo, and it is also what the handler consults to know whether anything is on screen.
!!
!! §31 **The pointer leaving arrives as a hover too**, with the coordinates it crossed the boundary at: `mouseleave` is what carries the words away again, and the arithmetic finds nothing under a point outside the pane, which is why there is no separate "leave" here.
!!
!! §32 **`is not 1`, and not the `is 0` this guard was written as, because a declared variable with nothing in it is not zero.** The pane is on screen from the moment the editor's message arrives and is not *built* until the drawing's own build pass has run — and the first draw of a run announces itself and waits (`VizBusyNotice`) before that happens, so a pointer already over the picture has a real window in which to arrive here. Read as `is 0` the guard never fired: `VizTipDraw` then wrote `VizTip`, an element that did not exist yet, and threw on every pointer move. `VizTipHide` carries the same guard with the same hole; that one is reported rather than changed, because nothing here depends on it.
!!
!! §33 **The line under the pointer, which every sidebar tab is a view of** — reported here rather than on a press alone, which is what this gesture exists for: the reader moves the pointer over the script and the panel says what that part of it was for. It is two comparisons and a message when the line changes, and nothing at all while the pointer stays inside one row.
!!
!! §34 It comes *before* the tip's memo below, and that is a real order rather than a style: the memo returns as soon as the control under the pointer is the one already described, so the row would only ever be reported on the events that happened to change the tip.
!!
!! §35 **The words, and where they go.** The tip is right-aligned with the control it belongs to, because all three of them sit at the frame's right end, and it sits on the strip below the controls — the only row between them and the bar, which it covers while the pointer is up and uncovers when it leaves.
!!
!! §36 The width is a *count*, not a measurement: eleven-unit glyphs at about six units each, plus the padding the box wants at each end. The pane cannot measure text, and a box a little too wide reads as padding where one too narrow would clip the sentence.
!!
!! §37 reads `VizHoverShown`; writes the tip's words, its box and both their places. `VizHoverShown` 0 is the resting state, which is an empty box rather than a hidden one.
!!
!! §38 **Which arrival the pointer is *in effect* at — and why this is a walk rather than a hit box.** Every mark of a kind is one path, which is the same constraint that makes a layer cost string building rather than DOM nodes, so there is nothing for the DOM to hit and the answer has to be arithmetic — exactly as it is for the bars above. The walk is over the recording's *arrivals* rather than over what was drawn.
!!
!! §39 **The reading follows the pointer's *block*, not the row it is on, and that is the whole of why this is a band.** A visit is a few steps wide — a median of seven on a real recording, which at the fit is about twenty units of an eight-hundred-unit pane — so an arrival's own line is too narrow a thing to point at anywhere but the closest zoom. Its **block** is not: `scope` on the arrival gives the block it heads, `[first, last]`, and a row is always at least a row tall. So the walk takes every arrival whose band contains the pointed line, and for each takes its last occurrence at or before the pointer. **The values are the union of those**, so an inner block's sit on top of its caller's and each drops away when the pointer leaves its own band — which is what a period of interest means, and deliberately not variable scoping. The nearest-dot rule this replaced made a reader hunt for the dot to see anything, and it left the values blank everywhere except one row; the in-effect rule that followed it still could not be read at a zoom where a visit is thin.
!!
!! §40 The comparison is in the picture's own units, which is what `VizPlaceX` produces. **Reads `VizPtrX`, the pointer converted into those units** — `VizHandleAt` has already done that work for a press and `VizPointerUnits` for a hover, and converting a second time would be a second copy of the letterbox arithmetic, which is the one thing here that has already been got wrong once. The mapping is *called* rather than written out again, for the reason it exists: a mark and a rule that disagreed about where a line is was a real fault, found by measurement.
!!
!! §41 **An arrival with no band falls back to its own line, and that is one code path rather than two.** `scope` is absent on an arrival that heads no block — one in the linear flow — and from every recording made before the field existed; treating its band as `[line, line]` makes the test the same one test, and old recordings then behave exactly as they did. An arrival whose band misses the frame is skipped by the same rule the draw uses: it is not in the picture, so the pointer cannot be inside it.
!!
!! §42 **The visit number is counted per *anchor*, not per row, and the total is read back at the end.** A band spans several rows, so counting arrivals a row at a time would misnumber `visit V of T` for every row but the head. `pc` is the anchor's identity in the trace, so the counts go in a dict keyed by it; and because a name's total is only known once the walk has finished, the innermost band's anchor is remembered and its count read out after the loop rather than during it.
!!
!! §43 **The pane reports and does not decide.** Which doc block holds a line is the editor's model and the sidebar's to show, so one small dict goes back and nothing here changes on screen — the panel beside the picture *is* the feedback. And it now travels on a *move* as well as on a press, which is the change this feature is: the values have to follow the pointer for a reader to see them change, and the report is memoised on the arrival so a move that crosses no arrival sends nothing at all.
!!
!! §44 **A press on empty picture names the arrival in effect there, and a memo on the arrival is what keeps that from being work.** It used to name nothing, on the reasoning that a pan beginning on empty picture must not clear the panel on its way past. That reasoning is spent: the hover reports the line and the arrival as the pointer moves, so the panel is *already* showing the point the pan begins at, and a press that named nothing would be the one gesture with no visible effect. What survives is the property the old rule was protecting — the panel does not change across a press — and `plotview-check` asserts that directly instead of asserting the old proxy.
!!
!! §45 **And said here, where both numbers are in hand, rather than sent to be said elsewhere.** The wording is the panel's own — the subject first, then the figures, in plain words rather than symbols, because what a reader copies out of a status line should still read as a sentence. The total can be missing (a report from a pane that did not count) and "visit 3 of 0" would be worse than saying less. Said by `VizFootSay`, which both the press and the hover go through, so the pane's foot and the sidebar's heading cannot word the same arrival differently.

!!
!! §46 **Holding a handle, which is not a pan.** Holding the picture moves the window one unit per unit of pointer travel, so the drawing stays under the finger. Holding a *handle* moves the whole run past the window instead, so the handle follows the pointer — the two differ by the ratio of the run to the window, which is exactly what the handle's length already says. That ratio is why the familiar mapping is the right one rather than decoration: a drag that moved one line per line of handle would take a hundred drags to cross a hundred-line file.
!!
!! §47 The travel is read from what is *drawn*, so a drag can never disagree with the bar in front of the reader; the window it moves from is the one `VizGrab` kept.
!!
!! §48 The wheel alone scrolls the run, and both modifiers scroll it *across*: either way the step is a quarter of the window on the axis being scrolled rather than a number of lines or steps, so it feels the same at every zoom — a fixed step is a nudge on a long run and a leap on a short one, which is the prototype's lesson in its smallest form. The two axes are one subroutine because the axis is the only thing that differs between them, and `VizScrollAcross` — set by `VizWheel` — is what says which.
!!
!! §49 **The sign is the whole of the direction, and it went missing.** This read the amount but never asked whether it was positive, so every notch walked the same way down the file — Graham's "the page scrolls up whichever way I roll". The zoom always had its sign (`VizZoom` tests it); the scroll did not.
!!
!! §50 A notch in or out, on whichever axes `VizWheel` marked. Two steps rather than one, and they are exact inverses of each other: in shrinks a window by a fifth and out grows it by a quarter, and four fifths times five quarters is one, so a notch in and a notch out land back where they started. A single step could not do that here, because the runtime truncates every division.
!!
!! §51 The two axes are independent because the questions are: the steps are *when* and the lines are *where*, and how far apart two arrivals are in time says nothing about how far apart the lines they land on are. An axis that is not marked is left exactly as it was.
!!
!! §52 The *size* is clamped before the origin is moved to follow it, and that order is the whole of this. `VizClamp` has the last word on how small a window may get, so a centring computed from the size before the clamp slides the window every time a notch is refused: at the legible floor each notch shortened the window by a fifth, the clamp put it back, and the origin kept the centring's share of a change that never happened — which is "the wheel scrolls up one line".
!!
!! §53 Ask for a draw without ever losing the asking. Everything that wants the picture rebuilt comes through here — a gesture that moved the window, a new run handed over by the editor — so a request arriving while a draw is running is remembered rather than dropped, and `Draw` serves it on the way out. Drawing then reads the window as it stands *at that moment*, so a burst of notches during one draw costs one draw more and lands on the latest of them.
!!
!! §54 Keep the window inside the run, and never empty. The upper clamps are the outer limit of both gestures — the whole picture is as far as either can go — and they double as the way back to it, so the pane needs no reset button. The lower ones matter only on a tiny run: a window of under four units maps to under four pixels, and every division above would then round away. No zoom past the size the text is written at — see `VizGlyphRows`. It is applied *before* the clamp to the run, so a recording shorter than that many lines simply has no vertical zoom in it, which is right: the whole run is already legible and there is nothing to zoom into.
!!
!! §55 Fit the window to the run again on the next draw. The view calls this itself when the recording changes — see the comparison at the fit in `Draw`, which is where "a different run" is decided — and it stays an entry point because a host may have its own reason to start again. It is no longer what a visit does: leaving the pane and coming back keeps the window, which is what the reader wants and what a reset on entry destroyed.
!!
!! §56 Note a line the recording names, once. The rules are drawn for *these* lines and not for every line the run executed, which is what the window's counts carry: on a recording that walks a chain of labels the counts cover more than twice as many lines as carry anything, and a grid that dense stops being a guide. A line qualifies if an arrival landed on it or a transfer began or ended on it — which is exactly the set of lines the picture has something to say about.
!!
!! §57 The set is a padded string and the test is `includes`, because the language has one of those and no set type. The dedupe is not an optimisation: a rule is a line drawn right across the frame, so a line visited two hundred times would contribute two hundred identical rules to the same path attribute — invisible, and a waste that grows with the recording.
!!
!! §58 **A scrollbar's trough pages the view, and it is a *click* that does it rather than a press.** The handle is a share of the run and the trough is everything else in the strip, so a press in a bar is a trough press unless it is on the handle — which is what `VizTroughV`/`VizTroughH` classify at the one place a press is classified. **The trough already had a gesture** — a drag there pans the picture, as a drag anywhere else does — so paging could not take the press without taking the pan away. It takes the *release* instead, and tells a click from a drag by whether the pointer went anywhere in between: a flag set by `VizPan` would swallow every click that wobbled, because a drag event fires for a hand that is not perfectly still. A page is the window's own extent, which is what the trough means elsewhere and what keeps a click and a wheel from being the same amount; a window already at its end is left there by the clamp.
!   ---- the gestures ----
VizWheel: ! §1–§2
    put the wheel amount into VizWheelAmount ! §3–§5
    add 0 to VizWheelAmount
    if VizWheelAmount is 0 return
    put 0 into VizZoomAcross
    put 0 into VizZoomDown
    put 0 into VizScrollAcross
    if the wheel shift is 1 put 1 into VizZoomDown
    if the wheel control is 1 put 1 into VizZoomAcross
    ! **Both modifiers together is ignored, which is not the same as deleting the test.** With no special
    ! case the pair sets both flags and `VizZoom` applies both axes — the accidental two-at-once zoom that
    ! the plain arithmetic gave before this file had any gesture for the pair. Graham asked for the pair to
    ! go, so it goes: a notch with both modifiers down does nothing at all.
    if VizZoomAcross is 1 and VizZoomDown is 1 return
    put VizZoomAcross into VizMove
    add VizZoomDown to VizMove
    if VizMove is 0 gosub to VizScroll
    if VizMove is not 0 gosub to VizZoom
    return

VizGrab:
    put the pick position into VizGrabPos ! §6–§7
    put property `x` of VizGrabPos into VizGrabX
    put property `y` of VizGrabPos into VizGrabY
    ! §58 The drag point starts at the press, so a release that never moved is the click it looks like.
    put VizGrabX into VizDragX
    put VizGrabY into VizDragY
    put VizViewX0 into VizGrabFromX
    put VizViewY0 into VizGrabFromY
    put 0 into VizSlide
    gosub to VizHandleAt
    if VizSlide is 5 put 1 into VizKeepTail ! §8–§9
    if VizSlide is 6 put 2 into VizKeepTail
    if VizSlide is 5 or VizSlide is 6 gosub to VizCut
    if VizSlide is 7 gosub to VizUnclip
    if VizSlide is 5 or VizSlide is 6 or VizSlide is 7 return
    ! A press on the picture is also a *selection*. The bars are tested first because a press on one of
    ! those is a grab rather than a pointing at a mark, which is why this sits behind the test above
    ! rather than beside it — and a press that names no mark leaves the last selection alone.
    if VizSlide is 0 gosub to VizSelect
    put 1 into VizGrabbed
    return

VizRelease:
    put 0 into VizGrabbed ! §10
    ! **A click in a bar's trough pages the view; a drag there still pans.** Told apart by whether the
    ! pointer went anywhere between press and release — a flag set by `VizPan` would swallow every click
    ! that wobbled — with a few units of slack for a hand that is not perfectly still.
    if VizTroughV is 0 and VizTroughH is 0 return ! §58
    ! The drag point is seeded at the press, so a gesture that never moved reads as the click it is
    ! without depending on what `on drop` carries.
    take VizGrabX from VizDragX giving VizPtrA
    if VizPtrA is less than 0 multiply VizPtrA by -1
    if VizPtrA is greater than 4 return
    take VizGrabY from VizDragY giving VizPtrA
    if VizPtrA is less than 0 multiply VizPtrA by -1
    if VizPtrA is greater than 4 return
    gosub to VizPage
    return

VizPage:
    gosub to VizNote ! §58
    ! A page is the window's own extent: a click moves by what is showing, not by a wheel's notch.
    if VizTroughV is 1 take VizViewYH from VizViewY0 giving VizViewY0
    if VizTroughV is 2 add VizViewYH to VizViewY0
    if VizTroughH is 1 take VizViewXW from VizViewX0 giving VizViewX0
    if VizTroughH is 2 add VizViewXW to VizViewX0
    gosub to VizClamp
    gosub to VizRedraw
    return

VizPan:
    if VizGrabbed is 0 return ! §11
    gosub to VizNote
    put the drag position into VizDragPos
    put property `x` of VizDragPos into VizDragX
    put property `y` of VizDragPos into VizDragY
    take VizGrabX from VizDragX giving VizDx
    take VizGrabY from VizDragY giving VizDy
    gosub to VizPixelScale
    multiply VizDx by 1000
    divide VizDx by VizPixelsK
    multiply VizDy by 1000
    divide VizDy by VizPixelsK
    multiply VizDx by VizViewXW
    divide VizDx by VizPlotW
    multiply VizDy by VizViewYH
    divide VizDy by VizPlotH
    take VizDx from VizGrabFromX giving VizViewX0
    take VizDy from VizGrabFromY giving VizViewY0
    gosub to VizClamp
    gosub to VizRedraw
    return

VizPixelScale:
    put the width of VizHost into VizPixelsW ! §12–§14
    put the height of VizHost into VizPixelsH
    multiply VizPixelsH by 1000 giving VizPaneH
    divide VizPaneH by 740
    put VizPaneH into VizPixelsK
    if VizPixelsW is less than VizPixelsK put VizPixelsW into VizPixelsK
    if VizPixelsK is less than 1 put 1 into VizPixelsK
    return

VizBars:
    put VizPlotH into VizBarLen ! §15–§17
    put VizSpanLines into VizBarExtent
    put VizViewYH into VizBarSpan
    put VizViewY0 into VizBarStart
    put VizMinLine into VizBarOrigin
    gosub to VizBarFit
    put VizSlidePos into VizHandleVPos
    put VizSlideSize into VizHandleVSize
    add VizMargin to VizSlidePos
    set attribute `y` of VizHandleV to VizSlidePos
    set attribute `height` of VizHandleV to VizSlideSize

    put VizPlotW into VizBarLen
    ! The steps axis runs from `VizMinSteps` to `VizMaxSteps` — 0 and the run's last step when there
    ! is no clip, the clip's own range when there is one. The origin is what the handle's position is
    ! measured from, so the pair has to move together or a clipped bar would put the handle at the
    ! wrong place along it.
    put VizSpanSteps into VizBarExtent
    put VizViewXW into VizBarSpan
    put VizViewX0 into VizBarStart
    put VizMinSteps into VizBarOrigin
    gosub to VizBarFit
    put VizSlidePos into VizHandleHPos
    put VizSlideSize into VizHandleHSize
    add VizMargin to VizSlidePos
    set attribute `x` of VizHandleH to VizSlidePos
    set attribute `width` of VizHandleH to VizSlideSize
    return

VizBarFit:
    multiply VizBarSpan by VizBarLen giving VizSlideSize ! §18–§19
    divide VizSlideSize by VizBarExtent
    if VizSlideSize is less than 10 put 10 into VizSlideSize
    if VizSlideSize is greater than VizBarLen put VizBarLen into VizSlideSize
    take VizSlideSize from VizBarLen giving VizSlideTravel
    take VizBarSpan from VizBarExtent giving VizSlideRoom
    put 0 into VizSlidePos
    ! At the fit there is no travel and nothing to divide by. That is not a fallback: it is the case
    ! where the handle fills its bar and cannot be moved, so zero is the answer.
    if VizSlideTravel is greater than 0
    begin
        take VizBarOrigin from VizBarStart giving VizSlidePos
        multiply VizSlidePos by VizSlideTravel
        divide VizSlidePos by VizSlideRoom
    end
    return

VizDrag:
    ! Which of the three ways to move a drag is, decided once by the press that began it: a pan of the
    ! picture, or one of the two handles. A symbol rather than a handler body so that the gesture is one
    ! entry point like the others — `VizGrab`, `VizWheel`, `VizReset` — and so a test can drive the real
    ! path instead of the pair it would otherwise have to guess at.
    if VizSlide is 0 gosub to VizPan
    if VizSlide is 1 gosub to VizSlideTo
    if VizSlide is 2 gosub to VizSlideTo
    if VizSlide is 3 gosub to VizClipMove
    if VizSlide is 4 gosub to VizClipMove
    return

VizHandleAt:
    put VizGrabX into VizRawX ! §20–§22
    put VizGrabY into VizRawY
    ! **Which side of a handle a press landed, or on it.** A press in a bar's band is a trough press unless
    ! it is on the handle itself, and the side is what a *click* pages toward — a drag in the same place
    ! still pans, which is the gesture the trough has always had. Both flags are cleared here because this
    ! is the one place a press is classified, so a stale one from the last press cannot survive.
    put 0 into VizTroughV
    put 0 into VizTroughH
    gosub to VizPointerUnits
    if VizPtrX is greater than 940 and VizPtrX is less than 958
    begin
        put 2 into VizTroughV ! below the handle unless the press is above it
        take VizHandleVPos from VizPtrY giving VizPtrA
        if VizPtrA is less than 4 put 1 into VizTroughV
        add 4 to VizPtrA
        if VizPtrA is not less than 0
        begin
            take VizHandleVSize from VizPtrA
            if VizPtrA is less than 5 begin
                put 1 into VizSlide
                put 0 into VizTroughV
            end
        end
    end
    if VizPtrY is greater than 642 and VizPtrY is less than 660
    begin
        put 2 into VizTroughH ! to the right of the handle unless the press is to the left of it
        take VizHandleHPos from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA
        if VizPtrA is less than 4 put 1 into VizTroughH
        add 4 to VizPtrA
        if VizPtrA is not less than 0
        begin
            take VizHandleHSize from VizPtrA
            if VizPtrA is less than 5 begin
                put 2 into VizSlide
                put 0 into VizTroughH
            end
        end
    end
    ! **The controls, tested before the bar's own strip.** They sit *in* that strip, so a press meant for
    ! `keep` must not fall through to a marker standing under it. The test itself is `VizControlAt`,
    ! shared with the hover rather than written twice: a control that answered a press and a hover
    ! differently would be a control whose tooltip named the wrong thing.
    gosub to VizControlAt
    if VizCtrlHit is 1 put 5 into VizSlide
    if VizCtrlHit is 2 put 6 into VizSlide
    if VizCtrlHit is 3 put 7 into VizSlide
    if VizPtrY is greater than 24 and VizPtrY is less than 60 ! §23
    begin
        take VizClipInX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA
        if VizPtrA is greater than -5 and VizPtrA is less than 5
        begin
            put 3 into VizSlide
            put VizClipInX into VizGrabMarker
        end
        take VizClipOutX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA
        if VizPtrA is greater than -5 and VizPtrA is less than 5
        begin
            put 4 into VizSlide
            put VizClipOutX into VizGrabMarker
        end
    end
    return

VizPointerUnits:
    put the left of VizHost into VizPtrX ! §24–§25
    put the top of VizHost into VizPtrY
    gosub to VizPixelScale
    take VizPtrX from VizRawX giving VizPtrX
    take VizPtrY from VizRawY giving VizPtrY
    take VizPixelsK from VizPixelsW giving VizPtrA
    divide VizPtrA by 2
    take VizPtrA from VizPtrX
    multiply VizPixelsK by 740 giving VizPtrA
    divide VizPtrA by 1000
    take VizPtrA from VizPixelsH giving VizPtrA
    divide VizPtrA by 2
    take VizPtrA from VizPtrY
    multiply VizPtrX by 1000
    divide VizPtrX by VizPixelsK
    multiply VizPtrY by 1000
    divide VizPtrY by VizPixelsK
    return

VizControlAt:
    put 0 into VizCtrlHit ! §26–§28
    take VizCtlY from VizPtrY giving VizPtrA
    if VizPtrA is greater than 0 and VizPtrA is less than VizCtlH
    begin
        take VizKeepX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA
        if VizPtrA is greater than 0 and VizPtrA is less than VizKeepW put 1 into VizCtrlHit
        take VizOuterX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA
        if VizPtrA is greater than 0 and VizPtrA is less than VizOuterW put 2 into VizCtrlHit
        take VizResetX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA
        if VizPtrA is greater than 0 and VizPtrA is less than VizResetW put 3 into VizCtrlHit
    end
    return

VizHover:
    if VizShown is 0 return ! §29–§31
    if VizReady is not 1 return ! §32
    put the hover position into VizHoverPos
    put property `x` of VizHoverPos into VizRawX
    put property `y` of VizHoverPos into VizRawY
    gosub to VizPointerUnits
    ! **A frozen pane reports no line, and the pointer coming back over the picture is what unfreezes
    ! it.** The test is before the row is worked out rather than inside `VizRowTell`, because frozen means
    ! the *whole* of the row machinery stands down — and the lift itself is decided in `VizFrozenLook`,
    ! which is the state machine in one place.
    if VizFrozen is 1 gosub to VizFrozenLook
    if VizFrozen is 0
    begin
        gosub to VizRowAt ! §33–§34
        gosub to VizRowTell
    end
    gosub to VizControlAt
    if VizCtrlHit is VizHoverShown return
    put VizCtrlHit into VizHoverShown
    gosub to VizTipDraw
    return

VizTipHide:
    ! Called when the editor says the pane is no longer on screen, so a tip cannot be left standing over
    ! a picture nobody is looking at. Guarded by `VizReady` because a pane can be left before it was ever
    ! drawn, and the elements it would clear do not exist then.
    if VizReady is 0 return
    put 0 into VizHoverShown
    gosub to VizTipDraw
    return

VizTipDraw:
    if VizHoverShown is 0 ! §35–§37
    begin
        put `` into VizTipText
        set the text of VizTip to VizTipText
        set attribute `fill` of VizTipBox to `none`
        return
    end
    put `Keep the window: the part between the markers` into VizTipText
    if VizHoverShown is 2 put `Keep the outside: cut the part between the markers` into VizTipText
    if VizHoverShown is 3 put `Reset: show the whole recording again` into VizTipText
    set the text of VizTip to VizTipText
    put the length of VizTipText into VizTipLen
    multiply VizTipLen by 6 giving VizTipW
    add 12 to VizTipW
    put VizKeepX into VizTipX
    add VizKeepW to VizTipX
    if VizHoverShown is 2 put VizOuterX into VizTipX
    if VizHoverShown is 2 add VizOuterW to VizTipX
    if VizHoverShown is 3 put VizResetX into VizTipX
    if VizHoverShown is 3 add VizResetW to VizTipX
    take VizTipW from VizTipX
    if VizTipX is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizTipX
    set attribute `x` of VizTipBox to VizTipX
    set attribute `y` of VizTipBox to 32
    set attribute `width` of VizTipBox to VizTipW
    set attribute `height` of VizTipBox to 18
    set attribute `fill` of VizTipBox to `#fffbe6`
    put VizTipX into VizPtrA
    add 6 to VizPtrA
    move VizTip to VizPtrA 45
    return

VizArrivalAt:
    put 0 into VizHit ! §38–§42
    put 0 into VizHitVisit
    put 0 into VizHitTotal
    put 0 into VizHitAnchor
    put json of `{}` into VizHitValues
    ! **The walk is over the bands the pointer is inside, not over the one row it names.** A visit is a
    ! few steps wide and a row is always a row, so an arrival's own line is too narrow a thing to point
    ! at at any zoom but the closest; its *block* is what a reader can keep the pointer in.
    put json of `{}` into VizEffSeen
    put 0 into VizEffDepth
    put 0 into VizHitI
    while VizEvents has element VizHitI
    begin
        put element VizHitI of VizEvents into VizEvent
        if property `cat` of VizEvent is `anchor`
        begin
            put property `args` of VizEvent into VizArgs
            put property `line` of VizArgs into VizLine
            add 0 to VizLine
            ! The block this arrival heads — or its own line when it heads none, which is what an
            ! arrival in the linear flow is, and what every recording made before `scope` existed
            ! gives. Either way the test below is one test.
            put property `scope` of VizArgs into VizEffBand
            if VizEffBand is empty
            begin
                put VizLine into VizEffFirst
                put VizLine into VizEffLast
            end
            else
            begin
                put element 0 of VizEffBand into VizEffFirst
                put element 1 of VizEffBand into VizEffLast
                add 0 to VizEffFirst
                add 0 to VizEffLast
            end
            if VizEffFirst is not greater than VizRowLine and VizEffLast is not less than VizRowLine
            begin
                ! This arrival's number on its *anchor*, which is the count including it: the first
                ! arrival at a block is visit 1. Counted per anchor and not per row, because a band
                ! covers several rows and a row count would misnumber the visit the reader is shown.
                put property `pc` of VizArgs into VizEffPc
                put property VizEffPc of VizEffSeen into VizEffCount
                if VizEffCount is not greater than 0 put 0 into VizEffCount
                add 1 to VizEffCount
                set property VizEffPc of VizEffSeen to VizEffCount
                put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps
                add 0 to VizSteps
                put VizSteps into VizTickValue
                gosub to VizPlaceX
                put VizTickPos into VizHitX
                ! Outside the frame is outside the picture, and the margin is where the frame begins.
                if VizHitX is not less than VizMargin and VizHitX is not greater than VizPlotRight
                begin
                    if VizHitX is not greater than VizPtrX
                    begin
                        ! **Additive, and a later arrival wins a name.** Every band the pointer is
                        ! inside contributes what it was watching at this moment, so an inner block's
                        ! values sit on top of its caller's and each drops away when the pointer
                        ! leaves its own band — which is what a period of interest means here, and
                        ! is deliberately not variable scoping.
                        put property `values` of VizArgs into VizEffValues
                        if VizEffValues is not empty
                        begin
                            put the json keys of VizEffValues into VizValueKeys
                            put 0 into VizValueK
                            while VizValueK is less than the json count of VizValueKeys
                            begin
                                put element VizValueK of VizValueKeys into VizValueName
                                put property VizValueName of VizEffValues into VizValueText
                                set property VizValueName of VizHitValues to VizValueText
                                add 1 to VizValueK
                            end
                        end
                        ! And the *innermost* band names the reading: the deepest head wins, and the
                        ! last arrival of it at or before the pointer is the one it is showing.
                        if VizEffFirst is not less than VizEffDepth
                        begin
                            put VizEffFirst into VizEffDepth
                            put VizLine into VizHit
                            put VizEffCount into VizHitVisit
                            put VizEffPc into VizHitAnchor
                        end
                    end
                end
            end
        end
        add 1 to VizHitI
    end
    ! The anchor's own total, read out of the table the walk filled. It is the whole of what "visit 3 of
    ! 40" needs, and it is only known once every arrival has been counted — which is why the number the
    ! innermost band's arrival was given during the walk is not the one printed.
    if VizHit is not 0
    begin
        put property VizHitAnchor of VizEffSeen into VizHitTotal
    end
    return

VizFootSay:
    ! The pane's own report, on the line under its status line. Written only here, so the figures a reader
    ! copies off the foot are the figures the message carried — the same two numbers, said once.
    if VizHit is 0 ! §45
    begin
        set the text of VizHitStatus to ``
        return
    end
    put `line ` cat VizHit cat `   visit ` cat VizHitVisit into VizHitText
    if VizHitTotal is greater than 0 put VizHitText cat ` of ` cat VizHitTotal into VizHitText
    set the text of VizHitStatus to VizHitText
    return

VizSelect:
    ! **The row first, because the arrival is on it.** A press is a place on the picture like a hover is,
    ! and the two have to name the same arrival or a press would move the panel to a dot the reader was
    ! not pointing at — which is why this is `VizRowAt` and `VizArrivalAt` and not a hit test of its own.
    ! `VizHandleAt` has already put the pointer in the picture's units by the time this runs.
    gosub to VizRowAt ! §43–§44
    gosub to VizArrivalAt
    if VizHit is 0 return
    gosub to VizFootSay
    put `{}` into VizMark
    set property `kind` of VizMark to `mark`
    set property `line` of VizMark to VizHit
    set property `visit` of VizMark to VizHitVisit
    set property `total` of VizMark to VizHitTotal
    ! Only when the script said something worth watching: an empty dict would tell the sidebar to print
    ! nothing, which is the same as saying nothing at all, and one message shape is easier to read.
    if VizHitValues is not empty set property `values` of VizMark to VizHitValues
    send VizMark to parent
    return


VizSlideTo:
    if VizGrabbed is 0 return ! §46–§47
    if VizSlide is 0 return
    gosub to VizNote
    put the drag position into VizDragPos
    put property `x` of VizDragPos into VizDragX
    put property `y` of VizDragPos into VizDragY
    if VizSlide is 1
    begin
        take VizGrabY from VizDragY giving VizDy
        take VizHandleVSize from VizPlotH giving VizSlideTravel
        take VizViewYH from VizSpanLines giving VizSlideRoom
    end
    if VizSlide is 2
    begin
        take VizGrabX from VizDragX giving VizDy
        take VizHandleHSize from VizPlotW giving VizSlideTravel
        take VizViewXW from VizSpanSteps giving VizSlideRoom
    end
    ! At the fit the handle fills its bar: no travel, no room, nothing to move. This is what makes a
    ! hundred per cent immovable rather than merely hard to move.
    if VizSlideTravel is less than 1 return
    if VizSlideRoom is less than 1 return
    gosub to VizPixelScale
    multiply VizDy by 1000
    divide VizDy by VizPixelsK
    multiply VizDy by VizSlideRoom
    divide VizDy by VizSlideTravel
    if VizSlide is 1
    begin
        put VizGrabFromY into VizViewY0
        add VizDy to VizViewY0
    end
    if VizSlide is 2
    begin
        put VizGrabFromX into VizViewX0
        add VizDy to VizViewX0
    end
    gosub to VizClamp
    gosub to VizRedraw
    return

VizScroll:
    gosub to VizNote ! §48–§49
    divide VizViewYH by 4 giving VizMove
    if VizScrollAcross is 1 divide VizViewXW by 4 giving VizMove
    if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove
    if VizWheelAmount is less than 0 take VizMove from 0 giving VizMove
    if VizScrollAcross is 1 add VizMove to VizViewX0
    if VizScrollAcross is not 1 add VizMove to VizViewY0
    gosub to VizClamp
    gosub to VizRedraw
    return

VizZoom:
    gosub to VizNote ! §50–§51
    if VizWheelAmount is greater than 0 go to VizZoomOut
    if VizZoomAcross is 1
    begin
        divide VizViewXW by 5 giving VizMove
        if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove
        take VizMove from VizViewXW
    end
    if VizZoomDown is 1
    begin
        divide VizViewYH by 5 giving VizMove
        if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove
        take VizMove from VizViewYH
    end
    go to VizZoomSettled
VizZoomOut:
    if VizZoomAcross is 1
    begin
        divide VizViewXW by 4 giving VizMove
        if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove
        add VizMove to VizViewXW
    end
    if VizZoomDown is 1
    begin
        divide VizViewYH by 4 giving VizMove
        if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove
        add VizMove to VizViewYH
    end
VizZoomSettled:
    gosub to VizClamp ! §52
    ! Now hold the middle still, using the size the clamp actually allowed.
    take VizViewXW from VizWasXW giving VizMove
    divide VizMove by 2
    add VizMove to VizViewX0
    take VizViewYH from VizWasYH giving VizMove
    divide VizMove by 2
    add VizMove to VizViewY0
    gosub to VizClamp
    gosub to VizRedraw
    return

VizNote:
    ! What the window was before a gesture, so the gesture can tell whether it changed anything. Kept
    ! here rather than re-derived, because `VizClamp` writes the window in place and the old value is
    ! gone by the time the answer is wanted.
    put VizViewX0 into VizWasX0
    put VizViewXW into VizWasXW
    put VizViewY0 into VizWasY0
    put VizViewYH into VizWasYH
    return

VizRequest:
    if VizDrawing is 1 ! §53
    begin
        put 1 into VizPending
        return
    end
    gosub to Draw
    return

VizRedraw:
    ! A draw only if the window actually moved. A refused gesture — a zoom at its limit, a pan against
    ! the edge of the run — would otherwise redraw the whole picture to produce the picture that is
    ! already on screen, which is wasted work and a visible flash on a slow machine.
    put 0 into VizMoved
    if VizViewX0 is not VizWasX0 put 1 into VizMoved
    if VizViewXW is not VizWasXW put 1 into VizMoved
    if VizViewY0 is not VizWasY0 put 1 into VizMoved
    if VizViewYH is not VizWasYH put 1 into VizMoved
    if VizMoved is 1 gosub to VizRequest
    return

VizClamp:
    if VizViewYH is less than VizGlyphRows put VizGlyphRows into VizViewYH ! §54
    ! The steps axis is bounded by the *run* the picture is of, which is `VizMinSteps` to `VizMaxSteps`
    ! — 0 to the last step with no clip, the clip's own range with one. `VizSpanSteps` is that width,
    ! and the lower clamp is where the run now begins, so both gestures stop at the clip's edges
    ! exactly as they stop at the recording's when there is no clip.
    ! **The floor comes first and the run's own width second, and the order is the whole of it.** A window
    ! may not be narrower than four steps (an axis needs something to label) and may not be wider than the
    ! run; capping to the run first let the floor raise a one-step run's window to four, and the pull-back
    ! below then put its left edge at `maxSteps - 4` — a window that starts *before* the recording. Found
    ! on 2026-10-13 by drawing a script with no recording, whose run is a single step: the steps axis read
    ! `-3` to `0`. **Any run shorter than four steps did this**, not only an empty one; the empty picture is
    ! just where it became visible.
    if VizViewXW is less than 4 put 4 into VizViewXW
    if VizViewYH is less than 4 put 4 into VizViewYH
    if VizViewXW is greater than VizSpanSteps put VizSpanSteps into VizViewXW
    if VizViewYH is greater than VizSpanLines put VizSpanLines into VizViewYH
    if VizViewX0 is less than VizMinSteps put VizMinSteps into VizViewX0
    if VizViewY0 is less than VizMinLine put VizMinLine into VizViewY0
    put VizViewX0 into VizViewTemp
    add VizViewXW to VizViewTemp
    if VizViewTemp is greater than VizMaxSteps
    begin
        take VizViewXW from VizMaxSteps giving VizViewX0
    end
    add VizSpanLines to VizMinLine giving VizViewTemp
    put VizViewY0 into VizMove
    add VizViewYH to VizMove
    if VizMove is greater than VizViewTemp
    begin
        take VizViewYH from VizViewTemp giving VizViewY0
    end
    return

VizReset:
    put 0 into VizViewFit ! §55
    return

VizRuleAdd:
    put ` ` cat VizLine cat ` ` into VizRuledLine ! §56–§57
    if VizRuledLines includes VizRuledLine return
    put VizRuledLines cat VizRuledLine into VizRuledLines
    return

VizSourceMeasure:
!! @hash 1a8bfa0b
!! @verified 0f1ac9ba
!!!
!! And a doc block is not drawn in it.
!!
!! Every line of the file keeps its row, and a line belonging to a doc block — the `!!` prose, the `@hash`/`@verified` pair, the `!!!` terminator — is drawn as an empty row. The picture is of the *program*, and on a file written to this convention the prose is longer than the code by a wide margin and buries the flow; a bare `!` comment is a note to the line beside it rather than a block, so it stays. Blanked rather than skipped, because the rows are what the axis, the rules and the marks line up with: the pane's numbering still matches the editor's, and that is what makes the two views comparable at all.
!! **The trap this had to dodge:** numbers read out of the trace stay *text*, and text compares lexically — `9` is not less than `10` — so the range-finding below converts with `add 0 to …` before it compares anything. Without that the picture is silently wrong, which is the failure mode the project's own notes warn about.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 `json split` with no `on` splits on the newline, and it is the one to use: it leaves a JSON array behind, which `element N of` can read. The plain `split` does not — it stores value objects, and `element N of` tries to `JSON.parse` each one and reports "Can't parse JSON".
!!
!! §2 Every row of the file is drawn here, once, at the size the text is written at, and nothing about it changes afterwards: a zoom or a pan moves the *window* rather than redrawing the picture. That is the whole point of the pane, and it is also why the width is measured over every line — the picture's proportions are fixed by its longest line, and the window decides how much of it is in view.
!!
!! §3 **The picture is the whole file, and the recording is a window over it.** Drawing only the lines the run touched made the pane a picture of the run with no way to see it in context, and it left the window unable to travel past the last line that happened to execute — a scroll that stops dead for no visible reason, which is what Graham hit. So the extent here is 1..the file's own line count, every line is drawn, and the marks, the rules and the heat are what say which part ran. That is the more informative picture as well as the more navigable one: a stretch with no marks at all now reads as a stretch that did not run, which is a fact about the program.
!!
!! §4 **Every row of the file is drawn, and each carries its own absolute `y`.** It used to be one `<text>` whose rows were placed by `dy="18"` and accumulated — the cheaper document, and the one that came apart: a `<tspan>` with no content does not advance the text position, so a row that draws nothing collapses and every row below it is drawn too high. See the note where the rows are built; the drift is the number of empty rows above a line, which is why blanking the doc blocks made an invisible fault visible. `y="-4"` on the element keeps the first row's descenders off the document's edge for a reader of the source; the rows themselves name their own place.
!!
!! §5 **The redaction's *measures* are taken here, in the same pass, and the bars themselves are built from them on every zoom.** The two are not the same job. What belongs with the picture built once per run is the reading of the *source* — each row's indentation and each row's length, which is what the walk below already has in its hand. What belongs with the window is where a bar is drawn, because that is the only part that the zoom moves: a bar's left edge is on its line's first character, which scales with the text, and its width is pinned so the redaction is not shrunk into the margin by narrowing the window. Keeping one number a row is what makes rebuilding affordable — reading the file's lines back instead would be a second walk of `VizSourceLines` on every zoom, and `element N of` on a json array re-parses the whole array, which is the cost that made this a once-per-picture job to begin with. **One element per *line number*, the last one included**, because these are indexed by the line's own number rather than by its place in the walk: the arrays are as long as the file's last drawn line, so an index is a line and no arithmetic stands between the two.
!!
!! §6 **A doc block is drawn as an empty row.** The picture is of the *program*, and a doc block is the part of the file that is not the program: on a file written to this convention the prose is longer than the code by a wide margin, and it buries the flow. Everything the convention adds to a file is under `!!` — the prose, the `@hash`/`@verified` pair and the `!!!` terminator — so one test covers the lot. A bare `!` comment is left alone: it is a note to the line beside it rather than a block, and the reader of the picture is reading the code.
!!
!! §7 **Blanked rather than skipped, and that is the whole of the care taken here.** Every row is drawn, and the rows are what the axis, the rules and the marks line up with — so a blanked line keeps its number and the pane's numbering still matches the editor's. Skipping it would renumber the file from its first doc block onwards, which is the one thing this must not do.
!!
!! §8 **This row's measures, and they are taken off the text rather than chosen.** Its indentation is where the line's *first character* is, and its body is what is left of the line from there — in columns, because the bar is built from these at the zoom in force and the document's eight units a column is what turns them into the units the rules use, so the two agree by construction about where a line ends.
!!
!! §9 **The indent is part of the shape, and leaving it out was the fault.** A bar that began at the margin said every line started at column zero: true of `Main:` and false of every line under it, so the silhouette was of a file whose code was flush left. Graham's correction, 2026-10-08 — *"they should show code indentation, instead of starting at the margin they should start on the first character of the script"* — and it is the same argument the blanking already makes: the bars are the code, so they have to have the code's shape. It is also why the left edge cannot be pinned with the width: a bar drawn from a fixed left edge would step in from the text as soon as the window narrowed.
!!
!! §10 A row that draws nothing gets no bar, and gets none rather than a zero-width one: the blanked doc blocks are most of a file written to this convention, and a zero-width rectangle per one of them would be several hundred pieces of path data saying nothing at all. A line of nothing but spaces has no characters either, and takes the same road. Its measures are written as zeroes first, so the row needs no second test when the bars are built.
!!
!! §11 A line the run names but the file does not have is left as an empty row rather than skipped: the rows are what lines up with the axis, so a missing one has to keep its place.
!!
!! §12 Three entities, and the ampersand first — escaping it after the others would escape the `&` of the escapes just written.
!!
!! §13 **Each row is placed at its own absolute `y`, and that is not a detail.** The rows used to be positioned by `dy="18"` alone — the offset accumulating as the text was laid out, so a whole file could be one element with no per-line coordinate computed at all. That reads as the cheaper picture, and it is wrong: a `<tspan>` with **no content does not advance the text position**, so every row that draws nothing collapses and every row below it is drawn that much too high. With fourteen blank lines in a file of six hundred the drift was under a row and invisible; blanking the doc blocks added a hundred and seventy-eight more, and the picture came apart — Graham's *"the whole graph is still misaligned with the text"*, and it was neither: the text was moved, by exactly the number of empty rows above each line.
!!
!! §14 So the saving is gone and the coordinate is computed. It is the one thing a renderer cannot skip: an empty row is still given its `y`, and the row *after* it names its own place and does not care what the empty one did. `14` is where the first row's baseline sits inside its eighteen-unit row — four units down for the descenders, as the element's own `y` used to say, plus the row it is in.
    ! ---- reading the source, and drawing it once ----
    json split VizSource giving VizSourceLines ! §1–§2
    put the json count of VizSourceLines into VizSourceCount
    ! The em of the text, in the document's own units — the size the rows are written at, and therefore
    ! the width of the letters a rule has to clear before it begins. Kept as a value rather than written
    ! into the `<text>` element below as a number, because the rules' arithmetic is a second reader of
    ! it: two spellings of one measurement is how a gap drifts.
    put 13 into VizGlyphEm
    gosub to VizMeasureEm
    put 1 into VizMinLine ! §3
    put VizSourceCount into VizMaxLine
    take VizMinLine from VizMaxLine giving VizSpanLines
    add 1 to VizSpanLines
    put 0 into VizGlyphMax
    put VizMinLine into VizSourceIndex
    while VizSourceIndex is not greater than VizMaxLine
    begin
        take 1 from VizSourceIndex giving VizSourceAt
        if VizSourceAt is less than VizSourceCount
        begin
            put element VizSourceAt of VizSourceLines into VizSourceLine
            if the length of VizSourceLine is greater than VizGlyphMax put the length of VizSourceLine into VizGlyphMax
        end
        add 1 to VizSourceIndex
    end
    if VizGlyphMax is less than 1 put 1 into VizGlyphMax
    multiply VizGlyphMax by 8 giving VizGlyphWidth
    multiply VizSpanLines by 18 giving VizGlyphHeight

    put `<text x="0" y="-4" xml:space="preserve" font-family="monospace" font-size="` cat VizGlyphEmText cat `" fill="#5b6472">` into VizGlyphBody ! §4
    add 1 to VizMaxLine giving VizBarCount ! §5
    set the elements of VizBarIndents to VizBarCount
    set the elements of VizBarBodies to VizBarCount
    ! A pin no window can have, so the first `VizRedactBuild` of this picture always builds.
    put 0 into VizRedactPin
    put VizMinLine into VizSourceIndex
    while VizSourceIndex is not greater than VizMaxLine
    begin
        put `` into VizSourceLine
        take 1 from VizSourceIndex giving VizSourceAt
        if VizSourceAt is less than VizSourceCount
        begin
            put element VizSourceAt of VizSourceLines into VizSourceLine
        end
        ! §6–§7
        if VizSourceLine starts with `!!` put `` into VizSourceLine
        index VizBarIndents to VizSourceIndex ! §8–§10
        put 0 into VizBarIndents
        index VizBarBodies to VizSourceIndex
        put 0 into VizBarBodies
        if VizSourceLine is not empty
        begin
            gosub to VizLineIndent
            put the length of VizSourceLine into VizRedactW
            take VizIndentLen from VizRedactW
            if VizRedactW is greater than 0
            begin
                put VizIndentLen into VizBarIndents
                put VizRedactW into VizBarBodies
            end
        end
        replace `&` with `&amp;` in VizSourceLine ! §11–§12
        replace `<` with `&lt;` in VizSourceLine
        replace `>` with `&gt;` in VizSourceLine
        take VizMinLine from VizSourceIndex giving VizRowY ! §13–§14
        multiply VizRowY by 18
        add 14 to VizRowY
        put VizGlyphBody cat `<tspan x="0" y="` cat VizRowY cat `">` cat VizSourceLine cat `</tspan>` into VizGlyphBody
        add 1 to VizSourceIndex
    end
    put VizGlyphBody cat `</text>` into VizGlyphBody
    ! The bars themselves are built from those measures in `VizRedactBuild`, which the window calls: it is
    ! one `d` attribute, the same economy the marks live under — an AllSpeak element is a declared symbol,
    ! so N bars are one path rather than N elements.

    ! Encoded once, here, because the picture is never redrawn: a data URL has two characters an SVG
    ! document cannot spell for it, and `%` goes first or the `#` replacement would have its own `%`
    ! turned into `%25`. The header written per draw has neither, so re-encoding it is not needed.
    replace `%` with `%25` in VizGlyphBody
    replace `#` with `%23` in VizGlyphBody

    set attribute `width` of VizGlyphs to VizGlyphWidth
    set attribute `height` of VizGlyphs to VizGlyphHeight
    return

VizLabelCheck:
    put 0 into VizIsLabel ! §5–§7
    put 0 into VizLabelLen
    if VizSourceLine is `` return
    if VizSourceLine ends with `:` go to VizLabelShape
    return
VizLabelShape:
    if VizSourceLine starts with ` ` return
    if VizSourceLine starts with `!` return
    put 1 into VizIsLabel
    put the length of VizSourceLine into VizLabelLen
    take 1 from VizLabelLen giving VizLabelLen
    return

VizMeasureEm:
    put `xxxxxxxxx` into VizRulerText ! §8–§12
    put VizRulerText cat VizRulerText into VizRulerText
    put VizRulerText cat VizRulerText into VizRulerText
    put VizRulerText cat VizRulerText into VizRulerText
    put VizRulerText cat VizRulerText into VizRulerText
    put the length of VizRulerText into VizRulerCount
    set the content of VizRuler to VizRulerText
    put the width of VizRuler into VizRulerWidth
    if VizRulerWidth is less than 1 go to VizEmKeep ! §13
    put 1497600 into VizRulerEm
    divide VizRulerEm by VizRulerWidth
    if VizRulerEm is less than 800 go to VizEmKeep
    if VizRulerEm is greater than 3000 go to VizEmKeep
    divide VizRulerEm by 100 giving VizGlyphEm
    multiply VizGlyphEm by 100 giving VizRulerFrac
    take VizRulerFrac from VizRulerEm giving VizRulerFrac
    if VizRulerFrac is less than 10 put `0` cat VizRulerFrac into VizRulerFrac
    put VizGlyphEm cat `.` cat VizRulerFrac into VizGlyphEmText
    return
VizEmKeep:
    put VizGlyphEm into VizGlyphEmText
    return

VizLineIndent:
    put VizSourceLine into VizIndentWork ! §14–§17
    put 0 into VizIndentLen
    while VizIndentWork starts with ` `
    begin
        put from 1 of VizIndentWork into VizIndentWork
        add 1 to VizIndentLen
    end
    return

    ! ---- the bars, at the zoom in force ----
VizRedactBuild: ! §18–§20
    ! ---- the bars, at the zoom in force ----
    ! **The gate is the pin, not the draw.** A pan moves the window without changing its height, so the pin
    ! is what it was and the bars are already right; a zoom changes it and they are built again. The picture
    ! leaves the pin at zero, a value no window can have, so the first draw after a new source always builds.
    if VizRedactPin is VizRedactScale return
    put VizRedactScale into VizRedactPin
    put `` into VizRedactD
    put VizMinLine into VizBarAt
    while VizBarAt is not greater than VizMaxLine
    begin
        index VizBarBodies to VizBarAt
        put VizBarBodies into VizRedactW
        if VizRedactW is greater than 0
        begin
            ! The left edge is the line's own first column, in document units, so the `viewBox` puts it on
            ! the first character at whatever the zoom is — which is the whole of the change: the indent
            ! used to be scaled by the pin like the width, so a deep indent began to the right of the text
            ! it belongs to and the gap grew as the window narrowed.
            index VizBarIndents to VizBarAt
            put VizBarIndents into VizIndentLen
            multiply VizIndentLen by 8 giving VizRedactX
            ! Its top is the row's, and its height is the whole eighteen units, which is what puts the
            ! leading above and below the glyphs inside the bar as well as the glyphs themselves. The height
            ! is *not* pinned: it follows the row, and that is the "zooms with it, but only in Y".
            take VizMinLine from VizBarAt giving VizRedactY
            multiply VizRedactY by 18
            multiply VizRedactW by 8 ! §21
            multiply VizRedactW by VizRedactScale
            divide VizRedactW by 1000
            put VizRedactD cat `M` cat VizRedactX cat ` ` cat VizRedactY cat `h` cat VizRedactW cat `v18h-` cat VizRedactW cat `z` into VizRedactD
        end
        add 1 to VizBarAt
    end
    ! One attribute, which is the same economy the marks live under: an AllSpeak element is a declared
    ! symbol, so N bars are one path rather than N elements.
    set attribute `d` of VizRedact to VizRedactD
    return

VizSourcePicture:
!! @hash f12010c6
!! @verified cf6e1925
!!!
!! The picture never changes; the window over it does.
!!
!! `VizPane` is a nested `<svg>` the size of the frame, and its `viewBox` is the whole of the zoom and the pan — one attribute per draw, and the picture itself is drawn once when the run is opened. That is what keeps the text the shape it was written in: a window whose aspect followed the zoom instead is what stretched the letters as you zoomed in. The pane's size being the frame is also the clipping, which is the one thing a picture cannot do for itself — an `<image>` either scales its content to its own box or is clipped by the outer viewport.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 The pane's `viewBox`, which is the whole of the zoom and the pan. The three values are the draw's own, computed at its top — see `VizWindow` for why they are not worked out here, where they are wanted. In the document's units its aspect is the frame's, which is what gives the text the proportions it was written at (a window whose shape followed the zoom is what stretched the letters), and its height is one row per line in view, so a row lands on the line the axis names.
!!
!! §2 **Its `x` is always zero, so the source stays anchored to the left edge of the pane.** A line longer than the window shows goes off the right-hand edge — and there it stays, because the left-hand end of a line is the part that says what the line is. That offset used to be derived from the horizontal window, so panning along the run dragged the source sideways and took the indentation and the first word out of view with it. The tail of a line wider than the frame is what that costs, and the frame is not narrow: it shows about a hundred and ten columns at the legible floor, which is more than most lines and less than a few.
!!
!! §3 **The pin: how much the window has shrunk the document, inverted, in thousandths.** At the legible floor the document is drawn at its own size and the pin is one, which is the reference the bars are measured against — the length of a line *as a reader reads it*. Every narrower window has a larger pin, so the bars keep that length while the text they sit on gets smaller, which is what stops the redaction shrinking away with the zoom. It is never allowed below one: past the floor the text is at its own size and there is nothing left to compensate for.
    ! ---- the window on the picture ----
    set attribute `viewBox` of VizPane to `0 ` cat VizWindowY cat ` ` cat VizWindowW cat ` ` cat VizWindowH ! §1–§2
    multiply VizWindowH by 1000 giving VizRedactScale ! §3
    divide VizRedactScale by VizPlotH
    if VizRedactScale is less than 1000 put 1000 into VizRedactScale
    ! **And the bars, rebuilt from the picture's measures when that pin has moved** — not on every draw,
    ! because a pan does not change the window's height and so does not change the pin: a pan costs nothing
    ! for them, and a zoom costs one string.
    gosub to VizRedactBuild
    set attribute `href` of VizGlyphs to `data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="` cat VizGlyphWidth cat `" height="` cat VizGlyphHeight cat `">` cat VizGlyphBody cat `</svg>`
    return
!! @hash db51fb1b
!! @verified dc572580
!!!
!! The mappings: the one place a line's y and a step's x are worked out.
!!
!! `VizPlaceY` turns a line number into the y of the row it occupies, and `VizPlaceX` turns a step into the x of its column. Every mark, every rule and every axis label goes through one of them, which is why they are here together and why the other code calls them rather than doing the arithmetic: when the axis and the marks disagreed about where a line was, the fault was one of these two and the picture said so at a glance.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 Where a line number lands in the frame. **This is the one place that mapping lives**, because the marks, the rules and the axis labels all have to agree about it — and they did not: the mapping was written out at each site, and every one of them put a line at the *top* of the row of text it names.
!!
!! §2 **A row is eighteen document units tall and its glyphs are centred in it, so the line's own y is half a row lower.** With the mapping at the row's top, every rule and every dot sat just under the text of the line *above* the one it belonged to — which reads as a vertical misalignment and makes a rule that begins one em past its line's code look like it begins in the middle of the line above. Graham found it by eye and measurement confirmed it: of seventeen rules in a screenshot, every one sat between a fifth and a half of a row above its own glyphs.
!!
!! §3 The half row is added *before* the division, which is half of `VizPlotH` and exactly half a row once the divisor is applied — the same scaled-integer pattern the marks use, and no fraction to lose.
!!
!! §4 **`VizPlaceY` read the other way: which line of the file a y in the picture is over.** They are one mapping in two directions, so they sit together — `VizPlaceY` says why there must be one copy of it, and a mark and a rule that disagreed about where a line is is what it cost to learn.
!!
!! §5 **The half row `VizPlaceY` adds is deliberately not subtracted here.** It puts a line's glyphs in the middle of its row; it is smaller than a row, and the truncation below removes it, so pointing at a line's glyphs and pointing at its row's edge name the same line. Subtracting it would be a second opinion about the same number.
!!
!! §6 **0 means "no row", and it is the answer for every part of the pane that is not the script** — the frame the window is drawn in, the two bars above it, the flow key and the status line below, and anywhere outside the pane. A pointer over none of them leaves the *last* line reported standing: that is what lets a reader move the pointer off the picture, onto the sidebar or the bars, and still read the block it filled. `VizRowTell` is where that is decided.
!!
!! §7 **And "the script" means the plot in *both* directions, which is what the first version of this got wrong.** Only `y` was tested, so any `x` in the plot's band of heights named a line: the left margin, the right margin and the vertical scrollbar strip beside it, and a pointer past the canvas altogether. **Measured in chromium against the dev server, 2026-10-12** — the panel changed from block 1 to block 2 as the pointer's `y` moved over the scrollbar strip (x = 98.5% of the pane) and over the left margin (x = 0.5%) alike. It mattered most on the way out: **a pointer leaving the picture for the sidebar carries the coordinates of where it went, so a departure through the right edge re-named a line and the panel jumped** — the one thing the menu's `Freeze` had been covering for. Found by the browser rig the menu work built (`various/menu-measure.js`) and fixed by Graham's call on 2026-10-13, in the stated shape: the `y` test's own pair, applied to `x`.
!!
!! §8 **The shared trigger: one report per arrival the pointer settles on, not one per pointer event.** Every tab in the sidebar is a view of *this* — the doc block and the code are of the line, and the captured values are of the arrival in effect on it — so the report carries the line and, when the row has an arrival in effect at the pointer, that arrival's visit and values. What the reader is shown is the sidebar's business, which is why the pane decides nothing here.
!!
!! §9 **Why the memo and not a watcher.** A hover arrives at pointer rate, and `VizHover` already refuses to write the tip back in the same words for exactly this reason. The same argument settles this: a sweep down a file costs one message a **line** crossed, and a sweep *along* a row one per **arrival** crossed, with nothing sent in between — no queue to manage, because there is no per-event work to queue, and nothing here draws, so no drawing can be interrupted either. **The walk behind the arrival is the one thing on this path, and it is O(kept records) per hover** (`VizArrivalAt` reads `VizEvents` for the pointed row alone); §38's own note used to justify it by the press being rare, which a hover is not. It is one integer pass with no drawing, memoised so nothing downstream repeats, and if it ever reads as the pane's heavy gesture on a big recording the fix is the obvious one: cache the pointed row's arrivals and re-walk only when the row changes — one walk a row crossed, which is exactly what this memo already gates. Not built, because a measurement has to ask for it first.

    ! ---- the mappings ----
VizPlaceY:
    take VizViewY0 from VizTickValue giving VizTickPos ! §1–§3
    multiply VizTickPos by VizPlotH
    add VizRowHalf to VizTickPos
    divide VizTickPos by VizViewYH
    add VizMargin to VizTickPos
    return

VizPlaceX:
    take VizViewX0 from VizTickValue giving VizTickPos
    multiply VizTickPos by VizPlotW
    divide VizTickPos by VizViewXW
    add VizMargin to VizTickPos
    return

VizRowAt:
    put 0 into VizRowLine ! §4–§7
    if VizPtrX is less than VizMargin return
    put VizPlotW into VizRowTmp
    add VizMargin to VizRowTmp
    if VizPtrX is not less than VizRowTmp return
    if VizPtrY is less than VizMargin return
    put VizPlotH into VizRowTmp
    add VizMargin to VizRowTmp
    if VizPtrY is not less than VizRowTmp return
    take VizMargin from VizPtrY giving VizRowTmp
    multiply VizRowTmp by VizViewYH
    divide VizRowTmp by VizPlotH
    add VizViewY0 to VizRowTmp
    ! A y inside the plot but past the last row of the file — the picture is taller than the file when the
    ! window has room to spare — names a line that is not there, and reporting it would show the reader the
    ! block for a line below the script's end.
    if VizRowTmp is less than VizMinLine return
    if VizRowTmp is greater than VizMaxLine return
    put VizRowTmp into VizRowLine
    return

VizRowTell:
    if VizRowLine is 0 return ! §8–§9
    ! **The arrival in effect on this row, so the values a script asked to see follow the pointer.** The
    ! report is memoised on the *arrival* and no longer on the line: moving along a row crosses visits and
    ! each crossing is news, while moving between two of them is not — so a sweep down a file still costs
    ! one message a change rather than one a pointer event.
    gosub to VizArrivalAt
    if VizRowLine is VizRowSent and VizHitVisit is VizRowSentVisit return
    put VizRowLine into VizRowSent
    put VizHitVisit into VizRowSentVisit
    gosub to VizFootSay
    put `{}` into VizRowMsg
    set property `kind` of VizRowMsg to `line`
    set property `line` of VizRowMsg to VizRowLine
    ! The arrival, when the row carries one. A row carrying none is still a *line* worth reporting — a doc
    ! block and a section of code are about the line, and most lines have no mark on them — so the line
    ! goes on its own and the tabs that are about the line still have something to show.
    if VizHit is not 0
    begin
        set property `visit` of VizRowMsg to VizHitVisit
        set property `total` of VizRowMsg to VizHitTotal
        if VizHitValues is not empty set property `values` of VizRowMsg to VizHitValues
    end
    send VizRowMsg to parent
    return
!! @hash 3fd2ba02
!! @verified f74b0721
!!!
!! Holding the picture still while the pointer goes to the sidebar, and letting go on the way back.
!!
!! **What the freeze is for.** A reader points at a line, the sidebar shows the block it belongs to, and then they move the pointer across to *read* that block — and the journey itself changes the answer, because the pointer crosses rows on the way out. The freeze is the journey's protection: while it is set the pane reports nothing at all, so the sidebar keeps the block the reader chose. It is set from the editor's popup menu and cleared here, because the gesture that ends it is a pointer's.
!!
!! **The two halves of the state machine, and the measurement the whole of it rests on.** A `mouseleave` must *not* lift the freeze — the pointer leaving the picture is exactly the journey the freeze exists for — while the first hover back inside it must (Graham's own words: *"on re-entering to the graph the mouseover actions resume"*). So the only question ever asked is "is the pointer over the pane", and it is put to the pane's own box in client pixels against `the hover position`, which is the same pair of numbers. **One box test is enough because a leave's coordinates are already outside the box it left** — measured in chromium, 2026-10-12, on a 400×300 box at (100,50): leaving it to the right reports `x=505` and leaving it downward reports `y=360` — so a departure and a return can never read the same, and no separate "leave" event is needed anywhere in this file.
!!
!! **A pointer inside the box is not by itself a return, and the browser is what showed it: `VizAway` is the second half.** The menu is drawn *over* the picture, so the pointer that presses `Freeze` is inside the pane's box while it is over a box the pane does not own — and the first version, which lifted the freeze on any pointer inside the box, lifted it the moment the pointer moved a pixel. So the freeze lifts only on a hover that finds the pointer inside the box **having been seen outside it**, which is what a journey out to the sidebar and back is. **That is exactly why the row reads `Cancel`**: a reader who froze and never left has no journey to come back from, and `Cancel` is the way back — Graham's own sentence for the row, and this is the case it covers.
!!
!! **The pane tells the editor when it lifts the freeze, and that is the only reason it speaks.** The editor set the freeze and labels the menu's last row from it, and the editor cannot see a pointer; so a lift made here on the pane's own initiative would otherwise leave that row reading `Cancel` over a picture that had already resumed — and a reader who pressed it would be cancelling nothing. `VizFreezeTell` is that correction, and it is the one message in this file that is not about a line.
VizFrozenLook:
    put the left of VizHost into VizFrzX
    put the top of VizHost into VizFrzY
    add the width of VizHost to VizFrzX giving VizFrzX2
    add the height of VizHost to VizFrzY giving VizFrzY2
    ! The box is compared half-open — a coordinate exactly on the right or bottom edge counts as outside —
    ! because a leave is a coordinate *past* the edge rather than on it, and a move that names the very
    ! last pixel of the pane is not a journey anywhere.
    if VizRawX is not less than VizFrzX and VizRawX is less than VizFrzX2 and VizRawY is not less than VizFrzY and VizRawY is less than VizFrzY2 go to VizFrozenBack
    ! Outside it, so the reader is on their way somewhere and the freeze is doing its job. This is the half
    ! that remembers the journey, and it is why a pointer merely moving about *inside* the picture cannot
    ! lift the freeze: nothing below runs until a hover finds it outside first.
    put 1 into VizAway
    return

VizFrozenBack:
    ! Back inside, and whether that is a return or just a pointer that never left is the whole question.
    if VizAway is 0 return
    put 0 into VizAway
    put 0 into VizFrozen
    gosub to VizFreezeTell
    return

VizFreezeTell:
    put `{}` into VizFreezeMsg
    set property `kind` of VizFreezeMsg to `freeze`
    set property `frozen` of VizFreezeMsg to 0
    send VizFreezeMsg to parent
    return
!! @hash c2162976
!! @verified c2162976
!!!
!! Keep the picture to a range of steps, and give the whole recording back.
!!
!! A recording of a big run is slow to draw and, past a certain size, only useful as an overview: the reader wants one region of it, and everything outside that region is what costs the time. The clip is that, after the fact — nothing is rewritten, so the recording stays the run's own evidence and the same file can be read whole again.
!!
!! **The window is the selector.** The two numbers are taken from `VizViewX0` and its width, which are in the trace's own steps — the unit an IN/OUT bar would mark — so the range is chosen with gestures already in use: shift-wheel zooms the lines, control-wheel the steps, and a drag pans. There is nothing else to learn and no second control to find.
!!
!! **Nothing is stored but the range.** The recording is re-read from `VizTrace` on every draw, so `VizUnclip` is two assignments and the whole of the run is back on the next draw. That also means a clip does not survive leaving the pane — the same deal the window itself makes, and the honest one: a recording is a file, and a range over it is a way of looking, not a property of the run.
!!
!! **And the controls are two boxes above the bar.** A pane element cannot take a click — `svg` is the one element type the plugin registers without the `dom` extra — so a button cannot live inside the pane, and the pane's own idiom, an arithmetic hit test on the pointer, is what every one of its gestures already uses. For this job the bar is the better control for a reason that is measured rather than argued: **the zoom is the expensive gesture on a big recording**, each notch a whole drawing, so marking a range costs one drawing where reaching the same range by wheel and pan takes five or six.
!!
!! **A drag only selects; a press is what commits.** The two markers are the reader's, and moving one costs no drawing at all — but letting go applies nothing, which is what lets a range be adjusted as many times as it takes without paying for a cut each time. `<> keep` makes the window between the markers the run, and `reset` gives the whole recording back. Each is a box drawn from the same numbers the hit test matches, so the press lands on the rectangle that was drawn rather than on a coordinate written somewhere else.
!!
!! **The bar's axis is the working set's own extent**, so a cut brings both markers home to the ends of what is now on screen — the picture above them is a new total range, and the bar says where the selection sits within it. The guides and the washes are placed in the *picture's* mapping instead, so they are drawn at any zoom and with a clip on, which is what a reader pointing at the picture is looking with. `VizClip` — the window as the range — remains, and puts the markers on the window's ends so that a range chosen with the wheel shows on the bar too.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **Keep the window** — the host's own way in, and the same operation as the `in` control. The range is the window exactly as it stands: `VizViewX0` is the first step in view and the window's width is how many of them there are, so the last one is one short of their sum, the convention the status line prints. The selection is moved onto it too, so the markers show a range chosen with the wheel and pressing `keep` straight afterwards is a no-op rather than a cut to somewhere else.
!!
!! §2 **`add 0 to` the four numbers before any arithmetic on them.** A step read out of a parsed recording is *text*, and text compares lexically — an unconverted `10` is less than `9` — so the ranges this makes would cut the wrong records: measured in isolation before the filter was written, `10 is less than 9` is 1 without the conversion and 0 with it. This is where the selection, the remembered recording end and the range arithmetic all meet, so it is where the conversion belongs.
!!
!! §3 **The one operation the controls share: rewrite the list to what the reader chose to keep.** `VizKeepTail` says which part of the window that is — 0 the whole recording, 1 the window itself, 2 the two parts outside it — and the selection (`VizSelFrom`/`VizSelTo`) is the window the markers make. Keeping the inside intersects each live range with the window; keeping the outside subtracts it, and a subtraction can split one range into two, which is why the list is a list and why the new one is built in the spare pair before it replaces the live one.
!!
!! §4 **Nothing is rewritten but the list.** The recording is re-read on every draw, so a cut is a handful of numbers and the next draw's filter, and `reset` is the empty list. That also means a cut does not survive leaving the pane — the same deal the window itself makes, and the honest one: a recording is a file, and a set of ranges over it is a way of looking, not a property of the run.
!!
!! §5 **A cut that would keep nothing is refused, and the reader is told why.** Subtracting a selection from a list it already covers leaves nothing, and an empty list means "the whole recording" — so the pane would *widen* at a press, which is the one thing this control must never do. The history entry the cut had already saved goes back, and the notice says what happened rather than leaving a reader to work out which button was ignored. It is the notice and not the status line for the reason the design gives: an explanation of a control belongs where the controls are.
!!
!! §6 The whole recording, which is the empty list — both the starting state and what `reset` restores.
!!
!! §7 **It bumps the tag, because the kept set has to be rebuilt and the picture re-fitted.** What it does *not* do is ask for a cut's re-fit: it sets `VizKeepSet` to 0, and the fit reads that to tell the two apart — a cut re-fits the steps and leaves the lines, while giving the whole recording back fits both, which is what `reset` means. Without the bump a reset left the picture clipped: the filter's cache key did not change, so the kept set was never rebuilt (found by the harness, 2026-10-07).
!!
!! §8 One range into the list being built. Ranges go in in order — a live list is ordered and the two halves of a split are added left first — so the built list is ordered too, which is what lets the axis take its ends from the first and last entries and lets the filter's walk stay a walk.
!!
!! §9 **`VizKeepCap` is a ceiling and not a promise.** At it, a cut that would split a range further simply keeps less of it than it should — sixteen outer cuts, on one recording, without resetting. Said rather than hidden: an overrun here would be a wrong picture with no sign that anything was dropped.
!!
!! §10 **Keep the records the list covers, and count what was kept and seen.** Called from `Draw` before anything reads the events, so what it leaves in `VizEvents` is what the whole draw — the extent, the rule set, the busiest line over the ramp and the hit test's `visit N of T` — is built from. That is what makes the notice a fact rather than a label.
!!
!! §11 **An empty list means no narrowing**, which is what makes `reset` a state rather than an operation: there is nothing to remember about the whole recording, and every record is kept.
!!
!! §12 **`add 0 to` before a comparison is not tidiness.** A number that arrived as text compares *lexically*, so an unconverted `10` is less than `9` and the filter would keep the wrong records — measured in isolation before this was written: `10 is less than 9` is 1 without the conversion and 0 with it. A step comes out of a parsed recording, so it is text until something makes it a number.
!!
!! §13 Only `anchor` and `transfer` records are counted or kept. They are the two kinds the picture is made of; the metadata entries in a Chrome trace name the process and the thread and are read by nothing here. The `window` records are deliberately dropped with the rest: their `steps` is how long a window ran rather than a step anything is *at*, so keeping one would stretch the extent back out past the range the reader chose.
!!
!! §14 **The recording, read once, here and now.** This is the only place it is wanted: the drawing reads the set `VizEvents` holds, and only a cut has to look at everything to decide what survives it. **The set this filter reads: what is on screen for a cut, the recording for the two that widen.** `VizAll` holds the whole recording, read once per filter from the document already parsed; `VizEvents` holds what the last cut left. A cut walking `VizEvents` is proportional to what the reader is looking at, which is the point of Graham's ask of 2026-10-07 — *"each clip is taken from the original data set, so on big sets it's slow even when already clipped"* — and both are walked the same way below, so the per-record step stays one piece of code.
!!
!! §15 **Where the two markers stand, and the band between them.** Read from the clip's ends, or from the recording's ends when there is no clip, so clearing a clip puts them back without a second place knowing how. The band is the range drawn as a shape, which is the reading a reader wants off the bar: not *where* each end is, but how much of the run is in.
!!
!! §16 The markers are drawn centred on the step they name — they are four units wide — and both are clamped to the bar, because the floor `VizClamp` puts under a window can push the window's own range a step outside a very short run. The markers are the **selection**, which is the reader's and not the clip's. It goes home to the ends of the working set whenever the applied range changes — see the axis in pass one — so a cut brings both markers in to the ends of what is now the run, which is what the picture above them has just become.
!!
!! §17 Both are clamped to the bar, because the floor `VizClamp` puts under a window can push the window's own range a step outside a very short run.
!!
!! §18 **Everything the chosen range looks like, from the two markers' places.** One place, called by the draw (so the bar and its guides stand where the clip is) and by the drag (so a reader moving a marker sees them follow without a drawing). Two copies of this would be two answers to "where is the range", and the drag's would be the one nobody checked.
!!
!! §19 reads `VizClipInX`/`VizClipOutX`, the two step positions in the frame's own x.
!!
!! §20 **The markers and the band first, in the bar's own units.** They are the bar's furniture and belong to the axis it is drawn on; the guides and the washes below belong to the picture and are placed with its mapping.
!!
!! §21 **The guides and the washes are placed in the *picture's* mapping, not the bar's.** They say where the selection falls in the plot, so the plot's own step-to-x is the only mapping that can be right — and it is right at any zoom: zoom past an end and that end's guide leaves the frame, which is what a reader expects, rather than the pair standing aside as they used to. `VizPlaceX` is that mapping, the same one the marks and the rules use, so a guide cannot disagree with a line.
!!
!! §22 And the notice, in the recording's own numbers. The counts come from the filter, so they are only known once a cut has been applied — which is why a window being dragged is named without them, in `VizClipMove`. **What the last cut kept is said in words**, because that is the one thing a reader cannot read off the picture: `the window` and `the outside` are different sets of records, and the marks alone do not say which was asked for.
!!
!! §23 **One step, in the frame's own x** — the clip bar's half of the mapping the axis, the marks and the rules all share. The scaled-integer shape is the file's everywhere: the span is multiplied out before it is divided, because the runtime truncates every division and a proportion of a big run rounds to nothing the other way round.
!!
!! §24 reads `VizClipStep`, leaves `VizClipPos`, and `VizClipSpan` beside them because the inverse needs the same width.
!!
!! §25 And the inverse, for a marker being dragged: an x read as a step. It truncates, so a round trip can land a step low — which is why `VizClipMove` snaps a marker at the end of the bar rather than trusting the arithmetic not to leave it a step short.
!!
!! §26 reads `VizClipPos`, leaves `VizClipStep`.
!!
!! §27 **A marker being dragged, and the picture deliberately not redrawn.** The marker and the band are attributes, so moving them costs nothing; the clip is applied by `VizRelease` on the drop. See the section's own note for why that ordering is the whole point of the bar rather than an omission.
!!
!! §28 The partner's step is read *first*, because both markers have to be interpreted the same way whether or not a clip is already on — the other end is a drawn marker either way.
!!
!! §30 **The filter reads the parsed document, so it is guarded rather than trusting its callers.** `VizParsed` is deliberately left alone when there was nothing to parse, and this is the only place that reads it — reached from a draw *and* from a cutting gesture. With no recording the kept set is empty and the counts are zero because that is the truth about an empty run, not because a caller remembered to avoid it. The fault that found this: Graham's console error of 2026-10-13, at this line, on a script with no recording beside it — and **the read was only the first of three faults on the same path**, which is the reason it is written up as one story rather than three: the filter, then the redaction bars, then the frame pass that never ran.
!!
!! §29 **And a snap at the ends, within one floor's width.** A pointer cannot land exactly on the last step: the drag's x round-trips through pixels and truncates, so a marker dragged hard against the end stopped a step or two short, and a selection that was not quite the run. so "drag it home to clear the clip" did not work, measured on the 441-step trace while it happened to work on the 24-step one. Every scrollbar snaps at its ends for the same reason: the end is a place the reader means to reach, and it should not be a place they have to hit exactly.
    ! ---- the clip ----
VizClip:
    put VizViewX0 into VizSelFrom ! §1
    put VizViewX0 into VizSelTo
    add VizViewXW to VizSelTo
    take 1 from VizSelTo
    put 1 into VizKeepTail
    gosub to VizCut
    return

VizUnclip:
    ! **The whole recording back** — `reset`, and the only operation that widens. Nothing is un-done
    ! record by record, because nothing was ever rewritten: the empty list means no narrowing, and the next
    ! draw's filter keeps every record again.
    put 0 into VizKeepTail
    gosub to VizCut
    return

VizCut:
    add 0 to VizSelFrom ! §2
    add 0 to VizSelTo
    add 0 to VizAllTo
    if VizKeepTail is 0 go to VizCutWhole ! §3–§4
    ! A cut filters the set on screen; only the whole-recording path and an undo read the recording.
    put 0 into VizKeepWhole
    ! The list the cut replaces, so `undo` can put it back, and the tag, which is what makes a cut
    ! *visible* to the two places that decide a picture.
    put VizKeepTail into VizKeepSet
    gosub to VizHistPush
    put 0 into VizKeepN2
    put 0 into VizKeepI
    ! With nothing kept yet, the window *is* the answer — no range to intersect.
    if VizKeepN is 0 and VizKeepTail is 1
    begin
        put VizSelFrom into VizRangeFrom
        put VizSelTo into VizRangeTo
        gosub to VizCutAdd
    end
    ! And with nothing kept yet, the outside is the two ends of the recording. `0` is where the fit starts,
    ! and `VizAllTo` is the recording's own last step, remembered in pass one while the list is still empty.
    if VizKeepN is 0 and VizKeepTail is 2
    begin
        put 0 into VizRangeFrom
        take 1 from VizSelFrom giving VizRangeTo
        if VizRangeTo is not less than 0 gosub to VizCutAdd
        add 1 to VizSelTo giving VizRangeFrom
        put VizAllTo into VizRangeTo
        if VizRangeFrom is not greater than VizRangeTo gosub to VizCutAdd
    end
    ! Otherwise every live range is rewritten.
    while VizKeepI is less than VizKeepN
    begin
        index VizKeepFrom to VizKeepI
        put VizKeepFrom into VizKeepA
        index VizKeepTo to VizKeepI
        put VizKeepTo into VizKeepB
        if VizKeepTail is 1
        begin
            if VizSelFrom is greater than VizKeepA put VizSelFrom into VizKeepA
            if VizSelTo is less than VizKeepB put VizSelTo into VizKeepB
            if VizKeepA is not greater than VizKeepB
            begin
                put VizKeepA into VizRangeFrom
                put VizKeepB into VizRangeTo
                gosub to VizCutAdd
            end
        end
        if VizKeepTail is 2
        begin
            ! What is left of this range on each side of the window.
            if VizKeepA is less than VizSelFrom
            begin
                put VizKeepA into VizRangeFrom
                put VizSelFrom into VizRangeTo
                take 1 from VizRangeTo
                ! **And it cannot reach past the range it came from.** Without this the piece ran to the
                ! window's edge however far short of it the range ended, so keeping the *outside* of a window
                ! beyond a range *widened* that range: a 10-20 range against a 30-40 window gave `10-29`.
                ! Found 2026-10-13 by `tools/clip-check.js`, in both copies of this arithmetic.
                if VizRangeTo is greater than VizKeepB put VizKeepB into VizRangeTo
                if VizRangeFrom is not greater than VizRangeTo gosub to VizCutAdd
            end
            if VizKeepB is greater than VizSelTo
            begin
                put VizSelTo into VizRangeFrom
                add 1 to VizRangeFrom
                ! The mirror of the cap above: a range that starts past the window's far edge keeps its own
                ! start rather than the window's.
                if VizRangeFrom is less than VizKeepA put VizKeepA into VizRangeFrom
                put VizKeepB into VizRangeTo
                if VizRangeFrom is not greater than VizRangeTo gosub to VizCutAdd
            end
        end
        add 1 to VizKeepI
    end
    if VizKeepN2 is 0 ! §5
    begin
        take 1 from VizHistN
        put VizHistN into VizHistAt
        set the text of VizClipNotice to `nothing left to keep — the whole recording stands`
        return
    end
    gosub to VizCutSwap
    add 1 to VizSetTag
    gosub to VizRequest
    return

VizCutWhole:
    put 0 into VizKeepN ! §6–§7
    put 0 into VizKeepSet
    put 1 into VizKeepWhole
    add 1 to VizSetTag
    gosub to VizRequest
    return

VizCutAdd:
    if VizKeepN2 is not less than VizKeepCap return ! §8–§9
    index VizKeepFrom2 to VizKeepN2
    put VizRangeFrom into VizKeepFrom2
    index VizKeepTo2 to VizKeepN2
    put VizRangeTo into VizKeepTo2
    add 1 to VizKeepN2
    return

VizCutSwap:
    ! The built list becomes the live one. A loop rather than a swap, because a swap would need a third
    ! pair of holders, and either way this is a handful of numbers.
    put VizKeepN2 into VizKeepN
    put 0 into VizKeepI
    while VizKeepI is less than VizKeepN
    begin
        index VizKeepFrom2 to VizKeepI
        put VizKeepFrom2 into VizRangeFrom
        index VizKeepFrom to VizKeepI
        put VizRangeFrom into VizKeepFrom
        index VizKeepTo2 to VizKeepI
        put VizKeepTo2 into VizRangeTo
        index VizKeepTo to VizKeepI
        put VizRangeTo into VizKeepTo
        add 1 to VizKeepI
    end
    return

VizHistPush:
    ! **The list a cut is about to replace, as one string** — `0-20`, or `0-4;12-20`. The whole history in
    ! a handful of bytes is what makes an undo worth having: a saved *set* would be a copy of the
    ! recording, while this is a few numbers however big the run is, so keeping thirty of them costs
    ! nothing. An empty list is the recording unreduced, which is what undoing past the first cut restores.
    put `` into VizHistText
    put 0 into VizHistI
    while VizHistI is less than VizKeepN
    begin
        if VizHistText is not empty put VizHistText cat `;` into VizHistText
        index VizKeepFrom to VizHistI
        put VizKeepFrom into VizHistPiece
        put VizHistText cat VizHistPiece into VizHistText
        put VizHistText cat `-` into VizHistText
        index VizKeepTo to VizHistI
        put VizKeepTo into VizHistPiece
        put VizHistText cat VizHistPiece into VizHistText
        add 1 to VizHistI
    end
    index VizHist to VizHistN
    put VizHistText into VizHist
    add 1 to VizHistN
    put VizHistN into VizHistAt
    return

VizHistLoad:
    ! The text back into the list. Empty text is the recording unreduced — undo, past the first cut, is the
    ! whole run again. `split` fills a slot per piece, so the walk is `index` and `has element`, the same
    ! shape the editor's own buffer walk uses.
    put 0 into VizKeepN2
    if VizHistText is empty go to VizCutSwap
    split VizHistText by `;` into VizHistParts
    put 0 into VizHistI
    while VizHistParts has element VizHistI
    begin
        index VizHistParts to VizHistI
        put VizHistParts into VizHistPiece
        split VizHistPiece by `-` into VizHistPair
        index VizHistPair to 0
        put VizHistPair into VizRangeFrom
        index VizHistPair to 1
        put VizHistPair into VizRangeTo
        ! **And back to numbers.** A list read out of the history is *text*, and the next cut's arithmetic on
        ! it would compare lexically — `10 is less than 9` — so an undo followed by a cut would keep the
        ! wrong records. The same conversion the filter makes per record, made here for the same reason.
        add 0 to VizRangeFrom
        add 0 to VizRangeTo
        gosub to VizCutAdd
        add 1 to VizHistI
    end
    go to VizCutSwap

VizUndo:
    ! **Back to the list the last cut replaced**, and it is instant because it is a parse and a draw rather
    ! than a re-filter of the recording: the tag is bumped so the next draw rebuilds the set from the
    ! restored list, which is the same path a cut takes and is why an undo cannot drift from cutting.
    if VizHistAt is less than 1 return
    take 1 from VizHistAt
    index VizHist to VizHistAt
    put VizHist into VizHistText
    gosub to VizHistLoad
    ! An undo widens, so it cannot be answered from what is on screen: it reads the recording again.
    put 1 into VizKeepWhole
    add 1 to VizSetTag
    gosub to VizRequest
    return

VizKeepRun:
    ! **Nothing to filter without a recording, and the guard is here because a cutting gesture arrives at this
    ! label where no draw does.** With no recording the picture is the script and nothing else, so there is
    ! nothing to keep and nothing to count — and the parsed document below is deliberately unset when there
    ! was nothing to parse, which is the read that started all this: §30 has the fault's record and §31 what
    ! the empty picture turns out to need.
    if VizTrace is empty return
    json set VizKept to array ! §10–§13
    if VizKeepWhole is 1 put property `traceEvents` of VizParsed into VizAll ! §14
    if VizKeepWhole is 0 put VizEvents into VizAll
    put 0 into VizClipTotal
    put 0 into VizKeepCount
    put 0 into VizIndex
    while VizAll has element VizIndex
    begin
        put element VizIndex of VizAll into VizEvent
        put property `cat` of VizEvent into VizCategory
        put 0 into VizClipIs
        if VizCategory is `anchor` put 1 into VizClipIs
        if VizCategory is `transfer` put 1 into VizClipIs
        if VizClipIs is 1
        begin
            add 1 to VizClipTotal
            put property `args` of VizEvent into VizArgs
            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps
            add 0 to VizSteps
            put 0 into VizKeepKept
            if VizKeepN is 0 put 1 into VizKeepKept
            put 0 into VizKeepI
            while VizKeepI is less than VizKeepN
            begin
                index VizKeepFrom to VizKeepI
                put VizKeepFrom into VizKeepA
                index VizKeepTo to VizKeepI
                put VizKeepTo into VizKeepB
                if VizSteps is not less than VizKeepA and VizSteps is not greater than VizKeepB put 1 into VizKeepKept
                add 1 to VizKeepI
            end
            if VizKeepKept is 1
            begin
                json add VizEvent to VizKept
                ! The picture's own first step is the first *kept* record's, not the list's first range's:
                ! a range can hold no records at all, and a picture that began in the blank between two
                ! ranges would move the axis and the bars for nothing.
                if VizKeepCount is 0 and VizKeepN is not 0 put VizSteps into VizMinSteps
                add 1 to VizKeepCount
            end
        end
        add 1 to VizIndex
    end
    put VizKeepCount into VizClipKept
    put VizKept into VizEvents
    ! **And the tag it was built for, so that only a *change* of list rebuilds it.** The walk this makes
    ! is the expensive one — it reads every record, and reading a record costs about 15ms on a 435KB
    ! recording, because the runtime re-parses the whole array per access — so it is a cost to pay once
    ! when the reader cuts and never again while that list stands.
    put VizSetTag into VizKeptKey
    return

VizClipBar:
    put VizSelFrom into VizClipStep ! §15–§17
    gosub to VizClipToPos
    if VizClipPos is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizClipPos
    if VizClipPos is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizClipPos
    put VizClipPos into VizClipInX
    put VizSelTo into VizClipStep
    gosub to VizClipToPos
    if VizClipPos is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizClipPos
    if VizClipPos is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizClipPos
    put VizClipPos into VizClipOutX
    gosub to VizClipEdge
    return

VizClipEdge:
    take 2 from VizClipInX giving VizMove ! §18–§20
    set attribute `x` of VizClipMarkIn to VizMove
    take 2 from VizClipOutX giving VizMove
    set attribute `x` of VizClipMarkOut to VizMove
    set attribute `x` of VizClipBand to VizClipInX
    take VizClipInX from VizClipOutX giving VizMove
    set attribute `width` of VizClipBand to VizMove
    put VizSelFrom into VizTickValue ! §21
    gosub to VizPlaceX
    put VizTickPos into VizClipInXW
    put VizSelTo into VizTickValue
    gosub to VizPlaceX
    put VizTickPos into VizClipOutXW
    set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideIn to VizClipInXW
    set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideOut to VizClipOutXW
    ! An end the window has gone past is off the plot, and a plumb line drawn there would be a mark at the
    ! wall — so it is parked outside the frame, where it draws nothing.
    if VizClipInXW is less than VizMargin set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideIn to -10
    if VizClipInXW is greater than VizPlotRight set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideIn to -10
    if VizClipOutXW is less than VizMargin set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideOut to -10
    if VizClipOutXW is greater than VizPlotRight set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideOut to -10
    ! And the washes: what a `keep` would take away, on each side of the selection, clamped to the plot so
    ! that neither width can come out negative — a negative width is not a wide wash, it is an error.
    put VizClipInXW into VizMove
    if VizMove is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizMove
    if VizMove is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizMove
    set attribute `x` of VizClipMaskIn to VizMargin
    take VizMargin from VizMove giving VizClipWash
    set attribute `width` of VizClipMaskIn to VizClipWash
    put VizClipOutXW into VizMove
    if VizMove is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizMove
    if VizMove is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizMove
    set attribute `x` of VizClipMaskOut to VizMove
    take VizMove from VizPlotRight giving VizClipWash
    set attribute `width` of VizClipMaskOut to VizClipWash
    set the text of VizClipNotice to `` ! §22
    if VizKeepN is 0 return
    set the text of VizClipNotice to `kept the window: ` cat VizClipKept cat ` of ` cat VizClipTotal cat ` records`
    if VizKeepSet is 2 set the text of VizClipNotice to `kept the outside: ` cat VizClipKept cat ` of ` cat VizClipTotal cat ` records`
    return

VizClipToPos:
    take VizRunFrom from VizRunTo giving VizClipSpan ! §23–§24
    if VizClipSpan is less than 1 put 1 into VizClipSpan
    take VizRunFrom from VizClipStep giving VizClipPos
    multiply VizClipPos by VizPlotW
    divide VizClipPos by VizClipSpan
    add VizMargin to VizClipPos
    return

VizClipToStep:
    take VizRunFrom from VizRunTo giving VizClipSpan ! §25–§26
    if VizClipSpan is less than 1 put 1 into VizClipSpan
    take VizMargin from VizClipPos giving VizClipStep
    multiply VizClipStep by VizClipSpan
    divide VizClipStep by VizPlotW
    add VizRunFrom to VizClipStep
    return

VizClipMove:
    if VizGrabbed is 0 return ! §27–§28
    ! The partner's step, straight from the selection rather than read back from where it is drawn: both
    ! ends are ours, and reading one back through the mapping would be a second copy of the arithmetic the
    ! snap below already does.
    put VizSelFrom into VizClipOther
    if VizSlide is 3 put VizSelTo into VizClipOther
    ! The dragged marker's own x, from the pointer: the grab was measured in the same units and the
    ! delta scaled, which is the arithmetic the scrollbar handle drag uses and for the same reason —
    ! a position accumulated event by event drifts, because every division throws its remainder away.
    put the drag position into VizDragPos
    put property `x` of VizDragPos into VizDragX
    take VizGrabX from VizDragX giving VizDy
    gosub to VizPixelScale
    multiply VizDy by 1000
    divide VizDy by VizPixelsK
    add VizGrabMarker to VizDy
    if VizDy is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizDy
    if VizDy is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizDy
    put VizDy into VizClipPos
    gosub to VizClipToStep
    ! **Inside the recording, and not onto its partner** — and the floor between them has to yield to
    ! the run. On a three-step recording a four-step floor fights the run's own bounds, and the two
    ! clamps then disagreed and the later one lost: that is how a marker was drawn past the end of the
    ! bar the first time this was tried. The run's bounds are therefore applied *last*, after the floor.
    take VizRunFrom from VizRunTo giving VizClipMin
    if VizClipMin is greater than 4 put 4 into VizClipMin
    if VizSlide is 3
    begin
        take VizClipMin from VizClipOther giving VizClipPos
        if VizClipStep is greater than VizClipPos put VizClipPos into VizClipStep
    end
    if VizSlide is 4
    begin
        add VizClipMin to VizClipOther giving VizClipPos
        if VizClipStep is less than VizClipPos put VizClipPos into VizClipStep
    end
    if VizClipStep is less than VizRunFrom put VizRunFrom into VizClipStep
    if VizClipStep is greater than VizRunTo put VizRunTo into VizClipStep
    take VizClipMin from VizRunTo giving VizClipPos ! §29
    if VizClipStep is greater than VizClipPos put VizRunTo into VizClipStep
    add VizClipMin to VizRunFrom giving VizClipPos
    if VizClipStep is less than VizClipPos put VizRunFrom into VizClipStep
    if VizSlide is 3 put VizClipStep into VizSelFrom
    if VizSlide is 4 put VizClipStep into VizSelTo
    gosub to VizClipToPos
    if VizSlide is 3 put VizClipPos into VizClipInX
    if VizSlide is 4 put VizClipPos into VizClipOutX
    ! One call draws the whole appearance, the same one a drawing uses — see `VizClipEdge`.
    gosub to VizClipEdge
    ! **And the window being selected, named as it moves.** It says *select*, not *clip*, because that is
    ! what a drag now does: the counts belong to a clip some control has applied, and these words are the
    ! reader's only feedback while dragging. `VizClipEdge` has just cleared the notice, which is why this
    ! comes after it.
    if VizSlide is 3 set the text of VizClipNotice to `select steps ` cat VizClipStep cat `-` cat VizClipOther
    if VizSlide is 4 set the text of VizClipNotice to `select steps ` cat VizClipOther cat `-` cat VizClipStep
    return

!! @hash 769c4553
!!!
